Content:
\begin{definition}[Group]

 A group is a triple \(\pqty{G, \cdot, e}\) where:
 \begin{itemize}
 \item \(G\) is a set;
 \item \(\functiondomain{\cdot}{G \times G}{G}\) is a binary operation on \(G\); and
 \item \(e\) is an element \(e \in G\);
 \end{itemize}
 such that the following properties hold:
 \begin{itemize}
 \item Associativity. For all \(a, b, c \in G\), we have \(\pqty{a \cdot b} \cdot c = a \cdot \pqty{b \cdot c}\);
 \item Identity. For all \(a \in G\), we have \(e \cdot a = a \cdot e = a\);
 \item Inverses. For all \(a \in G\), there exists \(a \inverse \in G\), such that \(a \cdot a \inverse = a \inverse \cdot a = e\).
 \end{itemize}

 The size of \(G\) is called the order of \(\pqty{G, \cdot, e}\), written by \(\abs{G}\).
\end{definition}

