Type: definition
Title: \(\RR\)
Local macros: \RR := \mathbb{R}; \multiplicative := ^{\times}; \pqty := \left(#1\right)
Content:
\begin{definition}[\(\RR\)]
 The real numbers \(\RR\) are a set with elements \(0, 1 \in \RR\) where \(0 \neq 1\), equipped with operations \(+\) and \(\times\), and an ordering \(<\) satisfying the following axioms:
 \begin{enumerate}
 \item \(\pqty{\RR, +, 0}\) is an abelian group: \(+\) is commutative and associative with identity \(0\), and inverses exist under \(+\);
 \item \(\pqty{\RR\multiplicative, \times, 1}\) is an abelian group: \(\times\) is commutative and associative with identity \(1\), and inverses exist under \(\times\) for non-zero elements;
 \item \(\times\) is distributive over \(+\):
 \[
 a \times \pqty{b + c} = a \times b + a \times c,
 \]
 and \(\pqty{\RR, +, \times}\) form a field;
 \item \(\pqty{\RR, <}\) is a totally ordered field: for all \(a, b\), exactly one of \(a < b\), \(a = b\) and \(a > b\) holds; for all \(a, b, c\), if \(a < b\) and \(b < c\), then \(a < c\);
 \item the ordering preserve arithmetic: for all \(a, b, c\), if \(a < b\), then \(a + c < b + c\), and \(ac < bc\) for \(c > 0\);
 \item given any set \(S\) of reals that is non-empty and bounded above, \(S\) has a least upper bound.
 \end{enumerate}

 The final axiom is known as the least upper bound axiom.
\end{definition}

