\begin{remarks}
 \begin{enumerate}
 \item For an increasing sequence to converge, we only need to know that it is bounded above. The sequence converges to its supremum.
 \item The sequence \(\pqty{a_n}_{n = 1}^{\infty}\) with \(a_n = n\) is increasing and not bounded above -- and it diverges.
 \item Monotone convergence theorem is in fact equivalent to the least upper bound axiom -- meaning that if we substitute the latter with the former, we get an equivalent formation of the reals.
 \item Every sequence must have a monotonic subsequence.
 \end{enumerate}
\end{remarks}

