\begin{proof}
 \(\implies\). Suppose \(\functiondomain{f}{A}{B}\) is injective. We fix an arbitrary \(a_0 \in A\), and define \(\functiondomain{g}{B}{A}\), as follows. For \(b \in B\),
 \begin{itemize}
 \item if \(b \in \img f\), then let \(a \in A\) be \(f \pqty{a} = b\), and such \(a\) is unique; 
 \item if \(b \notin \img f\), let \(a = a_0\),
 \end{itemize}
 and let \(g\pqty{b} = a\). Equivalently, let
 \[
 \functionmap{g}{b}{\begin{cases}
 unique a \in A where f\pqty{a} = b, & b \in \img f, \\
 a_0, & b \notin \img f.
 \end{cases}}
 \]

 Then \(g\) is surjective.

 \(\impliedby\). Suppose \(\functiondomain{g}{B}{A}\) is surjective. Let
 \[
 \function{f}{A}{B}{a}{some b \in B where f \pqty{b} = a.}
 \]

 Then \(f\) is injective.
