\begin{remarks}
 \begin{enumerate}
 \item It would be nice to say that for any two sets \(A\) and \(B\), either \(A\) injects into \(B\), or \(B\) injects into \(A\) -- this allows us to define the comparison of sizes on any two sets, showing that our order is total. This is known as the Principle of Cardinal Comparability (PCC), and is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice (AC).

 \item The Continuum Hypothesis (CH) states that there is no set whose size lies between \(\NN\) and \(\RR\), i.e. any subset of \(\RR\) is either countable or bijects with \(\RR\).

 This is proven to be independent of Zermelo--Fraenkel Set Theory, with or without Axiom of Choice, i.e. within ZF or ZFC.
 \end{enumerate}
\end{remarks}

