This leads to the gradient descent algorithm, as described in \autoref{alg:gradient-descent}.

\begin{algorithm}[ht!]
    \caption{Gradient Descent Algorithm}\label{alg:gradient-descent}%
    \begin{algorithmic}
        \Function{GradientDescent}{\(f, \vb{x}_0\)}
        \State \(t \gets 0\)
        \Repeat
            \State \(\vb{v}_t \gets - \grad f\pqty{\vb{x}_t}\)
            \State Choose Step Size \(\eta_t > 0\)
            \State \(\vb{x}_{t + 1} \gets \vb{x}_t + \eta_t \vb{v}_t\)
            \State \(t \gets t + 1\)
        \Until{Certain Stopping Criteria(s)}
        \State \Return{\(\vb{x}_{t}\)}
        \EndFunction
    \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{remark}\label{optim-unit-0010}
    The stopping criteria(s) can be, for example, when out computing power runs out, or the gradient is very small.
\end{remark}

We note that the gradient descent algorithm could fail: if the step size is too large, we might not necessarily reach the minimum, for example, we overshoot if the function is very steep. Similar issues appear when the step size is too small, for example, it will take very long to reach the minimum if the function is very flat. 

\begin{figure}[ht!]\centering\begin{minipage}{0.94\linewidth}For the quadratic \(f(x)=\beta x^2/2\), gradient descent gives \(x_{t+1}=(1-\eta\beta)x_t\). Thus \(0<\eta<2/\beta\) yields contraction, whereas \(\eta>2/\beta\) yields divergence for nonzero initial data. Small positive steps contract slowly.\end{minipage}\caption{Step Sizes in Gradient Descent (mathematical description)}\label{fig:gradient-descent-step-sizes}\end{figure}

Therefore, we restrict our class of functions.

\begin{definition}[\(\alpha\)-strong convexity, \(\beta\)-smooth]\label{optim-unit-0011}
    \label{def:alpha-sc-beta-smooth}%
    Let \(\alpha, \beta > 0\). A twice continuously differentiable function \(\functiondomain{f}{\RR^n}{\RR}\) is \emph{\(\beta\)-smooth} if \(-\beta\identityM\preceq\grad^2 f\pqty{\vb{x}} \negDefEq \beta \identityM\) for all \(\vb{x} \in \RR^n\); and is \emph{\(\alpha\)-strongly convex} if \(\grad^2 f\pqty{\vb{x}} \posDefEq \alpha \identityM\) for all \(\vb{x} \in \RR^n\).
\end{definition}

