Content:
\begin{remark} 
 Bad algorithms to solve linear programs could be:
 \begin{itemize}
 \item list all \(\binom{n}{m}\) basic solutions;
 \item filter out basic feasible solutions (BFS) from this list;
 \item evaluate \(\vb{c}\transpose \vb{x}\) for each BFS and choose the best one.
 \end{itemize}

 But even if \(m \approx \flatfrac{n}{2}\), then the time taken would grow exponentially.
\end{remark}

