Course: Variational Principles: Convexity, Mechanics and the Calculus of Variations
Section: Convex Functions
Type: definition
Title: Convex Function
Label: variation-unit-0010
Local macros: \ccintv := [#1,#2]; \dom := dom; \oointv := (#1,#2); \pqty := \left(#1\right)
Content:
\begin{definition}[Convex Function] 

 Let \(S=\dom(f)\). A function \(f\) is convex, if
 \begin{itemize}
 \item its domain \(\dom\pqty{f}\) is convex (as a set); and
 \item the graph of \(f\) lies below, or on, its chords; mathematically, for any \(\vb{x}, \vb{y} \in S\) and \(t \in \ccintv{0}{1}\), we have
 \begin{equation}
 f\pqty{t \vb{x} + \pqty{1 - t} \vb{y}} \leq t f\pqty{\vb{x}} + \pqty{1 - t} f\pqty{\vb{y}}. 
 \end{equation}
 \end{itemize}

 If instead, [REF:eq:convex-condition] is strict: for any \(\vb{x}, \vb{y} \in S\) with \(\vb{x} \neq \vb{y}\), and \(t \in \oointv{0}{1}\), we have
 \begin{equation}
 f\pqty{t \vb{x} + \pqty{1 - t} \vb{y}} < t f\pqty{\vb{x}} + \pqty{1 - t} f\pqty{\vb{y}}, 
 \end{equation}
 then we say \(f\) is strictly convex.

 We say \(f\) is (strictly) concave if \(-f\) is (strictly) convex.
\end{definition}

