Consider electric and magnetic fields \(\vb{E} \pqty{t, \vb{x}}\) and \(\vb{B} \pqty{t, \vb{x}}\). Two of Maxwell's Equations are
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{equation}
        \label{eq:b-curlless}%
        \div \vb{B} = 0
    \end{equation}
    \begin{equation}
        \label{eq:faraday}%
        \curl \vb{E} = - \pdv{t} \vb{B}.
    \end{equation}
\end{subequations}

Locally on a contractible spatial region, \autoref{eq:b-curlless} tells us that \(\vb{B} = \curl \vb{A}\) for some \(\vb{A} \pqty{t, \vb{x}}\). Then putting this into \autoref{eq:faraday}, gives us
\[
    \curl \pqty{\vb{E} + \pdv{t} \vb{A}} = \vb{0}
\]
and hence
\[
    \vb{E} = - \grad \phi - \pdv{t} \vb{A}
\]
for some \(\phi\pqty{t, \vb{x}}\). They are called the \emph{magnetic potential} and \emph{electric potential} respectively.

