A trick to remembering the direction of the inequality is that since
\[
 \Var\pqty{X} = \Expt\bqty{\pqty{X - \Expt\bqty{X}}^2} \geq 0
\]
and also
\[
 \Var\pqty{X} = \Expt\bqty{X^2} - \Expt\bqty{X}^2,
\]
and note \(f\pqty{x} = x^2\) is convex and
\[
 \Expt\bqty{X^2} \geq \pqty{\Expt\bqty{X}}^2.
\]

To visualise [REF:probability-thm:jensen] (Jensen's Inequality), [REF:probability-fig:jensen-inequality] considers a uniform distribution over \(\Bqty{x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4}\), and the centre of the quadrilateral is marked red, with \(y\)-coordinate \(\Expt\bqty{f\pqty{X}}\), while \(f\pqty{\Expt\bqty{X}}\) is marked as the blue dot on the curve.

The same supporting-line proof works for a finite convex function on any open interval containing the values of the integrable variable and its mean. We first produce useful lemmas for the proof.

