\begin{definition}[Positive/Negative Definiteness]

 Let \(\vb{M}\) be an \(n \times n\) real symmetric matrix. Then we say it is
 \begin{itemize}
 \item positive definite, denoted as \(\vb{M} \posDef 0\), if for all \(\vb{u} \in \RR^n \setminus \Bqty{\vb{0}}\), we have \(\vb{u}\transpose \vb{M} \vb{u} > 0\);
 \item positive semi-definite, or non-negative definite, denoted as \(\vb{M} \posDefEq 0\), if for all \(\vb{u} \in \RR^n\), we have \(\vb{u} \transpose \vb{M} \vb{u} \geq 0\);
 \item negative definite, denoted as \(\vb{M} \negDef 0\), if for all \(\vb{u} \in \RR^n \setminus \Bqty{\vb{0}}\), we have \(\vb{u}\transpose \vb{M} \vb{u} < 0\);
 \item negative semi-definite, or non-positive definite, denoted as \(\vb{M} \negDefEq 0\), if for all \(\vb{u} \in \RR^n\), we have \(\vb{u} \transpose \vb{M} \vb{u} \leq 0\).
 \end{itemize}

 Equivalently, in terms of eigenvalues, it is
 \begin{itemize}
 \item positive definite, if all its eigenvalues are positive;
 \item positive semi-definite, or non-negative definite, if all its eigenvalues are non-negative;
 \item negative definite, if all its eigenvalues are negative;
 \item negative semi-definite, or non-positive definite, if all its eigenvalues are non-positive.
 \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

