\begin{definition} 
Sets $A$ and $B$ are equinumerous (have the same cardinality), written $A\sim B$, if
there is a bijection $f:A\to B$. For $n\in\N$ write $\N_n=\set{1,2,\dots,n}$.
A set $A$ is
finite if $A=\es$ or $A\sim\N_n$ for some $n$ (and then $\abs A=n$);
infinite if not finite;
denumerable (countably infinite) if $A\sim\N$;
countable if finite or denumerable;
uncountable if not countable.
\end{definition}

