Type: method
Title: Verifying that a candidate $d$ is a metric
Local macros: \R := \mathbb{R}
Content:
\begin{method}{Verifying that a candidate $d$ is a metric}
Recognition trigger. You are handed a formula $d(p,q)$ and asked whether it is a
metric (Rudin Ex.~11 lists five candidates on $\R^1$; only some pass).
\begin{enumerate}
\item Positivity/definiteness: confirm $d\ge0$ everywhere and that $d(p,q)=0$
 forces $p=q$. (Squaring a metric, e.g.\ $(x-y)^2$, usually kills the triangle inequality;
 a non-injective inner function, e.g.\ $\abs{x^2-y^2}$, usually kills definiteness since
 $d(1,-1)=0$.)
\item Symmetry: check $d(p,q)=d(q,p)$ --- typically immediate.
\item Triangle inequality: the hard axiom. For $\abs{\,\cdot\,}$-based formulas,
 reduce to the absolute-value triangle inequality; for $\sqrt{\abs{x-y}}$ use
 $\sqrt{a+b}\le\sqrt a+\sqrt b$; to disprove it, find a concrete failing triple.
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

