Content:
We can extend this idea of $C$ into a higher-dimensional object $H$, known as the Hamiltonian quaternions. This forms a vector space over $R$ with a basis $\{1,i,j,k\}$ where the ring multiplication is defined by the group $Q_8$. Some ``fun'' facts about $H$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $H$ is not commutative;
\item $H$ is a ring with identity. For any $a + bi + cj + dk$, the inverse is $(a - bi - cj - dk)/(a^2+b^2+c^2+d^c)$.
\end{enumerate}

We can extend these definitions even further.

