Title: Geometric picture: left translation permutes a finite group
Content:
\begin{remark}[Geometric picture: left translation permutes a finite group]
Concretely, for $G=Z/(4)Z=\{0,1,2,3\}$ under addition, left translation $L_1:x\mapsto x+1$ is the rotation arrows below. Every vertex has exactly one incoming and one outgoing arrow, so $L_1$ is a bijection -- the geometric content of the rearrangement theorem.

\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=2.4cm]
\node[draw,circle] (z0) at (0,2) {$0$};
\node[draw,circle] (z1) at (2,0) {$1$};
\node[draw,circle] (z2) at (0,-2) {$2$};
\node[draw,circle] (z3) at (-2,0) {$3$};
\draw[->,thick] (z0) to[bend left=20] (z1);
\draw[->,thick] (z1) to[bend left=20] (z2);
\draw[->,thick] (z2) to[bend left=20] (z3);
\draw[->,thick] (z3) to[bend left=20] (z0);
\node at (0,0) {$L_1$};
\end{tikzpicture}

The single $4$-cycle $0\to1\to2\to3\to0$ shows $L_1$ has no fixed point and visits every element once, i.e.\ each row of the $Z/(4)Z$ Cayley table is the cyclic shift of the header.
\end{remark}

