Content:
The additive clock for $(Z/(6)Z,+)$ below shows the single cycle generated by $\bar 1$; adding $\bar 1$ steps one notch clockwise, and $6$ steps return to $\bar 0$.


\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.4]
 \node[cgdot] (n0) at (90:1.4) {};
 \node[cgdot] (n1) at (30:1.4) {};
 \node[cgdot] (n2) at (-30:1.4) {};
 \node[cgdot] (n3) at (-90:1.4) {};
 \node[cgdot] (n4) at (-150:1.4) {};
 \node[cgdot] (n5) at (150:1.4) {};
 \draw[cgr] (n0) to[bend left=15] (n1);
 \draw[cgr] (n1) to[bend left=15] (n2);
 \draw[cgr] (n2) to[bend left=15] (n3);
 \draw[cgr] (n3) to[bend left=15] (n4);
 \draw[cgr] (n4) to[bend left=15] (n5);
 \draw[cgr] (n5) to[bend left=15] (n0);
 \node[cglab,above=2pt of n0] {$\bar 0$};
 \node[cglab,right=2pt of n1] {$\bar 1$};
 \node[cglab,right=2pt of n2] {$\bar 2$};
 \node[cglab,below=2pt of n3] {$\bar 3$};
 \node[cglab,left=2pt of n4] {$\bar 4$};
 \node[cglab,left=2pt of n5] {$\bar 5$};
 \node[cglab] at (0,0) {$+\bar 1$};
\end{tikzpicture}


Generators of $(Z/(6)Z,+)$ are exactly the units mod $6$: $\bar 1$ (clockwise step) and $\bar 5=-\bar 1$ (counterclockwise step), since $\gcd(1,6)=\gcd(5,6)=1$. The map $Z\toZ/(6)Z$, $k\mapsto k\bar 1$, factors through the quotient, giving the universal picture of a cyclic group:
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=large,column sep=large]
 Z \arrow[r, two heads, "\pi"] \arrow[dr, two heads, "k\,\mapsto\,k\bar 1"'] & Z/(6)Z \arrow[d, dashed, "\exists!\,\cong"] \\ & \langle \bar 1\rangle
\end{tikzcd}\]
\begin{dgx}
\dgi{Objects:} Three groups: $Z$ (the infinite additive group of integers), $Z/(6)Z$ (the additive quotient group $Z/6Z$ of residue classes mod $6$, order $6$), and $\langle\bar 1\rangle$ (the cyclic subgroup of $Z/(6)Z$ generated by $\bar 1$, which equals all of $Z/(6)Z$ since $\bar 1$ generates).
\dgi{Arrows:} $\pi:Z\toZ/(6)Z$ is the canonical surjection $k\mapsto\bar k$, the quotient map that kills the subgroup $6Z$; the double-head notation records surjectivity. The diagonal $k\mapsto k\bar 1$ is the evaluation homomorphism sending $k$ to the $k$-th multiple of $\bar 1$ in $Z/(6)Z$; it maps onto $\langle\bar 1\rangle$, so it also carries a double head, and following it with the inclusion $\langle\bar 1\rangle\hookrightarrowZ/(6)Z$ gives $\pi$. The dashed vertical arrow is the unique isomorphism $Z/(6)Z\xrightarrow{\,\cong\,}\langle\bar 1\rangle$ induced by the universal property of the quotient.
\dgi{What commutes:} The triangle commutes: $(dashed)\circ\pi = (k\mapsto k\bar 1)$, i.e., applying $\pi$ then the isomorphism gives the same result as mapping $k$ directly to $k\bar 1$.
\dgi{Why it commutes:} The diagonal map $k\mapsto k\bar 1$ kills $6Z$ (since $6\bar 1=\bar 0$), so the universal property of $Z/(6)Z=Z/6Z$ provides a unique induced map $Z/(6)Z\to\langle\bar 1\rangle$ making the triangle commute. Well-definedness: if $k\equiv k'\pmod 6$ then $(k-k')\bar 1=\bar 0$, so $k\bar 1=k'\bar 1$; the coset representative does not matter.
\dgi{Why this matters here:} This diagram encodes the fact that $(Z/(6)Z,+)$ is cyclic: the single element $\bar 1$ generates all of $Z/(6)Z$, so the unique induced map is an isomorphism. It is the universal picture the text uses to explain generators of cyclic groups immediately after the clock diagram.
\dgi{Concrete example:} Take $k=8$. Then $\pi(8)=\bar 2$ and $8\bar 1=\bar 8=\bar 2$, confirming the triangle for this element. The dashed isomorphism sends $\bar 2\mapsto 2\bar 1=\bar 2$, which is the identity on $Z/(6)Z=\langle\bar 1\rangle$.
\dgi{Common pitfall:} Students sometimes read the dashed arrow as defining $\langle\bar 1\rangle$ rather than recording the universal property. The isomorphism exists because the kernel of the diagonal map equals $6Z=\ker\pi$; if the kernel were different, no such induced map would exist.
\dgi{Takeaway:} The point of this diagram is that every cyclic group of order $n$ is a quotient of $Z$, and the quotient map factors through the group via the unique isomorphism given by the universal property.
\end{dgx}

