\begin{wex}{Orders of several elements of $(\mathbb{Z}/(12)\mathbb{Z},+)$}
Compute $\ord{\bar a}$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(12)\mathbb{Z},+)$ for $a=0,1,2,3,4,6,8,9$.
\wrec This is an additive group $\mathbb{Z}/(12)\mathbb{Z}$, so the shortcut $\ord{\bar a}=12/\gcd(a,12)$ from Prop.~\ref{p:order-zn@c1} applies directly; the only computation needed is a gcd for each $a$.
\wsol We tabulate $d=\gcd(a,12)$ and $\ord{\bar a}=12/d$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
a & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 & 8 & 9\\\hline
\gcd(a,12) & 12 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 & 4 & 3\\
\ord{\bar a}=12/\gcd & 1 & 12 & 6 & 4 & 3 & 2 & 3 & 4
\end{array}
\]
Spot-check $a=8$ by listing additive multiples: $\bar 8,\ \overline{16}=\bar 4,\ \overline{24}=\bar 0$; that is $1\cdot\bar8=\bar8$, $2\cdot\bar8=\bar4$, $3\cdot\bar8=\bar0$, so $\ord{\bar 8}=3$, matching $12/\gcd(8,12)=12/4=3$. Sanity check: every order divides $|\mathbb{Z}/(12)\mathbb{Z}|=12$, and indeed $\{1,12,6,4,3,2,3,4\}$ all divide $12$. Also $\ord{\bar 0}=1$ (the identity), as required.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{An element of order $7$ in $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$}
In $(\mathbb{Z}/(14)\mathbb{Z},+)$, find $\ord{\bar 4}$ and $\ord{\bar 2}$.
\wrec Additive group again, so use $\ord{\bar a}=14/\gcd(a,14)$. The trigger is simply ``order in $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ under $+$.''
\wsol $\gcd(4,14)=2$, so $\ord{\bar 4}=14/2=7$. Check: $1\cdot\bar4=\bar4,\ 2\cdot\bar4=\bar8,\ 3\cdot\bar4=\overline{12},\ 4\cdot\bar4=\overline{16}=\bar2,\ 5\cdot\bar4=\bar6,\ 6\cdot\bar4=\overline{10},\ 7\cdot\bar4=\overline{14}=\bar0$. First return at $k=7$. Similarly $\gcd(2,14)=2$ gives $\ord{\bar 2}=7$. Sanity check: $7\mid 14=|\mathbb{Z}/(14)\mathbb{Z}|$. Both $\bar2$ and $\bar4$ generate the same order-$7$ subgroup $\{\bar0,\bar2,\bar4,\bar6,\bar8,\overline{10},\overline{12}\}$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Generators of $\mathbb{Z}/(10)\mathbb{Z}$}
Which classes $\bar a$ generate $(\mathbb{Z}/(10)\mathbb{Z},+)$, i.e. have $\ord{\bar a}=10$?
\wrec By Prop.~\ref{p:order-zn@c1}, $\ord{\bar a}=10 \Longleftrightarrow \gcd(a,10)=1$. So generators $=$ units mod $10$. Trigger: ``which elements have full order $n$.''
\wsol Test $a=1,\dots,9$: $\gcd$ with $10$ equals $1$ exactly for $a\in\{1,3,7,9\}$ (the others share factor $2$ or $5$). So the generators are $\bar1,\bar3,\bar7,\bar9$, and there are $\varphi(10)=\varphi(2)\varphi(5)=1\cdot4=4$ of them. Check $a=3$: $\bar3,\bar6,\bar9,\overline{12}=\bar2,\bar5,\bar8,\overline{11}=\bar1,\bar4,\bar7,\overline{10}=\bar0$ -- exactly $10$ distinct classes before returning to $\bar0$, so $\ord{\bar 3}=10$. Sanity check: number of generators of a cyclic group of order $n$ is $\varphi(n)$, and $\varphi(10)=4$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Reading off an order without the formula}
In $(\mathbb{Z}/(9)\mathbb{Z},+)$ compute $\ord{\bar 6}$ both by listing and by the gcd formula, and confirm they agree.
\wrec Additive cyclic group; the point is to verify the shortcut against brute force.
\wsol Listing: $1\cdot\bar6=\bar6,\ 2\cdot\bar6=\overline{12}=\bar3,\ 3\cdot\bar6=\overline{18}=\bar0$. First return at $k=3$, so $\ord{\bar6}=3$. Formula: $\gcd(6,9)=3$, $\ord{\bar6}=9/3=3$. They agree. Sanity check: $3\mid 9=|\mathbb{Z}/(9)\mathbb{Z}|$, and the subgroup generated is $\{\bar0,\bar3,\bar6\}$ of size $3$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Why $\bar6$ is a unit mod $25$ but not mod $9$}
Decide whether $\bar 6\in(\mathbb{Z}/(25)\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$ and whether $\bar 6\in(\mathbb{Z}/(9)\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$, and (where it is a unit) find $\ord{\bar 6}$ and its inverse.
\wrec ``Is $\bar a$ a unit mod $n$?'' triggers the criterion $\gcd(a,n)=1$ (Prop.~\ref{p:unit-iff-coprime@c1}). The two moduli differ by their prime factors, which is exactly what the gcd sees.
\wsol Mod $25$: $\gcd(6,25)=1$ (since $25=5^2$ and $5\nmid 6$), so $\bar6\in(\mathbb{Z}/(25)\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$. Mod $9$: $\gcd(6,9)=3\ne1$ (both divisible by $3$), so $\bar6\notin(\mathbb{Z}/(9)\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$ -- it has no multiplicative inverse. Concretely mod $9$ the multiples $6\cdot1=6,\,6\cdot2=12\equiv3,\,6\cdot3=18\equiv0,\dots$ never hit $\bar1$, confirming non-invertibility. Inverse mod $25$ by Euclid: $25=4\cdot6+1$, so $1=25-4\cdot6$, giving $-4\cdot6\equiv1$, i.e. $\bar6^{-1}=\overline{-4}=\overline{21}$. Check $6\cdot21=126=125+1\equiv\bar1\pmod{25}$.\ $\checkmark$ Order of $\bar6$ mod $25$: $\varphi(25)=25-5=20$, so $\ord{\bar 6}\mid20$. Compute $6^2=36\equiv\overline{11}$, $6^4\equiv11^2=121\equiv\overline{21}$, $6^5=6^4\cdot6\equiv21\cdot6=126\equiv\bar1$. First return at $5$, so $\ord{\bar 6}=5$, and $5\mid20$.\ $\checkmark$
\end{wex}

