e ar1$ (since $n-1\ge2$), so $\ord{\overline{-1}}
e1$. And $(\overline{-1})^2=\overline{1}$, so $\ord{\overline{-1}}=2$. Note $\gcd(n-1,n)=\gcd(-1,n)=1$, so $\overline{-1}$ really is a unit. Concretely $n=8$: $\overline{-1}= ar7$, and $7^2=49=48+1\equiv ar1\pmod 8$, while $ ar7
e ar1$; hence $\ord{ ar 7}=2$. In $\mathbb{R}^	imes$: $(-1)^1=-1
e1$ but $(-1)^2=1$, so $\ord{-1}=2$ there too. Sanity check: in any group an element $x
e1$ with $x^2=1$ (i.e. $x=x^{-1}$) has order exactly $2$; that is precisely the case here.\ $\checkmark$
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{An element of infinite order in $(\mathbb{Q},+)$}
Show $\tfrac12\in(\mathbb{Q},+)$ has infinite order. Contrast with $(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z},+)$ where every order is finite.
\wrec ``Infinite order'' triggers: prove NO positive multiple is the identity. Here the identity of $(\mathbb{Q},+)$ is $0$, and we examine $k\cdot\tfrac12$.
\wsol For any integer $k>0$, $k\cdot\tfrac12=\tfrac{k}{2}$. This equals the additive identity $0$ only if $k=0$, which is not allowed ($k>0$). Hence $k\cdot\tfrac12\ne0$ for all $k>0$, so $\ord{\tfrac12}=\infty$. The same argument shows every nonzero $q\in\mathbb{Q}$ has infinite additive order: $kq=0$ with $q\ne0$ forces $k=0$. Sanity check: $\mathbb{Q}$ is torsion-free under $+$; the only finite-order element is $0$ itself, with $\ord{0}=1$. This is exactly why $(\mathbb{Q},+)$ cannot be isomorphic to any finite $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{An element of infinite order in $(\mathbb{R}^\times,\cdot)$}
Show $2\in(\mathbb{R}^\times,\cdot)$ has infinite order; then show $\ord{-1}=2$ to highlight the contrast.
\wrec ``Infinite order'' in a multiplicative group: prove no positive power equals the identity $1$. The trigger is the multiplicative identity $1$ versus the powers $2^k$.
\wsol For $k>0$, $2^k\ge2>1$, so $2^k\ne1$ for every $k>0$; hence $\ord{2}=\infty$. (Same for any real $t$ with $|t|>1$, or $0<|t|<1$: $|t^k|=|t|^k\ne1$ for all $k\ge1$.) By contrast, $-1$ has $|-1|=1$ and $(-1)^2=1$ with $(-1)^1=-1\ne1$, so $\ord{-1}=2$. Sanity check: in $(\mathbb{R}^\times,\cdot)$ the only finite-order elements are $1$ ($\ord{}=1$) and $-1$ ($\ord{}=2$); these are exactly the real solutions of $x^k=1$, namely the real roots of unity.\ $\checkmark$
\end{wex}

\begin{pitfall}
Lagrange tells you $\ord{\bar a}$ divides $\varphi(n)$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$, but NOT that it equals $\varphi(n)$. In $(\mathbb{Z}/(15)\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$ every element has order $1,2,$ or $4$, none equal to $\varphi(15)=8$, so $(\mathbb{Z}/(15)\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$ has no generator. Always test the candidate divisors; do not assume the group is cyclic.
\end{pitfall}

\begin{remark}\label{r:notation-bar-mrg@c1}
D\&F write the elements of $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ as the bars $\bar a$ (or $\overline{a}$), where $\bar a=\bar b$ exactly when $n\mid(a-b)$. The set $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ carries \emph{two} natural operations, $+$ and $\times$, but only $(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z},+)$ is a group for every $n$; under $\times$ one passes to the units $(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$ to get a group. The phrase ``the integers mod $n$'' refers to the additive group, while ``$(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$'' or ``the units mod $n$'' refers to the multiplicative group.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}\label{r:zn-cyclic-named-mrg@c1}
For \emph{every} $n\ge1$ the additive group $(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z},+)$ is generated by the single element $\bar 1$, since $\bar k=k\cdot\bar 1$ for all $k$. Thus $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}=\langle \bar 1\rangle$ is cyclic of order $n$. This is the model D\&F use whenever they say ``the cyclic group of order $n$.''
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}\label{r:d8-naming-mrg@c1}
D\&F denote the dihedral group of symmetries of a regular $m$-gon by $D_{2m}$, indexed by its \emph{order} $2m$, not by $m$. So the symmetry group of the square has order $8$ and is written $D_8=\langle r,s\rangle$ with $r^4=s^2=1$ and $sr=r^{-1} s$ (some texts call this group $D_4$). We keep D\&F's convention throughout.
\end{remark}

\begin{wex}{Orders in the dihedral group $D_8$}
In $D_8=\langle r,s\rangle$ with $r^4=s^2=1$ and $sr=r^{-1} s$, find the order of every element.
\wrec D\&F use the dihedral groups as a standard source of mixed orders: a rotation $r^k$ has order $4/\gcd(k,4)$, while every reflection $sr^k$ squares to the identity.
\wsol The eight elements are $1,r,r^2,r^3,s,sr,sr^2,sr^3$. Rotations: $\ord{1}=1$, $\ord{r}=\ord{r^3}=4$, $\ord{r^2}=2$. Reflections: each satisfies $(sr^k)^2=sr^k sr^k=s(r^k s)r^k=s(s r^{-k})r^k=s^2=1$, using $r^k s=s r^{-k}$; and $sr^k\ne1$, so $\ord{sr^k}=2$ for $k=0,1,2,3$. Collected:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc|cccc}
g & 1 & r & r^2 & r^3 & s & sr & sr^2 & sr^3\\\hline
\ord{g} & 1 & 4 & 2 & 4 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 2
\end{array}
\]
Sanity check: all orders divide $|D_8|=8$; there are exactly $\varphi(4)=2$ elements of order $4$ inside the rotation subgroup $\langle r\rangle\cong\mathbb{Z}/(4)\mathbb{Z}$, namely $r$ and $r^3$, as the cyclic theory predicts.\ $\checkmark$
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Infinite order in $\GL_2(\mathbb{R})$}
Let $A=\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1\\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}\in\GL_2(\mathbb{R})$. Show $\ord{A}=\infty$, and contrast with $B=\begin{pmatrix}0 & -1\\ 1 & 0\end{pmatrix}$, which has finite order.
\wrec D\&F use $\GL_n$ as the standard infinite group in which individual elements may still have finite or infinite order. Compute the powers directly.
\wsol By induction $A^k=\begin{pmatrix}1 & k\\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$. For $k>0$ the top-right entry is $k\ne0$, so $A^k\ne I$ for every $k\ge1$; hence $\ord{A}=\infty$. For $B$: $B^2=\begin{pmatrix}-1 & 0\\ 0 & -1\end{pmatrix}=-I$, $B^3=\begin{pmatrix}0 & 1\\ -1 & 0\end{pmatrix}$, $B^4=I$, so $\ord{B}=4$ (this $B$ is rotation by $90^\circ$). Sanity check: $\det A=\det B=1\ne0$, so both lie in $\GL_2(\mathbb{R})$; a single group can contain elements of every order, finite and infinite.\ $\checkmark$
\end{wex}

\begin{remark}\label{r:additive-vs-mult-orders-mrg@c1}
The contrast across these examples is the recurring D\&F theme. The additive groups $(\mathbb{R},+)$, $(\mathbb{Q},+)$, $(\mathbb{Z},+)$ are \emph{torsion-free}: the identity $0$ is the only element of finite order ($\ord{0}=1$), since $kx=0$ with $k>0$ forces $x=0$. The multiplicative group $(\mathbb{R}^\times,\cdot)$ has just two elements of finite order, $\ord{1}=1$ and $\ord{-1}=2$, and $(\mathbb{Q}^\times,\cdot)$ has the same two. By comparison the finite groups $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$, $(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$, $S_3$, $D_8$, $Q_8$ realize a whole range of orders. Passing from $+$ to $\times$, or from $\mathbb{Z}$ to $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$, is exactly what creates nontrivial finite orders.
\end{remark}

