embedding_text:
Course: abstract algebra notes
Section: Introduction to Groups > The Quaternion Group > Orders of the eight elements
Type: prose
Content:
We can picture the cyclic subgroup $\langle i\rangle=\{1,i,-1,-i\}$ as a clock with the rotation $x\mapsto ix$:


\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
 \node[cgdot] (a) at (90:1.5) {};
 \node[cgdot] (b) at (0:1.5) {};
 \node[cgdot] (c) at (-90:1.5) {};
 \node[cgdot] (d) at (180:1.5) {};
 \draw[cgr] (a) to[bend left=20] (b);
 \draw[cgr] (b) to[bend left=20] (c);
 \draw[cgr] (c) to[bend left=20] (d);
 \draw[cgr] (d) to[bend left=20] (a);
 \node[cglab,above=2pt of a] {$1$};
 \node[cglab,right=2pt of b] {$i$};
 \node[cglab,below=2pt of c] {$-1$};
 \node[cglab,left=2pt of d] {$-i$};
 \node at (0,-2.2) {multiply by $i$: $1\to i\to -1\to -i\to 1$ (order $4$)};
\end{tikzpicture}


\begin{dgx}
\dgi{Objects:} Four nodes arranged clockwise at the cardinal points of a circle: $1$ (top), $i$ (right), $-1$ (bottom), $-i$ (left). Each node is an element of the cyclic subgroup $\langle i \rangle = \{1, i, -1, -i\} \leq Q_8$, which is a cyclic group of order $4$.
\dgi{Arrows:} Each curved arrow from node $x$ to node $ix$ represents the left-multiplication map $L_i \colon x \mapsto ix$ restricted to $\langle i \rangle$. Specifically: $1 \to i$ (since $i \cdot 1 = i$), $i \to -1$ (since $i \cdot i = -1$), $-1 \to -i$ (since $i \cdot (-1) = -i$), $-i \to 1$ (since $i \cdot (-i) = -(i \cdot i) = 1$). The arrows form a single directed $4$-cycle.
\dgi{What commutes:} The diagram encodes one cyclic path: applying $L_i$ four times returns to the start, so $L_i^4 = \operatorname{id}$. The composed path $1 \xrightarrow{L_i} i \xrightarrow{L_i} -1 \xrightarrow{L_i} -i \xrightarrow{L_i} 1$ is the group relation $i^4 = 1$.
\dgi{Why it commutes:} Chase $1$ around: $i^1 = i$, $i^2 = -1$, $i^3 = -i$, $i^4 = 1$. The fourth power returns to the identity because $i^2 = -1$ and $(-1)^2 = 1$ in $Q_8$, so $i^4 = (i^2)^2 = (-1)^2 = 1$. No shorter power returns to $1$ (each intermediate value is distinct), confirming $\operatorname{ord}(i) = 4$.
\dgi{Why this matters here:} This picture appears immediately after the proposition asserting $\operatorname{ord}(i) = 4$. It gives a geometric reason: the orbit of $1$ under repeated left-multiplication by $i$ visits exactly $4$ distinct elements before cycling, so the period is $4$, not $1$ or $2$. It also makes visible why $-1$ is ``halfway around,'' matching $i^2 = -1$.
\dgi{Concrete example:} In $Z/4Z$ under addition, the map $x \mapsto x + 1$ gives the same $4$-cycle: $0 \to 1 \to 2 \to 3 \to 0$. The identification $0 \leftrightarrow 1$, $1 \leftrightarrow i$, $2 \leftrightarrow -1$, $3 \leftrightarrow -i$ gives an explicit isomorphism $\langle i \rangle \cong Z/4Z$.
\dgi{Common pitfall:} Students sometimes read the arrows as right-multiplication ($x \mapsto xi$) rather than left-multiplication ($x \mapsto ix$). In an abelian group these coincide, but in $Q_8$ they can differ for other choices of acting element. Here, however, every node lies in the cyclic (hence abelian) subgroup $\langle i \rangle$, so $ix = xi$ for each node and the two readings give the same arrows; the distinction matters only for $x \notin \langle i \rangle$, e.g.\ $ij = k$ but $ji = -k$.
\dgi{Takeaway:} The point of this diagram is that the four elements $1, i, -1, -i$ form a single closed orbit under left-multiplication by $i$, giving a visual proof that $\operatorname{ord}(i) = 4$ and that $\langle i \rangle$ is cyclic of order $4$.
\end{dgx}

