Type: method
Title: To PROVE $G\cong H$: build an explicit bijective homomorphism
Content:
\begin{method}{To PROVE $G\cong H$: build an explicit bijective homomorphism}
Trigger. You suspect two concretely given groups are the same, or the problem says ``show $G\cong H$.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item Guess the map. Match generators of $G$ to elements of $H$ that satisfy the same relations; or use a known structural formula ($\exp$, $\log$, $g^k\mapsto k$, a permutation action).
\item Check well-defined + homomorphism: verify $\varphi(xy)=\varphi(x)\varphi(y)$ for all $x,y\in G$, i.e.\ that the multiplication square commutes. If $\varphi$ is defined by images of generators, it suffices (and is necessary) to check that these images satisfy the defining relations of $G$; checking the product rule only on pairs of generators is not enough.
\item Check bijective. Easiest route: show $\varphi$ is injective ($\ker\varphi=\{1\}$, i.e.\ $\varphi(x)=1\Longrightarrow x=1$) AND $|G|=|H|$ finite; or exhibit an explicit two-sided inverse map.
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

