Title: All subgroups of $Z/(16)Z$ (a chain)
Content:
\begin{wex}{All subgroups of $Z/(16)Z$ (a chain)}
List every subgroup of $Z/(16)Z$ and draw the lattice.
\wrec Cyclic of order $16=2^4$, a prime power. For $n=p^k$ the divisors are totally ordered ($1\mid p\mid p^2\mid\dots$), so by Theorem~ [REF:t:cycsub@c2] (3) the subgroup lattice is a single chain. Recognizing $n$ is a prime power is the trigger for ``chain.''
\wsol Divisors of $16$: $1,2,4,8,16$, so $\tau(16)=5$ subgroups, each containing the previous:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|l}
d & 16/d & H=\langle \overline{16/d}\rangle\\\hline
1 & 16 & \{0\}\\
2 & 8 & \langle \overline 8\rangle=\{0,8\}\\
4 & 4 & \langle \overline 4\rangle=\{0,4,8,12\}\\
8 & 2 & \langle \overline 2\rangle=\{0,2,4,\dots,14\}\\
16 & 1 & Z/(16)Z
\end{array}
\]
Each order divides the next ($1\mid2\mid4\mid8\mid16$), so by the order rule we get a totally ordered chain $\{0\}\subset\langle \overline 8\rangle\subset\langle \overline 4\rangle\subset\langle \overline 2\rangle\subsetZ/(16)Z$.
 \begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=1.05,xscale=1.25]
 \node[ltdot] (bot) at (0,0) {};
 \node[ltdot] (a) at (0,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (b) at (0,2) {};
 \node[ltdot] (c) at (0,3) {};
 \node[ltdot] (top) at (0,4) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (bot)--(a)--(b)--(c)--(top);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,below=1.5pt of bot] {$\{0\}$};
 \node[ltlab,right=3pt of a] {$\langle \overline 8\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,right=3pt of b] {$\langle \overline 4\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,right=3pt of c] {$\langle \overline 2\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,above=1.5pt of top] {$Z/(16)Z$};
\end{tikzpicture} 
Check. $5=\tau(16)$, and a $p$-group cyclic lattice must be a chain --- confirmed, no branching. Correct.
\end{wex}

