\begin{definition} 
Let $H,K\sgr G$.
\begin{itemize}
\item The meet of $H$ and $K$ is their intersection $H\cap K$. It is the largest subgroup contained in both: it lies below both $H$ and $K$ in the diagram, and any subgroup below both lies below $H\cap K$.
\item The join of $H$ and $K$ is $\langle H,K\rangle$, the smallest subgroup containing both. It is the intersection of all subgroups that contain $H\cup K$; concretely it is the set of all finite products $x_1x_2\cdots x_m$ with each $x_i\in H\cup K$. It is the smallest subgroup lying above both $H$ and $K$.
\end{itemize}
We write $H\wedge K=H\cap K$ and $H\vee K=\langle H,K\rangle$.
\end{definition}

