Type: example
Title: Lattice of $V_4$
Content:
\begin{wex}{Lattice of $V_4$}
Draw the subgroup lattice of the Klein four-group $V_4=\{1,a,b,c\}$ with $a^2=b^2=c^2=1$ and $ab=c$.
\wrec
This is a request to ``build the lattice'' (the four-step Method). $|V_4|=4$, so by Lagrange the only possible subgroup orders are $1,2,4$. There is no element of order $4$ (every non-identity element is an involution), so $V_4$ is non-cyclic; its order-$2$ subgroups must be found directly.
\wsol
Step 1 (subgroups by order). Order $1$: $\{1\}$. Order $4$: $V_4$ itself. Order $2$: each is generated by an involution, $\langle a\rangle=\{1,a\}$, $\langle b\rangle=\{1,b\}$, $\langle c\rangle=\{1,c\}$. These three are distinct, and there are no others (any subgroup of order $2$ is $\{1,g\}$ with $g^2=1$, $g\ne1$). Total: $5$ subgroups.

Step 2 (heights). Bottom $1$; middle row $\langle a\rangle,\langle b\rangle,\langle c\rangle$ (order $2$); top $V_4$.

Step 3 (covering edges). Each $\langle a\rangle,\langle b\rangle,\langle c\rangle$ contains $1$ with index $2$ (prime), so each covers $1$: three edges up from the bottom. Each is contained in $V_4$ with index $2$ (prime), so $V_4$ covers each: three edges to the top. No edge from $1$ to $V_4$ directly.
 \begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=1.05,xscale=1.4]
 \node[ltdot] (bot) at (0,0) {};
 \node[ltdot] (a) at (-1.4,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (b) at (0,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (c) at (1.4,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (top) at (0,2) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (bot)--(a);
 \draw (bot)--(b);
 \draw (bot)--(c);
 \draw (a)--(top);
 \draw (b)--(top);
 \draw (c)--(top);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,below=1.5pt of bot] {$1$};
 \node[ltlab,left=3pt of a] {$\langle a\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,right=3pt of b] {$\langle b\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,right=3pt of c] {$\langle c\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,above=1.5pt of top] {$V_4$};
\end{tikzpicture} 

Check. The ``diamond'' shape has $1$ at bottom, three pairwise-incomparable atoms, and $V_4$ on top, which matches the known fact that $V_4\congZ/(2)Z\timesZ/(2)Z$ has exactly three subgroups of order $2$. Sanity: $3$ middle $+2$ ($1$ and $V_4$) $=5$ subgroups.
\end{wex}

