Content:
\begin{wex}{Divisor lattice of $Z/(12)Z$}
Draw the subgroup lattice of $Z/(12)Z=\langle x\rangle$, $\ord{x}=12$.
\wrec
$G$ is cyclic, so Proposition~ [REF:p:cycliclattice@c2] applies: subgroups $\leftrightarrow$ divisors of $12$, and the Hasse diagram is the divisor lattice of $12$. No element-by-element search is needed.
\wsol
Step 1. Divisors of $12$ are $1,2,3,4,6,12$, giving one subgroup of each order:
\[
1=\langle x^{12}\rangle,\ \langle x^6\rangle\ (\ord 2),\ \langle x^4\rangle\ (\ord 3),\ \langle x^3\rangle\ (\ord 4),\ \langle x^2\rangle\ (\ord 6),\ \langle x\rangle=Z/(12)Z.
\]

Step 2. Heights by order: $1$ at the bottom; $\langle x^6\rangle$ (order $2$) and $\langle x^4\rangle$ (order $3$) low; $\langle x^3\rangle$ (order $4$) and $\langle x^2\rangle$ (order $6$) high; $Z/(12)Z$ on top.

Step 3 (coverings via prime index). $1\lessdot\langle x^6\rangle$ (index $2$), $1\lessdot\langle x^4\rangle$ (index $3$). Then $\langle x^6\rangle\lessdot\langle x^2\rangle$ (orders $2\mid6$, ratio $3$) and $\langle x^6\rangle\lessdot\langle x^3\rangle$ (orders $2\mid4$, ratio $2$); $\langle x^4\rangle\lessdot\langle x^2\rangle$ (orders $3\mid6$, ratio $2$). Finally $\langle x^3\rangle\lessdotZ/(12)Z$ (index $3$) and $\langle x^2\rangle\lessdotZ/(12)Z$ (index $2$). The result is a $2\times3$ ``grid'' (product of a $2$-chain and a $3$-chain), matching $12=2^2\cdot3$.
 \begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=1.0,xscale=1.3]
 \node[ltdot] (bot) at (0,0) {};
 \node[ltdot] (x6) at (-1.2,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (x4) at (1.2,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (x3) at (-1.2,2) {};
 \node[ltdot] (x2) at (1.2,2) {};
 \node[ltdot] (top) at (0,3) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (bot)--(x6);
 \draw (bot)--(x4);
 \draw (x6)--(x3);
 \draw (x6)--(x2);
 \draw (x4)--(x2);
 \draw (x3)--(top);
 \draw (x2)--(top);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,below=1.5pt of bot] {$1$};
 \node[ltlab,left=3pt of x6] {$\langle x^6\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,right=3pt of x4] {$\langle x^4\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,left=3pt of x3] {$\langle x^3\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,right=3pt of x2] {$\langle x^2\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,above=1.5pt of top] {$Z/12$};
\end{tikzpicture} 

Check. Six subgroups, matching the six divisors of $12$. Every maximal chain from $1$ to $Z/(12)Z$ has two index-$2$ steps and one index-$3$ step (e.g.\ $1\to\langle x^4\rangle\to\langle x^2\rangle\toZ/(12)Z$ has indices $3,2,2$), confirming the $2\times3$ grid for $12=2^2\cdot3$.
\end{wex}

