Type: example
Title: Lattice of $S_3$
Content:
\begin{wex}{Lattice of $S_3$}
Draw the subgroup lattice of $S_3$ (the symmetric group on $\{1,2,3\}$, order $6$).
\wrec
$|S_3|=6$, so Lagrange allows subgroup orders $1,2,3,6$. $S_3$ is non-cyclic, so we search by order: order-$2$ subgroups come from transpositions, the order-$3$ subgroup from a $3$-cycle. This is the Method's Step 1 done by hand.
\wsol
Step 1 (subgroups). The six elements are $e$, the transpositions $(1\,2),(1\,3),(2\,3)$ (each of order $2$), and the $3$-cycles $(1\,2\,3),(1\,3\,2)$ (each of order $3$).
\begin{itemize}
\item Order $1$: $\{e\}$.
\item Order $2$: $\langle (1\,2)\rangle=\{e,(1\,2)\}$, $\langle (1\,3)\rangle=\{e,(1\,3)\}$, $\langle (2\,3)\rangle=\{e,(2\,3)\}$ --- three of them.
\item Order $3$: $\langle (1\,2\,3)\rangle=\{e,(1\,2\,3),(1\,3\,2)\}$ --- exactly one, since $(1\,3\,2)=(1\,2\,3)^2$ generates the same group.
\item Order $6$: $S_3$ itself.
\end{itemize}
Total $6$ subgroups.

Step 2. Bottom $1$; middle row the three order-$2$ groups and the one order-$3$ group; top $S_3$.

Step 3 (coverings). Each order-$2$ subgroup covers $1$ (index $2$, prime) and is covered by $S_3$ (index $3$, prime). The order-$3$ subgroup covers $1$ (index $3$) and is covered by $S_3$ (index $2$). No two distinct middle subgroups are comparable (different prime orders, or two order-$2$ groups share only $e$), so no horizontal edges.
