\begin{wex}{Lattice of $Z/(18)Z$ (type $p^2q$)}
Draw the subgroup lattice of $Z/(18)Z=\langle x\rangle$, $\ord{x}=18=2\cdot3^2$.
\wrec
Cyclic of order $18$, so Proposition~ [REF:p:cycliclattice@c2] : subgroups $\leftrightarrow$ divisors of $18$. With $18=2\cdot3^2$ the divisor lattice is a $2\times3$ grid (a $2$-chain from the prime $2$ crossed with a $3$-chain from $3^2$), the same shape as $Z/(12)Z$ but with the chains swapped in length.
\wsol
Step 1. Divisors $1,2,3,6,9,18$ give one subgroup each:
\[
1=\langle x^{18}\rangle,\ \langle x^9\rangle\ (\ord2),\ \langle x^6\rangle\ (\ord3),\ \langle x^3\rangle\ (\ord6),\ \langle x^2\rangle\ (\ord9),\ \langle x\rangle=Z/(18)Z.
\]

Step 2--3 (coverings via prime index). $1\lessdot\langle x^9\rangle$ (index $2$), $1\lessdot\langle x^6\rangle$ (index $3$). Then $\langle x^9\rangle\lessdot\langle x^3\rangle$ (orders $2\mid6$, ratio $3$); $\langle x^6\rangle\lessdot\langle x^3\rangle$ (orders $3\mid6$, ratio $2$) and $\langle x^6\rangle\lessdot\langle x^2\rangle$ (orders $3\mid9$, ratio $3$). Finally $\langle x^3\rangle\lessdotZ/(18)Z$ (index $3$) and $\langle x^2\rangle\lessdotZ/(18)Z$ (index $2$). Note the order-$9$ branch $1\to\langle x^6\rangle\to\langle x^2\rangle$ is a length-$2$ chain (powers of $3$), while the order-$2$ direction adds a single rung.
