\begin{pitfall}
In $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ the subgroup ``$\langle m\rangle$'' means the cyclic subgroup generated by the residue $m$, and $|\langle m\rangle|=n/\gcd(m,n)$, \emph{not} $n/m$. For $\mathbb{Z}/(24)\mathbb{Z}$, $\langle 6\rangle$ has order $24/\gcd(6,24)=24/6=4$, but $\langle 8\rangle$ has order $24/\gcd(8,24)=24/8=3$. Compute the gcd; do not just divide by the label.
\end{pitfall}

\begin{wex}{Subgroup of $\mathbb{Z}/(24)\mathbb{Z}$ of each order, and one containment}
List the eight subgroups of $\mathbb{Z}/(24)\mathbb{Z}$ with their orders, and decide whether $\langle 6\rangle\sgr\langle 2\rangle$.
\wrec Cyclic group of order $24$ $\Rightarrow$ Theorem~\ref{t:cyclic@c2}: subgroups correspond to divisors of $24$, and containment is divisibility of orders. The containment question is answered purely by ``does $|\langle 6\rangle|$ divide $|\langle 2\rangle|$?''
\wsol Divisors of $24$: $1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24$. Order-$d$ subgroup is $\langle 24/d\rangle$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
d & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 & 8 & 12 & 24\\\hline
\langle 24/d\rangle & \langle 24\rangle=1 & \langle 12\rangle & \langle 8\rangle & \langle 6\rangle & \langle 4\rangle & \langle 3\rangle & \langle 2\rangle & \langle 1\rangle
\end{array}
\]
Now $|\langle 6\rangle|=24/\gcd(6,24)=4$ and $|\langle 2\rangle|=24/\gcd(2,24)=12$. Since $4\mid12$, Theorem~\ref{t:cyclic@c2} gives $\langle 6\rangle\sgr\langle 2\rangle$.

\emph{Check directly:} $\langle 6\rangle=\{0,6,12,18\}$ and $\langle 2\rangle=\{0,2,4,\dots,22\}$; indeed $6,12,18$ are even, so $\langle 6\rangle\subseteq\langle 2\rangle$. Consistent.
\end{wex}

\begin{method}{Using the lattice to (a) spot normal subgroups and (b) locate $N_G(H)$}
\textit{Recognition trigger.} Use this whenever you have the lattice drawn and want normality or a normalizer \emph{without} brute-force conjugating every element.

\textbf{(a) Normal subgroups.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Any subgroup of index $2$ (one level below the top, with $|G:H|=2$) is normal --- mark it immediately.
\item The center $Z(G)$ and any subgroup contained in $Z(G)$ is normal; locate $Z(G)$ as the largest subgroup every node is ``compatible'' with (in $D_8$ and $Q_8$ this is the unique central involution's group).
\item A subgroup that is the \emph{unique} subgroup of its order is characteristic, hence normal (conjugation permutes same-order subgroups, and a singleton orbit is fixed).
\item For the remaining small subgroups, conjugation permutes the subgroups of each fixed order; $H\nor G$ iff that orbit is $\{H\}$. Read off the same-order siblings from the lattice and test whether a single conjugation moves $H$ to a sibling.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{(b) The normalizer $N_G(H)$.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item $N_G(H)$ is a subgroup with $H\sgr N_G(H)\sgr G$, so it lies on the chain \emph{above} $H$ in the lattice --- only those finitely many candidates can occur.
\item $H\nor G$ $\Leftrightarrow$ $N_G(H)=G$ (top). If $H$ is not normal, $N_G(H)$ is a \emph{proper} subgroup containing $H$.
\item Test candidates from largest down: $g\in N_G(H)$ iff $gHg^{-1}=H$. The first proper overgroup $K$ with every generator of $K$ normalizing $H$, and with no larger overgroup doing so, is $N_G(H)$.
\item Sanity: $|G:N_G(H)|$ equals the number of distinct conjugates $gHg^{-1}$ (the orbit size), which you can also count among the same-order siblings in the lattice.
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

\begin{wex}{$N_{Q_8}(\langle i\rangle)$ and $N_{\mathbb{Z}/(24)\mathbb{Z}}(\langle 6\rangle)$}
Find both normalizers using the lattices.
\wrec In an abelian group every subgroup is normal, so $N_G(H)=G$ automatically --- that is the trigger for $\mathbb{Z}/(24)\mathbb{Z}$. In $Q_8$ every subgroup is normal (Proposition~\ref{p:q8@c2}), so again $N_G(H)=G$. Both reduce to Method (b) step 2: normal $\Rightarrow$ normalizer is the top.
\wsol \emph{$Q_8$:} $\langle i\rangle$ has index $8/4=2$, hence normal, so $N_{Q_8}(\langle i\rangle)=Q_8$. \emph{$\mathbb{Z}/(24)\mathbb{Z}$:} $\mathbb{Z}/(24)\mathbb{Z}$ is abelian, so $g+\langle 6\rangle-g=\langle 6\rangle$ for all $g$ (equivalently $g\langle 6\rangleg^{-1}=\langle 6\rangle$), giving $N_{\mathbb{Z}/(24)\mathbb{Z}}(\langle 6\rangle)=\mathbb{Z}/(24)\mathbb{Z}$.

\textit{Check.} Normalizer at the top of the lattice $\Leftrightarrow$ single conjugate $\Leftrightarrow$ $|G:N_G(H)|=1$. For $\langle i\rangle$: $j i j^{-1}=-i\in\langle i\rangle$ as computed earlier, so conjugation fixes $\langle i\rangle$, giving one conjugate. For $\langle 6\rangle$: abelian, one conjugate. Both consistent.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Why same lattice does not force isomorphism}
Explain how $Z_2\times Z_8$ and $M_{16}$ can share a subgroup lattice yet fail to be isomorphic, and give a concrete invariant that distinguishes them.
\wrec The trigger is the phrase ``same lattice, not isomorphic.'' The lattice records only \emph{which subgroups contain which}; it is blind to \emph{how} elements multiply (commutativity, conjugation). So to separate the two groups we must exhibit a group-theoretic invariant that the lattice cannot see --- abelian vs.\ non-abelian is the cleanest.
\wsol Both groups have order $16$, and their subgroup lattices coincide combinatorially: each has $11$ subgroups, with exactly $1$ of order $1$, three of order $2$, three of order $4$, three of order $8$, and $1$ of order $16$, arranged with identical inclusions (this is the content of the D\&F remark). Crucially this is \emph{not} because $M_{16}$ has all subgroups normal --- it does not: from $vuv^{-1}=u^5$ one gets $uvu^{-1}=u^4v$, so the order-$2$ subgroup $\langle v\rangle$ is non-normal, with conjugate $\langle u^4v\rangle$. The relation $vuv^{-1}=u^5$ does guarantee that $u^5$ generates the same cyclic subgroups as $u$ (since $\gcd(5,8)=1$, e.g.\ $\langle u^5\rangle=\langle u\rangle$), so conjugation by $v$ fixes every subgroup of $\langle u\rangle$; but that alone does not force equal lattices (in $D_{16}$, $srs^{-1}=r^7$ with $\gcd(7,8)=1$, yet $D_{16}$ has $19$ subgroups). The agreement is a direct check: writing $Z_2\times Z_8=\langle x\rangle\times\langle y\rangle$ with $\ord{x}=2$, $\ord{y}=8$, the bijection $u^iv^j\mapsto y^ix^j$ carries the $11$ subgroups of $M_{16}$ exactly onto the $11$ subgroups of $Z_2\times Z_8$ (e.g.\ $\langle uv\rangle=\{u^{2k},u^{2k+1}v\}\mapsto\{y^{2k},y^{2k+1}x\}=\langle xy\rangle$). Hence the lattices agree even though the multiplication does not.

But the groups differ on a lattice-invisible invariant:
\begin{itemize}
\item $Z_2\times Z_8$ is \emph{abelian}: every pair of elements commutes, so its center is the whole group and $|Z|=16$.
\item $M_{16}$ is \emph{non-abelian}: $vuv^{-1}=u^5\neq u$ (since $u^5\neq u$ as $u$ has order $8$), so $u$ and $v$ do not commute. Its center is $\langle u^2\rangle\cong Z_4$, of order $4$.
\end{itemize}
Since $|Z(Z_2\times Z_8)|=16\neq4=|Z(M_{16})|$, and the order of the center is an isomorphism invariant, the two groups are not isomorphic.

\textit{Check.} An isomorphism must send center to center, so it would force $16=|Z(Z_2\times Z_8)|=|Z(M_{16})|=4$, a contradiction. Equivalently, one group is abelian and the other is not --- an invariant the bare inclusion lattice cannot record. This is precisely why we must verify isomorphism by group operations, never by lattice shape alone.
\end{wex}

\begin{pitfall}
Never conclude ``$G\cong H$ because they have the same subgroup lattice.'' The lattice forgets multiplication. $Z_2\times Z_8$ vs.\ $M_{16}$ (both order $16$, identical lattices, one abelian and one not) is the canonical counterexample. Likewise, do not read commutativity or element orders off a Hasse diagram --- it only encodes containments.
\end{pitfall}

