\begin{wex}{The two smallest lattices: $Z/(1)Z$ and $Z/(p)Z$}
Draw the subgroup lattices of the trivial group and of a cyclic group $Z/(p)Z$ of prime order.
\wrec These are the base cases. A group has the single-vertex lattice iff it is trivial; it has the
two-vertex ``single edge'' lattice iff it has no proper nontrivial subgroup, which for finite groups
(by Lagrange) means prime order.
\wsol Trivial group. $Z/(1)Z=\{1\}$ has only itself as a subgroup, so the lattice is a single point.
 \begin{tikzpicture}
 \node[ltdot] (a) at (0,0) {};
 \node[ltlab,below=1.5pt of a] {$1$};
\end{tikzpicture} 

Prime order. By Lagrange every subgroup of $Z/(p)Z$ has order $1$ or $p$, so the only subgroups are
$1$ and $Z/(p)Z$ itself. Since $[\,Z/(p)Z:1\,]=p$ is prime, $Z/(p)Z$ covers $1$: a single edge.
 \begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=1.0]
 \node[ltdot] (b) at (0,0) {};
 \node[ltdot] (t) at (0,1) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (b)--(t);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,below=1.5pt of b] {$1$};
 \node[ltlab,above=1.5pt of t] {$Z/(p)Z$};
\end{tikzpicture} 

Check. The single-edge lattice is exactly the case where the pitfall ``do not draw $1$ straight to
$G$'' is allowed: there is no proper nontrivial subgroup to interrupt it. For $p=2,3,5,\dots$ the
picture is identical, since the lattice sees only the divisor structure $1\mid p$.
\end{wex}

