Title: Verifying well-definedness of a coset operation
Content:
\begin{method}{Verifying well-definedness of a coset operation}
Trigger. You are asked to show $(aN)(bN)=(ab)N$ makes sense, or to test whether a candidate $N$ yields a quotient.
\begin{enumerate}
 \item Take two names for each input: $a' = a n_1$, $b' = b n_2$ with $n_1,n_2 \in N$.
 \item Compute $a'b' = ab\,(b^{-1} n_1 b)\,n_2$ and ask: is $b^{-1} n_1 b \in N$?
 \item If $N \nor G$, yes for all $b$ -- well defined. If you can find $g,n$ with $gng^{-1} \notin N$, the operation is not well defined; exhibit two representative choices giving different cosets.
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

