Throughout, $\varphi:G\to H$ is a group homomorphism. Recall that its \emph{kernel}
$\ker\varphi=\{g\in G:\varphi(g)=1_H\}$ is a normal subgroup of $G$ and its \emph{image}
$\im\varphi=\{\varphi(g):g\in G\}$ is a subgroup of $H$. We saw in 3.1 that for $N=\ker\varphi$
the fibers of $\varphi$ are exactly the cosets of $N$: $\varphi(x)=\varphi(y)\Longleftrightarrow xN=yN$. The
present section turns this observation into a structural identification of the quotient $G/N$.

\begin{remark}
Two reformulations worth memorizing. (1) \emph{Counting:} if $G$ is finite then
$|\im\varphi|=|G/\ker\varphi|=[G:\ker\varphi]=|G|/|\ker\varphi|$; so $|\im\varphi|$ divides
$|G|$, and also $|H|$ when $H$ is finite. (2) \emph{Size of fibers:} every nonempty fiber $\varphi^{-1}(\{h\})$ is a
coset of $\ker\varphi$, hence has exactly $|\ker\varphi|$ elements.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The First Isomorphism Theorem is the special case $K=\im\varphi$, $\psi=\varphi$ (codomain
restricted to the image) and $N=\ker\varphi$: then $N=\ker\psi$, so the induced map is not only
unique but an \emph{isomorphism}. In this sense the universal property is the engine and the
isomorphism theorem is its sharpest consequence.
\end{remark}

\begin{pitfall}
The map $gN\mapsto\varphi(g)$ lands in $\im\varphi$, \emph{not necessarily in all of} $H$.
The induced map $G/\ker\varphi\to H$ is an isomorphism only when $\varphi$ is surjective. Writing
``$G/\ker\varphi\cong H$'' for a non-surjective $\varphi$ is the single most common error.
Always check onto, or write $\im\varphi$.
\end{pitfall}

