Course: abstract algebra notes
Section: Quotient Groups and Homomorphisms > The Isomorphism Theorems > The fourth (lattice) isomorphism theorem
Type: example
Title: Lattice for $G/N$ with $G=Z/12Z$, $N=\langle 6\rangle$
Part: 1/2
Content:
\begin{wex}{Lattice for $G/N$ with $G=Z/12Z$, $N=\langle 6\rangle$}
Let $G=Z/12Z$ and $N=\langle 6\rangle=\{0,6\}$, $|N|=2$, so $|G/N|=6$ and $G/N\congZ/6Z$. List
all subgroups of $G/N$ via the correspondence.
\wrec Fourth Iso Theorem: subgroups of $G/N$ $\leftrightarrow$ subgroups of $Z/12$
containing $\langle 6\rangle$.
\wsol Subgroups of $Z/12$ are the $\langle d\rangle$ for $d\mid12$: $\langle 1\rangle=Z/12$, $\langle 2\rangle$, $\langle 3\rangle$,
$\langle 4\rangle$, $\langle 6\rangle$, $\langle 12\rangle=\langle 0\rangle=\{0\}$. Those containing $N=\langle 6\rangle$ are exactly the
ones with $6\in\langle d\rangle$, i.e.\ $d\mid6$: $\langle 1\rangle,\langle 2\rangle,\langle 3\rangle,\langle 6\rangle$. (The subgroups $\langle 4\rangle$
and $\{0\}$ do not contain $6$, so they drop out.) These four correspond to the four
subgroups of $G/N\congZ/6$:
\[\langle 1\rangle=Z/12\ \to\ Z/6,\quad \langle 2\rangle\ \to\ \langle 2\rangle\congZ/3,\quad
\langle 3\rangle\ \to\ \langle 3\rangle\congZ/2,\quad \langle 6\rangle=N\ \to\ \{0\}.\]
Check (count). $Z/6$ has divisors $1,2,3,6$ of $6$, hence exactly $4$ subgroups --
matching the $4$ upstairs. Index check: $[\langle 1\rangle:\langle 2\rangle]=12/6=2$ upstairs equals
$[Z/6:Z/3]=6/3=2$ downstairs. Good. The correspondence is best seen as two side-by-side diamonds: the subgroups of $Z/12$
containing $N=\langle 6\rangle$ (left) sit rung-for-rung opposite all the subgroups of
$G/N\congZ/6$ (right), with faint ties joining matched pairs.
 \begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=1.15,xscale=1.0]

 \node[ltdot] (Lb) at (0,0) {};
 \node[ltdot] (L3) at (-1.1,1.1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (L2) at (1.1,1.7) {};
 \node[ltdot] (Lt) at (0,2.8) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (Lb)--(L3); \draw (Lb)--(L2);
 \draw (L3)--(Lt); \draw (L2)--(Lt);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,below=1.5pt of Lb] {$\langle 6\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,left=3pt of L3] {$\langle 3\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,above right=0pt and 1pt of L2] {$\langle 2\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,above=1.5pt of Lt] {$Z/12$};

 \node[ltdot] (Rb) at (6,0) {};
 \node[ltdot] (R3) at (4.9,1.1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (R2) at (7.1,1.7) {};
 \node[ltdot] (Rt) at (6,2.8) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (Rb)--(R3); \draw (Rb)--(R2);
 \draw (R3)--(Rt); \draw (R2)--(Rt);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,below=1.5pt of Rb] {$\{0\}$};
 \node[ltlab,above left=0pt and 1pt of R3] {$\langle \overline{3}\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,right=3pt of R2] {$\langle \overline{2}\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,above=1.5pt of Rt] {$Z/6$};

 \begin{scope}[ltcorr]
 \draw (Lb)--(Rb);
 \draw (L3)--(R3);
 \draw (L2)--(R2);
 \draw (Lt)--(Rt);
 \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture} 

\begin{dgx}
\dgi{Objects:} Two diamond lattices placed side by side. Left diamond: the four subgroups of
 $Z/12Z$ that contain $N=\langle 6\rangle$, namely $\langle 6\rangle$ (bottom), $\langle 3\rangle$ (left), $\langle 2\rangle$
 (right), and $Z/12$ (top). Right diamond: all four subgroups of $G/N\congZ/6$, namely
 $\{0\}$ (bottom), $\langle \overline{3}\rangle\congZ/2$ (left), $\langle \overline{2}\rangle\congZ/3$
 (right), and $Z/6$ (top). The horizontal ties connect matched pairs.
\dgi{Arrows:} The slanted edges within each diamond are subgroup inclusions (upward = larger
 subgroup). Horizontal tie-lines are the bijection $A\mapsto A/N$ of the Fourth Isomorphism
 Theorem (left to right) and its inverse $\overline{A}\mapsto\pi^{-1}(\overline{A})$ (right
 to left). Each tie connects $A$ on the left to $A/N$ on the right, realized as the image of
 $A$ under the canonical projection $\pi\colonZ/12\toZ/6$.
\dgi{What commutes:} Preservation of inclusion: if $A\sgr B$ upstairs (left edge), then
 $A/N\sgr B/N$ downstairs (right edge), so the tie-lines respect the heights of the rungs. Preservation of index: the index $[B:A]$ on the left equals $[B/N:A/N]$ on the
 right; e.g.\ $[\langle 2\rangle:\langle 6\rangle]=3$ left equals $[\langle \overline{2}\rangle:\{0\}]=3$
 right.
\dgi{Why it commutes:} For the inclusion: if $a\in A\sgr B$, then $a+N\in A/N\sgr B/N$,
 immediate. For the index: cosets of $A$ in $B$ biject with cosets of $A/N$ in $B/N$ via
 $\pi$ (since $N\sgr A$), giving equal indices.
\dgi{Why this matters here:} This picture makes the Fourth Isomorphism Theorem concrete: the
 student can verify by inspection that the two diamonds are identical in shape (isomorphic as
 lattices), that heights are preserved (no rung-crossings in the ties), and that the index
 along each left edge equals the index along the corresponding right edge. The picture
 replaces a proof with a visual check.
\dgi{Concrete example:} The diagram itself is the example: $G=Z/12$, $N=\langle 6\rangle$,
 $G/N\congZ/6$. The tie $\langle 3\rangle\leftrightarrow\langle \overline{3}\rangle$ records that
 $\pi(\langle 3\rangle)=\langle 3\rangle/\langle 6\rangle$, which is the unique subgroup of order $2$ in $Z/6$, namely
 $\langle \overline{3}\rangle$.
\dgi{Common pitfall:} Students sometimes try to include $\langle 4\rangle$ or $\{0\}$ in the left
 diamond, forgetting the ``contains $N=\langle 6\rangle$'' restriction. Neither $4$ nor $0$ generates
 a subgroup containing $6$, so they are invisible upstairs and have no tie-line.

