Course: abstract algebra notes
Section: Quotient Groups and Homomorphisms > Composition Series and the H\"older Program > Composition series, Jordan--H\"older, and solvable groups
Type: example
Title: Composition series of $Z/(12)Z$
Content:
\begin{wex}{Composition series of $Z/(12)Z$}
Give a composition series of $Z/(12)Z$ and list its composition factors.
\wrec Bottom-up via prime-index steps: $12=2\cdot 2\cdot 3$, so build a chain whose
successive indices are these primes.
\wsol Work additively in $Z/(12)Z=\{0,1,\dots,11\}$. The subgroups are exactly the
$\langle d\rangle$ for $d\mid 12$, with $\langle d\rangle$ of order $12/\gcd(d,12)$; here $\langle 6\rangle$ has
order $2$ and $\langle 3\rangle$ has order $4$. One chain:
\[
0\ \nor\ \langle 6\rangle\ \nor\ \langle 3\rangle\ \nor\ Z/(12)Z,
\]
with $|\langle 6\rangle|=2$, $|\langle 3\rangle|=4$ (note $6=2\cdot 3\in\langle 3\rangle$, so $\langle 6\rangle\le\langle 3\rangle$).
All subgroups of an abelian group are normal, so this is subnormal. Factors and their
orders:
\[
\langle 6\rangle/0\congZ/(2)Z,\quad
\langle 3\rangle/\langle 6\rangle\ has order 4/2=2\congZ/(2)Z,\quad
Z/(12)Z/\langle 3\rangle\ has order 12/4=3\congZ/(3)Z.
\]
Each factor is of prime order, hence simple. So this is a composition series, with factors
\[\boxed{Z/(2)Z,\ Z/(2)Z,\ Z/(3)Z}.\]
Check. $2\cdot 2\cdot 3=12=|Z/(12)Z|$. A different chain
$0\nor\langle 4\rangle\nor\langle 2\rangle\norZ/(12)Z$ (with $|\langle 4\rangle|=3$, $|\langle 2\rangle|=6$) has indices $3,2,2$,
giving factors $Z/(3)Z,Z/(2)Z,Z/(2)Z$ --- the same multiset, as Jordan--H\"older requires.

Both chains are vertical towers, each rung labeled by its composition factor; the two
differ in the order of the factors but produce the same multiset
$\{Z/(2)Z,Z/(2)Z,Z/(3)Z\}$ --- exactly the Jordan--H\"older invariance.
 \begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=1.25,xscale=1.0]

 \node[ltdot] (Ab) at (0,0) {};
 \node[ltdot] (A6) at (0,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (A3) at (0,2) {};
 \node[ltdot] (At) at (0,3) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (Ab)--(A6); \draw (A6)--(A3); \draw (A3)--(At);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,below=1.5pt of Ab] {$0$};
 \node[ltlab,left=3pt of A6] {$\langle 6\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,left=3pt of A3] {$\langle 3\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,above=1.5pt of At] {$Z/(12)Z$};
 \node[ltlab,right=2pt of $(Ab)!0.5!(A6)$] {$Z/(2)Z$};
 \node[ltlab,right=2pt of $(A6)!0.5!(A3)$] {$Z/(2)Z$};
 \node[ltlab,right=2pt of $(A3)!0.5!(At)$] {$Z/(3)Z$};

 \node[ltdot] (Bb) at (4,0) {};
 \node[ltdot] (B4) at (4,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (B2) at (4,2) {};
 \node[ltdot] (Bt) at (4,3) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (Bb)--(B4); \draw (B4)--(B2); \draw (B2)--(Bt);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,below=1.5pt of Bb] {$0$};
 \node[ltlab,left=3pt of B4] {$\langle 4\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,left=3pt of B2] {$\langle 2\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,above=1.5pt of Bt] {$Z/(12)Z$};
 \node[ltlab,right=2pt of $(Bb)!0.5!(B4)$] {$Z/(3)Z$};
 \node[ltlab,right=2pt of $(B4)!0.5!(B2)$] {$Z/(2)Z$};
 \node[ltlab,right=2pt of $(B2)!0.5!(Bt)$] {$Z/(2)Z$};
\end{tikzpicture} 
\noindent Orders climb $1,2,4,12$ on the left and $1,3,6,12$ on the right; the edge labels are
the successive quotients, and both towers list $Z/(2)Z,Z/(2)Z,Z/(3)Z$.
\end{wex}

