Content:
\begin{theorem}[Geometric classification of plane isometries] 
Every isometry of the plane is exactly one of:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] orientation-preserving ($\det = +1$):
 \begin{itemize}
 \item[(i)] a translation $t_v$ (the case $\theta = 0$), with no fixed point unless $v = 0$;
 \item[(ii)] a rotation through a nonzero angle $\theta$ about some point $p$ (a unique fixed point);
 \end{itemize}
\item[(b)] orientation-reversing ($\det = -1$):
 \begin{itemize}
 \item[(i)] a reflection in a line $\ell$ (the fixed-point set is the whole line $\ell$);
 \item[(ii)] a glide reflection: reflection in a line $\ell$ followed by translation by a nonzero vector parallel to $\ell$ (no fixed point).
