Section: Group Actions > Plane Crystallographic (Wallpaper) Groups > Glide vs.\ ordinary reflection: the brick pattern
Type: example
Title: Counting the $D_1$ types: pm, pg, cm
Content:
\begin{wex}{Counting the $D_1$ types: pm, pg, cm}
Problem. The point group $D_1=\{1,r\}$ has a single mirror direction. Explain why it yields three distinct wallpaper types rather than one.
\wrec Step 3 of the recipe: with $\overline G$ fixed at $D_1$, enumerate the ways the single reflection can sit relative to the lattice, plus the lattice shape.
\wsol
With $\overline G=D_1$ the only structure to vary is how the mirror $r$ of $\overline G$ is realized in $G$ and what lattice supports it. There are three inequivalent possibilities:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(pm)] $r$ is realized by an ordinary reflection (its axis is fixed pointwise), on a rectangular lattice;
\item[(pg)] $r$ is realized only by a glide (no fixed mirror line), on a rectangular lattice;
\item[(cm)] both a reflection and a glide occur, forced by a rhombic (centered) lattice.
\end{itemize}
Check. These three exhaust the cases: either there is a true mirror or not (pm vs.\ pg on the rectangular lattice), and the centered lattice gives the third, genuinely different, group cm. This matches the row $D_1\to 3$ types in Theorem~ [REF:art:plane_groups:seventeen@c4] . The same reflection-vs-glide-plus-lattice bookkeeping, applied to $D_2$, produces its $4$ types pmm, pmg, pgg, cmm.
\end{wex}

