Type: method
Title: Stating a group's two forms / converting between them
Content:
\begin{method}{Stating a group's two forms / converting between them}
Trigger. You are handed an abelian group as a product of cyclic groups, or asked for invariant factors / elementary divisors.
\begin{enumerate}
\item To get elementary divisors: split every cyclic factor $Z/(n)Z$ via CRT into $Z/(p^a)Z$ over the primes $p\mid n$. Collect all prime powers (with multiplicity).
\item To get invariant factors: group the elementary divisors by prime. For each prime $p$, write its powers in increasing order from left to right: $p^{e_{1}}\le p^{e_{2}}\le\cdots$ (pad shorter lists with $1$'s on the left). Form a table, one row per prime, right-justified. Multiply each column: rightmost column $\to n_k$ (largest), next $\to n_{k-1}$, \dots. The number of columns is $k=\max_p(\# of p-powers)$.
\item Check. $n_1\mid n_2\mid\cdots\mid n_k$, all $n_i\ge2$, and $\prod n_i = \prod(elem. divisors) = |G_{tors}|$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

