Type: exercise_set
Title: Commutative-diagram exercises.
Content:
\begin{exers}{Commutative-diagram exercises.}
\item Realize the structure theorem for $G=Z_4\timesZ_6$ as a short exact sequence
\[
0\longrightarrow Z^2 \xrightarrow{\ A\ } Z^2 \longrightarrow G\longrightarrow 0,
\]
by writing down the relation matrix and computing its Smith normal form; read off the invariant factors.

Answer. Generators $e_1,e_2$ with relations $4e_1=0$, $6e_2=0$ give the relation (presentation) matrix $A_0=\diag(4,6)$, so $G\congZ^2/A_0Z^2$. Row/column operations over $Z$ reduce $A_0$ to its Smith normal form
\[
A=\diag(2,12),\qquad \det A = 24 = \det A_0,\quad d_1=\gcd(4,6)=2,
\]
since the first invariant factor is the gcd of the entries and the product of the two is the determinant. Hence $G\congZ_2\timesZ_{12}$, invariant factors $2\mid 12$. The sequence
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
0 \arrow[r] & Z^2 \arrow[r, hook, "A"] & Z^2 \arrow[r, two heads] & G \arrow[r] & 0
\end{tikzcd}
\]
is exact with $A$ in diagonal Smith normal form.

\item (Naturality of primary decomposition.) For a prime $p$ and a finite abelian group $G$, let $G_p$ be its $p$-primary part (elements of $p$-power order). Show that every homomorphism $f:G\to H$ of finite abelian groups satisfies $f(G_p)\sgr H_p$, so the squares below commute and primary decomposition is functorial.

Answer. If $x\in G_p$ then $p^a x=0$ for some $a$, so $p^a f(x)=f(p^a x)=0$ and $\ord{f(x)}$ is a power of $p$; thus $f(x)\in H_p$, i.e.\ $f(G_p)\sgr H_p$. Writing $f_p:=f|_{G_p}:G_p\to H_p$ and letting $\pi^G_p,\pi^H_p$ denote projections onto the $p$-primary parts, the diagram
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
G \arrow[r, "f"] \arrow[d, "\pi^G_p"'] & H \arrow[d, "\pi^H_p"]\\
G_p \arrow[r, "f_p"'] & H_p
\end{tikzcd}
\]
commutes for every $p$. Hence $G\cong\prod_p G_p$ is natural in $G$ (the CRT/primary decomposition is a functor), since $f$ is determined by its components $f_p$.

\item (The $p$-th power sequence.) For a finite abelian $p$-group $A$, draw and verify exactness of
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
0 \arrow[r] & A[p] \arrow[r, hook] & A \arrow[r, "p\cdot"] & A \arrow[r, two heads] & A/pA \arrow[r] & 0,
\end{tikzcd}
\]
where $p\cdot$ is multiplication by $p$, and show $A[p]\cong A/pA$ as $\F_p$-vector spaces of equal rank.

Answer. Multiplication-by-$p$ has kernel $A[p]=\{x:px=0\}$ and image $pA$, so the inclusion $A[p]\hookrightarrow A$, the map $p\cdot$, and the quotient $A\twoheadrightarrow A/pA$ form an exact sequence: exactness at the first $A$ is $\ker(p\cdot)=A[p]$, and at the second $A$ is $\im(p\cdot)=pA=\ker(A\to A/pA)$. If $A\congZ_{p^{a_1}}\times\cdots\timesZ_{p^{a_k}}$ with all $a_i\ge 1$, then both $A[p]$ and $A/pA$ pick up exactly one factor of $Z_p$ from each cyclic summand, so each is elementary abelian of rank $k = $ (number of elementary divisors of $A$). Being $\F_p$-spaces of equal dimension $k$, they are isomorphic: $A[p]\cong(Z_p)^k\cong A/pA$.

\item (Lattice comparison.) Draw the subgroup lattice of $Z_2\timesZ_4$ and of $Z_8$, mark the atoms (subgroups of order $2$), and explain how the lattice shape reflects the invariant factors $2\mid 4$ versus the single factor $8$.

Answer. In $Z_2\timesZ_4$ the $2$-torsion is $(Z_2\timesZ_4)[2]\cong(Z_2)^2$, which has $\tfrac{2^2-1}{2-1}=3$ subgroups of order $2$ (three atoms), and the maximal cyclic subgroup $Z_4$ is not unique. In $Z_8$ the $2$-torsion is just $Z_2$, so there is a single atom and a unique chain. In the lattice of $Z_2\timesZ_4$ below, $A_1=\langle (1,0)\rangle$, $A_2=\langle (0,2)\rangle$, $A_3=\langle (1,2)\rangle$ are the atoms and $C_1=\langle (0,1)\rangle\congZ_4$, $C_2=\langle (1,1)\rangle\congZ_4$, $V=\langle (1,0),(0,2)\rangle\cong(Z_2)^2$ are the subgroups of order $4$; $A_2$ lies in all three, while $A_1$ and $A_3$ lie only in $V$:

\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1.05cm]

 \node[ltdot] (top) at (0,3) {};
 \node[ltdot] (c1) at (-2,2) {};
 \node[ltdot] (c2) at (-0.55,2) {};
 \node[ltdot] (v) at (1.6,2) {};
 \node[ltdot] (a2) at (-1.3,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (a1) at (0.9,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (a3) at (2.4,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (bot) at (0,0) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (c1) -- (top);
 \draw (c2) -- (top);
 \draw (v) -- (top);
 \draw (a2) -- (c1);
 \draw (a2) -- (c2);
 \draw (a2) -- (v);
