Section: Direct and Semidirect Products and Abelian Groups > Recognizing Direct Products > Internal direct products: the recognition theorem
Type: exercise_set
Title: Commutative-diagram exercises.
Content:
\begin{exers}{Commutative-diagram exercises.}
\item State and prove the universal property of abelianization: the quotient $\pi\colon G\to G/G'$ is initial among homomorphisms from $G$ to abelian groups. Fill in the dashed unique factorization in the triangle below and justify both existence and uniqueness. \\
Answer. Given $\varphi\colon G\to A$ with $A$ abelian, set $\overline{\varphi}([x])=\varphi(x)$. Existence: $G'\le\ker\varphi$ (commutators map to $1$ in the abelian $A$), so $\overline{\varphi}$ is well-defined and $\varphi=\overline{\varphi}\circ\pi$. Uniqueness: $\pi$ is surjective, so any map agreeing with $\varphi$ after $\pi$ is determined on every $[x]$. Hence $G/G'$ is the universal abelian quotient.
\[\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large]
G \arrow[r, two heads, "\pi"] \arrow[dr, "\varphi"'] & G/G' \arrow[d, dashed, "\overline{\varphi}"] \\
& A
\end{tikzcd}\]

\item Recognition theorem as a commuting diagram. Show that $\psi\colon H\times K\to G$, $(h,k)\mapsto hk$, is an isomorphism (under conditions $H,K\nor G$, $H\cap K=1$, $HK=G$) by exhibiting its inverse via the unique factorization $g=hk$ and checking the square below with the coordinate maps $q_H\colon G\to H$, $q_K\colon G\to K$. \\
Answer. By Step~1, $[H,K]=1$, so $\psi$ is a homomorphism; $HK=G$ gives surjectivity and $H\cap K=1$ gives injectivity (each $g$ has a unique $g=hk$). Define $\psi^{-1}(g)=(q_H(g),q_K(g))$ from that unique factorization; then for $(h,k)\in H\times K$ we have $q_H(\psi(h,k))=q_H(hk)=h=\pi_H(h,k)$, i.e.\ $q_H\circ\psi=\pi_H$, so the square commutes and $G\cong H\times K$ internally.
\[\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large]
H\times K \arrow[r, "\psi", "\cong"'] \arrow[d, "\pi_H"'] & G \arrow[d, "q_H"] \\
H \arrow[r, equal] & H
\end{tikzcd}\]

\item Draw the subgroup-lattice diamond for an internal direct product $G=HK$ (top $G$, shoulders $H,K$ both normal, bottom $1=H\cap K$), and contrast it with the diamond of the semidirect product $D_8=\langle r\rangle\sdp\langle s\rangle$, indicating which shoulder loses normality. \\
Answer. Direct: both shoulders are normal, $H\cap K=1$, $HK=G$, and $[H,K]=1$ --- the diamond is symmetric (both shoulders normal). Semidirect ($D_8$): same diamond but $K=\langle s\rangle$ is not normal ($rsr^{-1}=r^2s\notin\langle s\rangle$), so the right shoulder is a non-normal complement and the symmetry breaks. Normality of both shoulders is exactly what upgrades a semidirect to a direct product.

\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]

 \node[ltdot] (G) at (0,2) {};
 \node[ltdot] (H) at (-1.5,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (K) at (1.5,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (one) at (0,0) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (one) -- (H);
 \draw (one) -- (K);
 \draw (H) -- (G);
 \draw (K) -- (G);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,above=2pt of G] {$G=HK$};
 \node[ltlab,left=2pt of H] {$H$};
 \node[ltlab,right=2pt of K] {$K$};
 \node[ltlab,below=2pt of one] {$1$};

 \begin{scope}[xshift=6cm]
 \node[ltdot] (Gs) at (0,2) {};
 \node[ltdot] (Hs) at (-1.5,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (Ks) at (1.5,1) {};
 \node[ltdot] (ones) at (0,0) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (ones) -- (Hs);
 \draw (ones) -- (Ks);
 \draw (Hs) -- (Gs);
 \draw (Ks) -- (Gs);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,above=2pt of Gs] {$D_8=\langle r\rangle\langle s\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,left=2pt of Hs] {$\langle r\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,right=2pt of Ks] {$\langle s\rangle$};
 \node[ltlab,below=2pt of ones] {$1$};
 \node[ltlab] at ($(Hs)!0.5!(Gs)$) {$\nor$};
 \node[ltlab] at ($(Ks)!0.5!(Gs)$) {$not \nor$};
 \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}


\item Draw the abelianization short exact sequence $1\to G'\to G\to G/G'\to1$ and prove that every abelian quotient $G/N$ factors through it, i.e.\ $G/N$ is abelian $\iff G'\le N$. Indicate the induced commuting triangle when $G'\le N$. \\
Answer. The sequence is exact: $G'\nor G$ with cokernel $G/G'$. By Proposition~ [REF:prop:commutator@c5] (2), $G/N$ is abelian $\iff G'\le N$; in that case the third isomorphism theorem gives $G/N\cong(G/G')/(N/G')$, so $G\to G/N$ factors as $G\xrightarrow{\pi}G/G'\to G/N$, and the triangle below commutes. Functoriality: a homomorphism $G\to H$ carries $G'$ into $H'$, inducing $G/G'\to H/H'$.
\[\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=normal, row sep=large]
1 \arrow[r] & G' \arrow[r, hook] & G \arrow[r, two heads, "\pi"] \arrow[dr, two heads] & G/G' \arrow[d, dashed, two heads] \arrow[r] & 1 \\
& & & G/N &
\end{tikzcd}\]

\end{exers}

