\begin{theorem}[Burnside's $p^aq^b$ theorem]\label{thm:burnside@c6}
Let $p,q$ be primes and $a,b\ge0$. Every group of order $p^aq^b$ is solvable. Equivalently, a group whose order is divisible by at most two distinct primes is solvable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
The classical proof uses character theory of finite groups. One shows: if $G$ is a finite nonabelian simple group, then no nontrivial conjugacy class has size a prime power $p^k$ ($k\ge1$). The key lemma (via the column-orthogonality relations and algebraic-integer arguments on character values $\chi(g)$) is that a conjugacy class of size $p^k$ forces an irreducible character with $|\chi(g)|=|\chi(1)|$ at some $g\ne1$, producing a proper nontrivial normal subgroup -- contradicting simplicity. Now if $|G|=p^aq^b$ with $G$ a minimal counterexample (nonabelian simple), a Sylow $q$-subgroup $Q\ne1$ has nontrivial center; an element $g\in Z(Q)\setminus\{1\}$ has centralizer containing $Q$, so its class size divides $p^a$, i.e. is a power of $p$ -- contradiction. Hence no nonabelian simple group of order $p^aq^b$ exists, and by induction on $|G|$ (using Theorem~\ref{thm:closure@c6}(c) on a minimal normal subgroup) every such group is solvable. A purely group-theoretic proof exists (Goldschmidt--Matsuyama--Bender) but is far longer.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Three distinct primes can fail: $|A_5|=60=2^2\cdot3\cdot5$ has three prime divisors and is not solvable. So ``two primes'' in Burnside is sharp. The smallest non-solvable group is $A_5$.
\end{remark}

\begin{wex}{Using Burnside: orders forcing solvability}
Which of the following orders force every group of that order to be solvable: $200$, $108$, $105$, $60$?
\wrec Apply Burnside (Method shortcut (ii)): solvable is guaranteed if the order has at most two distinct prime factors; also recall $|G|<60\Longrightarrow$ solvable.
\wsol Factor each:
\begin{itemize}
\item $200=2^3\cdot5^2$: two primes $\Longrightarrow$ \textbf{solvable} (Burnside).
\item $108=2^2\cdot3^3$: two primes $\Longrightarrow$ \textbf{solvable} (Burnside).
\item $105=3\cdot5\cdot7$: three primes, so Burnside does not apply directly. We argue by Sylow counting. For the Sylow $7$-subgroups, $n_7\equiv1\pmod7$ and $n_7\mid15$, so $n_7\in\{1,15\}$ (note $15\equiv1\pmod7$, so $15$ is NOT excluded by the congruence). For the Sylow $5$-subgroups, $n_5\equiv1\pmod5$ and $n_5\mid21$, so $n_5\in\{1,21\}$. If both $n_7=15$ and $n_5=21$, the distinct Sylow $7$-subgroups contribute $15\cdot(7-1)=90$ elements of order $7$ and the distinct Sylow $5$-subgroups contribute $21\cdot(5-1)=84$ elements of order $5$; but $90+84=174>105$, impossible. So $n_5=1$ or $n_7=1$: some Sylow subgroup $P$ of prime order ($5$ or $7$) is normal. Then $G/P$ has order $21=3\cdot7$ or $15=3\cdot5$, solvable by Burnside, and $P$ is abelian; by~\ref{thm:closure@c6}(c), $G$ is solvable. \textbf{Every group of order $105$ is solvable} (by Sylow counting $+$\ref{thm:closure@c6}(c), not Burnside alone).
\item $60=2^2\cdot3\cdot5$: three primes; NOT forced. Indeed $A_5$ has order $60$ and is \textbf{not} solvable.
\end{itemize}
\textit{Check.} Burnside gives ``solvable'' for $200,108$ instantly; the congruence alone does NOT pin $n_7=1$ for order $105$ (since $15\equiv1\pmod7$), but the element count does; $60$ is the cautionary case (three primes, and a genuine non-solvable example exists). Consistent with $A_5$ being minimal non-solvable.
\end{wex}

\begin{theorem}[Philip Hall's theorem on Hall subgroups]\label{thm:hall@c6}
Let $G$ be a finite solvable group of order $n$, and let $\pi$ be a set of primes. Write $n=mk$ where $m$ is the $\pi$-part of $n$ (product of the prime powers $p^{a}\,\|\,n$ with $p\in\pi$) and $\gcd(m,k)=1$. A \emph{Hall $\pi$-subgroup} of $G$ is a subgroup of order $m$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] \textbf{(Existence)} $G$ has a Hall $\pi$-subgroup.
\item[(b)] \textbf{(Conjugacy)} Any two Hall $\pi$-subgroups of $G$ are conjugate in $G$.
\item[(c)] \textbf{(Containment)} Every $\pi$-subgroup of $G$ is contained in some Hall $\pi$-subgroup.
\end{enumerate}
Conversely (Hall's converse), if $G$ has a Hall $p'$-subgroup for every prime $p\mid|G|$, then $G$ is solvable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{pitfall}
Hall's theorem is \emph{special to solvable groups}. In $G=A_5$ ($|G|=60=2^2\cdot3\cdot5$), Hall $\pi$-subgroups can fail to exist. A Hall $\{2,5\}$-subgroup would have order $2^2\cdot5=20$ and a Hall $\{3,5\}$-subgroup would have order $3\cdot5=15$, but $A_5$ has no subgroup of order $15$ or $20$. For order $15$: a group of order $15$ is cyclic, so it would contain an element of order $15$; but $A_5$ has no element of order $15$ (element orders in $A_5$ are $1,2,3,5$), so no such subgroup exists. (Note: $A_4\le A_5$ \emph{is} a genuine Hall $\{2,3\}$-subgroup of order $12$, so SOME Hall subgroups happen to exist; the point is that existence for \emph{every} $\pi$ is exactly what fails outside the solvable case.)
\end{pitfall}

\begin{pitfall}
Do not confuse derived length with nilpotency class, and do not assume the derived series and lower central series coincide. For $S_3$: derived series reaches $1$ (length $2$) but lower central series stalls at $A_3$. They agree only in special cases; in general $G^{(i)}\le\gamma_{i+1}$ (the inclusion proved in Proposition~\ref{prop:nilp-solv@c6}), and this inclusion can be strict even in a nilpotent group: in $D_{16}$ (with $r$ of order $8$) the derived series is $D_{16}\ge\langle r^2\rangle\ge1$, so $D_{16}^{(2)}=1$, while $\gamma_3(D_{16})=\langle r^4\rangle\ne1$.
\end{pitfall}

\begin{theorem}[The three structural levers]
Let $G$ be a finite group, $p$ a prime dividing $|G|$, and $P\in\Syl_p(G)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\textnormal{(L1)}] $n_p=1 \Longleftrightarrow P\nor G$. (A unique Sylow $p$-subgroup is normal.)
\item[\textnormal{(L2)}] \textbf{(Sylow counting.)} If $P$ has prime order $p$, then distinct Sylow $p$-subgroups intersect trivially, so the Sylow $p$-subgroups contribute exactly $n_p(p-1)$ elements of order $p$.
\item[\textnormal{(L3)}] \textbf{(Action on $\Syl_p(G)$.)} Conjugation gives a transitive action of $G$ on $\Syl_p(G)$, hence a homomorphism $\varphi\colon G\to \Sym(\Syl_p(G))\cong S_{n_p}$ whose kernel $\ker\varphi=\bigcap_{g\in G}gN_G(P)g^{-1}$ is the largest normal subgroup of $G$ contained in $N_G(P)$ (the \emph{normal core} of $N_G(P)$). If $1<n_p$ and $|G|\nmid n_p!$, then $\ker\varphi$ is a proper nontrivial normal subgroup, so $G$ is not simple.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{method}{The medium-order recipe}
To analyze a group $G$ of order $n=p_1^{a_1}\cdots p_k^{a_k}$ (small $k$, modest exponents):
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{List $n_p$ candidates.} For each prime $p\mid n$ write $|G|=p^a m$ and list the divisors of $m$ that are $\equiv 1\pmod p$. These are the only allowed values of $n_p$.
\item \textbf{Hunt for a forced $n_p=1$.} If for some $p$ the only admissible value is $n_p=1$, then $P\nor G$ (lever L1). You have found a normal subgroup.
\item \textbf{Count elements (lever L2).} If several primes force $n_p>1$ and the corresponding Sylow subgroups have \emph{prime} order, add up the elements of prime order $n_p(p-1)$. If the totals overflow $|G|$, some $n_p$ must collapse to $1$. (This is the ``counting elements'' technique.)
\item \textbf{Embed via the action (lever L3).} If $n_p>1$ is unavoidable but small, map $G\to S_{n_p}$. Either $G$ embeds in $S_{n_p}$ (often a contradiction on order, or it pins $G$ down exactly), or the kernel is a proper normal subgroup.
\item \textit{Conclude.} Normality of a Sylow subgroup $\Longrightarrow$ not simple; often a chain of such facts builds the whole structure (semidirect product decomposition).
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

\begin{pitfall}
$n_p\equiv 1\pmod p$ is a constraint on $n_p$, \emph{not} on $|G|$ directly. A common error is to use the congruence alone and forget that $n_p$ must also divide $m=|G|/p^a$. For $|G|=24=2^3\cdot 3$ and $p=2$: $m=3$, so $n_2\in\{1,3\}$ (divisors of $3$ that are odd), NOT $\{1,3,5,\dots\}$ (all odd numbers). (Testing divisors of $|G|$ instead of $m$ gives the same list: a divisor of $p^a m$ that is $\equiv1\pmod p$ is prime to $p$, hence divides $m$.)
\end{pitfall}

\begin{pitfall}
Lever L2 (disjointness of Sylows) needs $|P|=p$ \emph{prime}. If $|P|=p^2$ two Sylow $p$-subgroups can intersect in a subgroup of order $p$, and the count $n_p(p^2-1)$ overcounts. Do not apply the counting bound blindly when $a\ge 2$.
\end{pitfall}

\begin{wex}{Order $30=2\cdot3\cdot5$ has normal Sylow $3$- and $5$-subgroups}
Let $|G|=30$. Show $G$ has a normal Sylow $5$-subgroup and a normal Sylow $3$-subgroup (so $G$ is not simple).
\wrec This is a squarefree order with three prime factors; primes $3,5$ are large relative to the cofactors, so their $n_p$ congruences are tight. Use lever L2 (counting elements) to rule out both $n_5>1$ and $n_3>1$ simultaneously.
\wsol
\textbf{Step 1 (admissible $n_p$).} Here $30=2\cdot3\cdot5$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $n_5\mid 6$ and $n_5\equiv 1\pmod 5$: divisors of $6$ are $1,2,3,6$; those $\equiv1\pmod5$ are $1,6$. So $n_5\in\{1,6\}$.
\item $n_3\mid 10$ and $n_3\equiv 1\pmod 3$: divisors of $10$ are $1,2,5,10$; those $\equiv1\pmod3$ are $1,10$. So $n_3\in\{1,10\}$.
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Step 2 (counting, lever L2).} Suppose for contradiction $n_5=6$ and $n_3=10$. Each Sylow $5$-subgroup has order $5$, prime, so they meet trivially and give $6\cdot(5-1)=24$ elements of order $5$. Each Sylow $3$-subgroup has order $3$, prime, giving $10\cdot(3-1)=20$ elements of order $3$. But $24+20=44>30=|G|$, impossible.
\textbf{Step 3.} Hence $n_5=1$ or $n_3=1$; either way $G$ has a normal Sylow subgroup, say $P\nor G$ with $|P|\in\{3,5\}$. Let $P_3\in\Syl_3(G)$, $P_5\in\Syl_5(G)$. Since one of $P_3,P_5$ is normal, the product $H:=P_5P_3$ is a subgroup, of order $|P_5||P_3|/|P_5\cap P_3|=5\cdot3/1=15$. A group of order $15=3\cdot5$ is cyclic (as $n_3=n_5=1$ there: $n_5\mid3,\ n_5\equiv1\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 5)\Longrightarrow n_5=1$; $n_3\mid5,\ n_3\equiv1\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 3)\Longrightarrow n_3=1$), so $H\cong\mathbb{Z}_{15}$ is cyclic of index $2$, hence $H\nor G$. Inside $H$ both its Sylow $3$- and Sylow $5$-subgroups are characteristic (unique), and a characteristic subgroup of a normal subgroup is normal in $G$. Therefore the Sylow $3$- and Sylow $5$-subgroups of $G$ are both normal in $G$.
\textit{Check.} With $P_3,P_5\nor G$ we get $P_3P_5\cong\mathbb{Z}_{15}\nor G$ and $G\cong \mathbb{Z}_{15}\sdp\mathbb{Z}_2$; the four homomorphisms $\mathbb{Z}_2\to\Aut(\mathbb{Z}_{15})\cong\mathbb{Z}_2\times\mathbb{Z}_4$ landing in the $2$-torsion $\mathbb{Z}_2\times\mathbb{Z}_2$ recover the four groups of order $30$: $\mathbb{Z}_{30},\ D_{30},\ \mathbb{Z}_3\times D_{10},\ \mathbb{Z}_5\times S_3$. Consistent, and $G$ is never simple.
\end{wex}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the simple group of order $60$]
If $G$ is a simple group of order $60$, then $G\cong A_5$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[A simple group of order $60$ has $n_5=6$ and $n_2\in\{5,15\}$, and embeds in $A_5$]
Let $G$ be simple of order $60=2^2\cdot3\cdot5$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\textnormal{(a)}] $n_5=6$, $n_3=10$, $n_2\in\{5,15\}$.
\item[\textnormal{(b)}] $G$ has a subgroup of index $5$, hence $G\hookrightarrow A_5$, hence $G\cong A_5$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
\textbf{(a) Forcing the $n_p$.}
\begin{itemize}
\item $n_5\mid12$, $n_5\equiv1\pmod5$: candidates $1,6$. Simplicity forbids $n_5=1$ (that would make $P_5\nor G$), so $n_5=6$.
\item $n_3\mid20$, $n_3\equiv1\pmod3$: candidates $1,4,10$. If $n_3=4$, the action $G\to S_4$ on $\Syl_3(G)$ has $|S_4|=24<60$, so a nontrivial proper normal kernel appears, contradicting simplicity. So $n_3=10$ (and $n_3\neq1$ by simplicity).
\item $n_2\mid15$, $n_2\equiv1\pmod2$: candidates $1,3,5,15$. Simplicity kills $n_2=1$. If $n_2=3$, then $G\to S_3$ ($|S_3|=6<60$) has nontrivial proper kernel --- contradiction. So $n_2\in\{5,15\}$.
\end{itemize}
\textbf{(b) A subgroup of index $5$.} We claim $G$ has a subgroup $H$ with $[G:H]=5$. Then the left-multiplication action of $G$ on the $5$ cosets $G/H$ gives $\rho\colon G\to S_5$ whose kernel is the core of $H$. Since $G$ is simple and $\rho$ is nontrivial (transitive on $5\ge2$ points), $\ker\rho=1$, so $G\hookrightarrow S_5$. Then $G\cap A_5\nor G$ is either $1$ or $G$; if it were $1$ then $G\cong G/(G\cap A_5)\hookrightarrow S_5/A_5\cong\mathbb{Z}_2$, impossible for $|G|=60>2$, so $G\subseteq A_5$. As $|G|=60=|A_5|$, $G=A_5$.

It remains to produce the index-$5$ subgroup.
\emph{Case $n_2=5$.} Then $[G:N_G(P_2)]=n_2=5$, so $H:=N_G(P_2)$ has index $5$. Done.
\emph{Case $n_2=15$.} We show two Sylow $2$-subgroups meet nontrivially. If all $15$ Sylow $2$-subgroups (order $4$) were pairwise trivially intersecting, they would contribute $15\cdot(4-1)=45$ elements of $2$-power order $>1$; together with $n_5(5-1)=6\cdot4=24$ elements of order $5$ that already gives $45+24=69>60$, impossible. So pick $P_2\neq Q_2$ with $|P_2\cap Q_2|=2$; set $D:=P_2\cap Q_2$, $|D|=2$, and let $M:=N_G(D)$. Both $P_2,Q_2$ are $2$-groups normalizing their order-$2$ subgroup $D$ (a subgroup of index $2$ in a group of order $4$ is normal), so $P_2,Q_2\subseteq M$, giving $|M|>4$ and $4\mid|M|$, so $|M|\in\{12,20,60\}$. Simplicity rules out $|M|=60$ (else $D\nor G$). If $|M|=20$ then $[G:M]=3$ and $G\to S_3$ has nontrivial kernel --- contradiction. Hence $|M|=12$, so $[G:M]=5$: take $H:=M$, index $5$. Done.

In either case $G$ has a subgroup of index $5$, so by the first paragraph $G\cong A_5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch of the Theorem]
By the Lemma, any simple $G$ of order $60$ has a subgroup of index $5$ and therefore embeds in $A_5$; comparing orders forces $G=A_5$. Conversely $A_5$ is simple: a proper nontrivial normal subgroup would be a union of conjugacy classes including $\{1\}$, with order a divisor of $60$, but the class sizes $1,15,20,12,12$ admit no sub-collection containing $1$ whose sum is a proper nontrivial divisor of $60$. Hence $A_5$ is, up to isomorphism, the unique simple group of order $60$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why $60$ is the threshold]
For every $n<60$ that is not a prime power, the recipe above forces a normal Sylow subgroup or a small-index core, so \emph{no} non-abelian simple group exists below order $60$. Orders like $24,36,48,56$ fall to lever L3 or L2 as shown; $30,105,$ and all $pqr$ fall to L1/L2. The order $60$ is the first where the levers are exactly balanced ($n_5=6$, $n_2\in\{5,15\}$, $n_3=10$ all $>1$, yet an index-$5$ subgroup survives), permitting --- and pinning down uniquely --- the simple group $A_5$.
\end{remark}

\begin{pitfall}
In the $n_2=15$ case of the Lemma it is tempting to count Sylow $2$-subgroups as if disjoint and conclude immediately. They are \emph{not} guaranteed disjoint ($|P_2|=4$ is not prime), and indeed the proof exploits a forced overlap $|P_2\cap Q_2|=2$. The clean ``$n_p(p-1)$'' count is only valid for the prime-order Sylow $5$-subgroups here; for the order-$4$ Sylow $2$-subgroups you must argue via the normalizer $N_G(P_2\cap Q_2)$.
\end{pitfall}

