 \node[ltdot] (one) at (0,0) {};
 \begin{scope}[ltedge]
 \draw (G) -- node[ltlab,above left]{$\nor$} (K);
 \draw (G) -- (H);
 \draw (K) -- (one);
 \draw (H) -- (one);
 \end{scope}
 \node[ltlab,above=2pt of G] {$G=K\rtimes H$};
 \node[ltlab,left=3pt of K] {$K$ (normal Sylow)};
 \node[ltlab,right=3pt of H] {$H$ (complement)};
 \node[ltlab,below=2pt of one] {$1$};
 \draw[-{Stealth[length=2.4mm]},black!70,line width=0.9pt,bend left=8]
 (H) to node[ltlab,below]{$H\to\Aut(K)$} (K);
\end{tikzpicture}


For $|G|=21$: $K=C_7$, $H=C_3$, and the map $C_3\to\Aut(C_7)\cong C_6$ is either
trivial ($C_{21}$) or has image of order $3$ (the nonabelian group, $i=2$). For
$|G|=12$ in Case 3: $K=C_3$, $H$ a Sylow $2$-subgroup, and the map
$H\to\Aut(C_3)\cong C_2$ is nontrivial, giving $Q$ when $H\cong C_4$ and $D_6$
when $H\cong C_2\times C_2$. The trivial action recovers the abelian products,
and the ``rogue'' Case 2 ($s_3=4$, no normal Sylow $3$-subgroup) produces the one
group, $A_4$, that is not such a semidirect product over $C_3$.

