Type: definition
Title: Ring
Content:
\begin{definition}[Ring]
A ring is a set $R$ together with two binary operations $+$ and $\cdot$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $(R,+)$ is an abelian group (with identity $0$ and inverses $-a$);
\item $\cdot$ is associative: $(ab)c = a(bc)$ for all $a,b,c\in R$;
\item the distributive laws hold: $(a+b)c = ac+bc$ and $a(b+c) = ab+ac$.
\end{enumerate}
$R$ is commutative if $ab=ba$ for all $a,b$. $R$ has an identity (or $1$) if there is $1\in R$ with $1\cdot a = a\cdot 1 = a$ for all $a$. Unless stated otherwise our rings have an identity with $1\neq 0$.
\end{definition}

