\begin{method}{Proving a specific ring is a PID}
Trigger. You must show every ideal of an integral domain $R$ is principal, or you are handed a familiar ring ($\F[x]$, $Z$, $Z[i]$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item Cheapest route: exhibit a Euclidean norm $N$ on $R$ (a degree function, absolute value, complex norm). Then ED $\Longrightarrow$ PID is automatic. This handles $Z$ ($N=|\cdot|$), $\F[x]$ ($N=\deg$), $Z[i]$ ($N=\Nm$), $\F$ a field (every nonzero ideal is $R$).
\item No obvious norm: take an arbitrary ideal $I\neq\langle 0\rangle$, choose a ``smallest'' nonzero element by some size measure, and prove every other element is a multiple of it (mimic the ED proof). This is how one shows $Z[\tfrac{1+\sqrt{-19}}{2}]$ is a PID with no Euclidean norm.
\item To DISPROVE PID: produce one ideal that is provably not principal (see the $Z[x]$ method below).
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

