\begin{proof}
Let $\langle p\rangle$ be a nonzero prime ideal of $R$ (every ideal is principal). Suppose $\langle p\rangle\subseteq M$ for some ideal $M$; write $M=\langle m\rangle$. From $\langle p\rangle\subseteq\langle m\rangle$ we get $p\in\langle m\rangle$, so $p=mr$ for some $r\in R$. Since $\langle p\rangle$ is prime, $p$ is a prime element, hence $p\mid m$ or $p\mid r$; that is, either $m\in\langle p\rangle$ or $r\in\langle p\rangle$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $m\in\langle p\rangle$, then $\langle m\rangle\subseteq\langle p\rangle$, so $M=\langle m\rangle=\langle p\rangle$.
\item If $r\in\langle p\rangle$, write $r=ps$. Then $p=mr=mps$, so $p(1-ms)=0$. As $R$ is a domain and $p\neq 0$, $1=ms$, so $m\in\units{R}$ and $M=\langle m\rangle=R$.
\end{itemize}
Thus the only ideals containing $\langle p\rangle$ are $\langle p\rangle$ and $R$, i.e. $\langle p\rangle$ is maximal.
\end{proof}

