\begin{method}{Showing a domain is, or is not, a UFD}
Trigger. Asked to decide whether a given domain has unique factorization.
\begin{enumerate}
\item To prove it fails (norm method). Exhibit one element with two factorizations into irreducibles whose multisets of associate-classes differ. Use a multiplicative norm to (a) prove the factors are irreducible by ruling out intermediate norms, and (b) show the factors across the two factorizations are non-associate (different norms, or not unit multiples). Example: $6$ in $Z[\sqrt{-5}]$.
\item To prove it holds (structure method). Show $R$ is a PID (or Euclidean), then invoke ``PID $\Longrightarrow$ UFD.'' Or verify both axioms directly: ACC on principal ideals gives existence, and ``irreducible $\Longrightarrow$ prime'' gives uniqueness.
\item Inheriting. If $R$ is a UFD then so is $R[x]$ (stated below), so $Z[x]$ and $\F[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ are UFDs.
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

