\begin{wex}{Eisenstein on a cubic}
Show $2x^3+9x^2+6x+3$ is irreducible over $Q$.
\wrec Eisenstein with $p=3$: $3\mid$ all but leading, $3\nmid2$, $9\nmid3$.
\wsol $a_3=2,a_2=9,a_1=6,a_0=3$. With $p=3$: $3\mid9,3\mid6,3\mid3$;
$3\nmid2$; $9\nmid3$. Eisenstein applies, so the polynomial is irreducible over $Q$.
Check. It is primitive ($\gcd(2,9,6,3)=1$), so it is also irreducible in $Z[x]$;
no rational root ($\pm1,\pm3,\pm\tfrac12,\pm\tfrac32$ all fail), consistent.
\end{wex}

