\begin{theorem}[{Maximal ideals of $F[x]$}]
Let $F$ be a field and let $p(x) \in F[x]$. The ideal $(p(x))$ is a maximal ideal of $F[x]$ if and only if $p(x)$ is irreducible in $F[x]$. In that case the quotient ring $F[x]/(p(x))$ is a field.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $F[x]$ is a commutative ring with $1$, an ideal $M$ is maximal iff $F[x]/M$ is a field. We show $(p)$ is maximal $\Longleftrightarrow$ $p$ is irreducible.

$(\Leftarrow)$ Suppose $p$ is irreducible. Then $p$ is nonzero and nonunit, so $(p) \neq F[x]$ and $(p) \neq (0)$. Let $I$ be an ideal with $(p) \subseteq I \subseteq F[x]$. Because $F[x]$ is a PID, $I = (a(x))$ for some $a$. From $(p) \subseteq (a)$ we get $a \mid p$, say $p = a b$. Irreducibility of $p$ forces $a$ or $b$ to be a unit. If $a$ is a unit then $I = (a) = F[x]$; if $b$ is a unit then $a = p b^{-1}$, so $(a) = (p)$, i.e.\ $I = (p)$. Thus no ideal lies strictly between $(p)$ and $F[x]$, so $(p)$ is maximal.

$(\Rightarrow)$ Suppose $(p)$ is maximal. Then $(p) \neq F[x]$, so $p$ is not a unit, and $(p) \neq (0)$ (the zero ideal is not maximal in $F[x]$, since $(0) \subsetneq (x) \subsetneq F[x]$), so $p \neq 0$. If $p = ab$ with neither a unit, then $(p) \subseteq (a) \subseteq F[x]$. We cannot have $(a) = F[x]$ (that would make $a$ a unit), so maximality forces $(a) = (p)$, hence $a$ and $p$ are associates and $b$ is a unit --- a contradiction. So $p$ is irreducible.

The final clause is the standard fact: $M$ maximal $\Rightarrow F[x]/M$ a field.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[{Prime ideals of $F[x]$}]
Let $F$ be a field and $p(x) \in F[x]$. The ideal $(p(x))$ is a prime ideal of $F[x]$ if and only if $p(x)$ is irreducible \emph{or} $p(x) = 0$. Equivalently: the prime ideals of $F[x]$ are exactly $(0)$ and the ideals $(p)$ with $p$ irreducible, and every nonzero prime ideal is maximal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(p)$ is prime iff $F[x]/(p)$ is an integral domain.

If $p = 0$, then $F[x]/(0) \cong F[x]$, which is a domain, so $(0)$ is prime.

If $p$ is a nonzero nonunit: in a PID (indeed any UFD) $p$ is prime as an element iff $p$ is irreducible, and $(p)$ is a prime ideal iff $p$ is a prime element. Directly: if $p$ is irreducible and $p \mid ab$, then since $F[x]$ is a UFD, $p$ (being irreducible) divides $a$ or $b$, so $(p)$ is prime. Conversely if $(p)$ is prime and $p = ab$ with $a,b$ nonunits, then $ab \in (p)$ forces $a \in (p)$ or $b \in (p)$; say $a = pc$, then $p = pcb$, so $cb = 1$ (cancel $p$ in the domain $F[x]$), making $b$ a unit --- contradiction. Hence $p$ is irreducible.

If $p$ is a nonzero unit, $(p) = F[x]$, which is not prime (a prime ideal is proper). This finishes the classification. By the previous theorem each nonzero prime $(p)$ (with $p$ irreducible) is maximal.
\end{proof}

