\item[(ii)] $>$ is compatible with multiplication: if $x^\alpha > x^\beta$ then $x^\gamma x^\alpha > x^\gamma x^\beta$ for every $\gamma$, i.e. $\alpha>\beta \Longrightarrow \alpha+\gamma > \beta+\gamma$.
\end{enumerate}
We identify monomials with their exponent vectors, so a monomial ordering is a total well-order on $\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}^n$ respecting addition. Conditions (i) and (ii) together force $x^\alpha \ge 1 = x^0$ for all $\alpha$: if $x^\alpha < 1$ then (ii) gives $1 > x^\alpha > x^{2\alpha} > x^{3\alpha} > \cdots$, a set of monomials with no least element, contradicting (i).
\end{definition}

