Type: definition
Title: Left $R$-module
Content:
\begin{definition}[Left $R$-module]
Let $R$ be a ring with $1$. A left $R$-module (or left module over $R$) is an abelian group $(M,+)$ together with an action
\[
R\times M\longrightarrow M,\qquad (r,m)\longmapsto rm,
\]
called (left) scalar multiplication, such that for all $r,s\in R$ and all $m,n\in M$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(M1)] $(r+s)m = rm + sm$ \hfill (distributivity over ring addition);
\item[(M2)] $(rs)m = r(sm)$ \hfill (associativity of the action);
\item[(M3)] $r(m+n) = rm + rn$ \hfill (distributivity over module addition);
\item[(M4)] $1m = m$ \hfill (the identity acts as identity).
\end{enumerate}
A module satisfying (M4) is called unital; we assume (M4) always. A right $R$-module is defined symmetrically with the action written $m\mapsto mr$ and (M2) reading $m(rs)=(mr)s$.
\end{definition}

