Type: method
Title: Building or reading an $F[x]$-module
Content:
\begin{method}{Building or reading an $F[x]$-module}
Recognition trigger: the ring is $F[x]$ (or $R[x]$, $C[x]$, $Z/(p)Z[x]$\,), or you see a vector space with a distinguished operator/matrix.
\begin{enumerate}
\item To build one: choose an $F$-vector space $V$ and an operator $T$ (often a matrix). Decree $x\cdot v=Tv$, hence $x^k\cdot v=T^k v$, and extend $F$-linearly: $\bigl(\sum a_i x^i\bigr)\cdot v=\sum a_i T^i v$.
\item To read one: the operator is $T(v)=x\cdot v$; the $F$-vector space structure is the action of the constants.
\item Submodules $=$ $T$-invariant subspaces (see below). Distinct $T$'s can give isomorphic modules iff the matrices are similar (Ch.\ 12).
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

