\begin{wex}{Submodules of $\,_RR$ are exactly the left ideals}
Let $R$ be a ring with $1$ and view $R$ as a left module over itself. Show: the submodules of $\,_RR$ are precisely the left ideals of $R$.
\wrec The module action is ring multiplication, so closure under the action $=$ absorption of left multiplication. The \emph{rule \& why}: ``$rn\in N$ for all $r\in R$'' for the regular module is exactly the defining absorption property of a \emph{left} ideal.
\wsol Let $N\subseteq R$. As a left $R$-module, $N\sgr\,_RR$ means: $N$ is an additive subgroup and $rn\in N$ for all $r\in R$, $n\in N$ (where $rn$ is ring multiplication). That is exactly the definition of a left ideal of $R$. Conversely every left ideal is an additive subgroup closed under left multiplication, hence a submodule. So $\{\text{submodules of }\,_RR\}=\{\text{left ideals of }R\}$. \textit{Check.} $R=\mathbb{Z}$: submodules of $\,_\mathbb{Z}\mathbb{Z}$ are the subgroups $n\mathbb{Z}$, which are exactly the ideals of $\mathbb{Z}$. $R=\mathbb{R}$ (a field): the only left ideals are $0$ and $\mathbb{R}$, matching ``a $1$-dim space has only trivial subspaces.'' Consistent. (For commutative $R$ this says: submodules of $R$ $=$ ideals.)
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Using the criterion: a torsion-style set and a non-example}
Let $R=\mathbb{Z}$, $M=\mathbb{Z}/(12)\mathbb{Z}$. Decide whether $N=\{\,\bar a\in\mathbb{Z}/(12)\mathbb{Z}:3\bar a=\bar 0\,\}$ is a submodule, and whether $S=\{\bar0,\bar1\}$ is.
\wrec Apply (i)+(ii) of the criterion directly. The \emph{rule \& why}: a single check $x+ry\in N$ settles it; for $S$ we look for one failure.
\wsol \emph{For $N$:} $3\bar a=\bar0$ in $\mathbb{Z}/(12)\mathbb{Z}$ means $12\mid 3a$, i.e.\ $4\mid a$, so $N=\{\bar0,\bar4,\bar8\}$. (i) $\bar0\in N$. (ii) For $x,y\in N$ and $r\in\mathbb{Z}$: $3(x+ry)=3x+r(3y)=\bar0+r\bar0=\bar0$, so $x+ry\in N$. Hence $N\sgr M$ (it is the $3$-torsion subgroup). \emph{For $S=\{\bar0,\bar1\}$:} take $x=\bar1\in S$, $r=2$, $y=\bar1$: $x+ry=\bar1+2\bar1=\bar3\notin S$. Criterion (ii) fails, so $S$ is \emph{not} a submodule. \textit{Check.} $N=\{\bar0,\bar4,\bar8\}$ is the unique subgroup of order $3$ in $\mathbb{Z}/(12)\mathbb{Z}$; closed under the $\mathbb{Z}$-action automatically, since every subgroup of an abelian group is a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule. Consistent. $S$ fails despite having order $2$: it is not even a subgroup, because $\bar1$ has additive order $12$, so $\bar1+\bar1=\bar2\notin S$ --- the criterion catches it.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Submodules of $F[x]$-modules are $T$-invariant subspaces}
Let $V$ be an $F[x]$-module with $x$ acting as $T$. Show $W\subseteq V$ is a submodule iff $W$ is an $F$-subspace with $TW\subseteq W$. Then for $V=\mathbb{R}^2$ with $T=\begin{psmallmatrix}2&0\\0&3\end{psmallmatrix}$, find all submodules.
\wrec Specialize the criterion to $R=F[x]$. The \emph{rule \& why}: the criterion's ``$x+ry\in N$ for all $r\in F[x]$'' reduces, since $F[x]$ is generated over $F$ by $x$, to: closed under $F$-scalars (subspace) and closed under $T$ (invariance).
\wsol ($\Rightarrow$) A submodule $W$ is closed under multiplication by constants $a\in F$ (so it is a subspace) and by $x$ (so $TW\subseteq W$). ($\Leftarrow$) If $W$ is a $T$-invariant subspace, then $W$ is closed under each $T^k$, hence under $p(T)=\sum a_i T^i$ for every $p\in F[x]$; with $W$ a subgroup this gives $w+p(x)w'\in W$, the criterion. \emph{For $T=\operatorname{diag}(2,3)$ on $\mathbb{R}^2$:} $T$-invariant subspaces are $0$, $\mathbb{R}^2$, and the eigenlines $\langle e_1\rangle$ (eigenvalue $2$), $\langle e_2\rangle$ (eigenvalue $3$). No other line is invariant: a line $\langle(a,b)\rangle$ with $a,b\neq0$ maps to $\langle(2a,3b)\rangle$, not a scalar multiple of $(a,b)$. So the submodules are $\{0,\ \langle e_1\rangle,\ \langle e_2\rangle,\ \mathbb{R}^2\}$. \textit{Check.} Four submodules match the four $T$-invariant subspaces of a diagonalizable operator with distinct eigenvalues on a $2$-dim space. Consistent.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Annihilators are submodules / ideals}
Let $M$ be a left $R$-module and $m\in M$. Show $\ann(m)=\{r\in R:rm=0\}$ is a left ideal of $R$, and $\ann(M)=\{r\in R:rM=0\}$ is a two-sided ideal.
\wrec View $\ann(m)$ as the kernel of $R\to M$, $r\mapsto rm$. The \emph{rule \& why}: this map is a left-$R$-module homomorphism from $\,_RR$, so its kernel is a submodule of $\,_RR$, i.e.\ a left ideal.
\wsol The map $\varphi:\,_RR\to M$, $\varphi(r)=rm$, satisfies $\varphi(r+s)=\varphi(r)+\varphi(s)$ and $\varphi(sr)=(sr)m=s(rm)=s\varphi(r)$, so it is a left-module homomorphism. Then $\ann(m)=\ker\varphi$ is a submodule of $\,_RR$, hence a left ideal (by the regular-module example). For $\ann(M)=\bigcap_{m\in M}\ann(m)$: it is a left ideal (intersection of left ideals); and it is closed under right multiplication, for if $rM=0$ and $s\in R$ then $(rs)M=r(sM)\subseteq rM=0$, so $rs\in\ann(M)$. Thus $\ann(M)$ is two-sided. \textit{Check.} $M=\mathbb{Z}/(12)\mathbb{Z}$ over $\mathbb{Z}$: $\ann(\bar4)=\{n:12\mid 4n\}=\{n:3\mid n\}=3\mathbb{Z}$, an ideal; $\ann(M)=12\mathbb{Z}$. Indeed $3\cdot\bar4=\bar0$ and $12\cdot\bar a=\bar0$ for all $\bar a$. Consistent.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Three quick $R$-algebras}
Verify each is an $R$-algebra: (a) $F[x]$ over $F$; (b) $M_n(R)$ over commutative $R$; (c) $R/I$ over $R$ (with $R$ commutative).
\wrec Exhibit the structure map $f:R\to A$ into the center. The \emph{rule \& why}: an $R$-algebra is exactly a ring with a central copy of $R$ inside it; produce that homomorphism.
\wsol (a) $f:F\hookrightarrow F[x]$ as constant polynomials; the image (scalars) is central since $F[x]$ is commutative, and $a\cdot p(x)=ap(x)$ is the module action. So $F[x]$ is an $F$-algebra. (b) $f:R\to M_n(R)$, $r\mapsto rI_n$; scalar matrices are central in $M_n(R)$, and $r\cdot(AB)=(rI)(AB)=((rI)A)B=(r\cdot A)B$. So $M_n(R)$ is an $R$-algebra (noncommutative for $n\ge2$). (c) $f:R\to R/I$ the quotient map; the image is all of $R/I$, central since $R/I$ is commutative, and $r\cdot(\bar a\bar b)=\overline{r a b}=(\,r\cdot\bar a)\bar b$. So $R/I$ is an $R$-algebra. \textit{Check.} In each, $f(1_R)=1_A$: $1\in F[x]$, $I_n\in M_n(R)$, $\bar1\in R/I$. Consistent.
\end{wex}

\begin{pitfall}
\textbf{An $R$-algebra is more than an $R$-module.} It is a ring \emph{and} a module with the two structures linked by $r(ab)=(ra)b=a(rb)$. Forgetting the centrality/compatibility condition lets in ``modules with a multiplication'' that are not algebras. Also: the structure map $f:R\to A$ need not be injective (e.g.\ $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ is a $\mathbb{Z}$-algebra with non-injective $f:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$), so an $R$-algebra need not literally \emph{contain} $R$.
\end{pitfall}

\begin{remark}
The two conditions combine into a single one: $\varphi$ is $R$-linear iff $\varphi(rm+sm')=r\,\varphi(m)+s\,\varphi(m')$ for all $r,s\in R$ and $m,m'\in M$. Taking $r=1$, $s=1$ gives additivity; taking $m'=0$ gives $\varphi(rm)=r\varphi(m)$. In particular every $\varphi\in\Hom_R(M,N)$ satisfies $\varphi(0)=0$ and $\varphi(-m)=-\varphi(m)$, since $\varphi$ is in particular a homomorphism of abelian groups.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Special cases]
The definition is a single template that specializes to familiar notions:
\begin{itemize}
\item $R=\mathbb{Z}$: a $\mathbb{Z}$-module is an abelian group, and $\mathbb{Z}$-linearity $\varphi(nm)=n\varphi(m)$ is automatic from additivity. So $\Hom_\mathbb{Z}(M,N)$ is exactly the set of \emph{abelian group homomorphisms}.
\item $R=\F$ a field: an $\F$-module is an $\F$-vector space, and $\Hom_\F(M,N)$ is the set of \emph{$\F$-linear transformations}.
\item $R=\F[x]$: an $\F[x]$-module is a pair $(V,T)$ where $V$ is an $\F$-vector space and $T$ is the $\F$-linear operator ``multiplication by $x$.'' An $\F[x]$-module homomorphism is an $\F$-linear map \emph{commuting with the operators} (see Worked Example below).
\end{itemize}
\end{remark}

\begin{method}{Checking $R$-linearity}
\textit{Trigger.} You are handed a map $\varphi\colon M\to N$ between $R$-modules and asked whether it is a homomorphism, or to find all homomorphisms.
\textit{Recipe.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Verify additivity: $\varphi(m+m')=\varphi(m)+\varphi(m')$.
\item Verify the scalar rule: $\varphi(rm)=r\varphi(m)$ for all $r\in R$. When $R$ is generated as a ring by a small set (e.g.\ $R=\mathbb{Z}$ generated by $1$, or $R=\F[x]$ generated by $\F$ and $x$), it suffices to check the rule on generators.
\item To \emph{find all} homs, note $\varphi$ is determined by its values on a generating set of $M$, subject to the relations among those generators.
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

\begin{proof}
We use the Submodule Criterion: a nonempty subset $S$ with $rx+y\in S$ for all $r\in R$ and $x,y\in S$ is a submodule. Both sets contain $0$. For the kernel: if $m,m'\in\ker\varphi$ and $r\in R$, then $\varphi(rm+m')=r\varphi(m)+\varphi(m')=r\cdot 0+0=0$, so $rm+m'\in\ker\varphi$. For the image: if $\varphi(m),\varphi(m')\in\im\varphi$ and $r\in R$, then $r\varphi(m)+\varphi(m')=\varphi(rm+m')\in\im\varphi$. Finally, if $\ker\varphi=0$ and $\varphi(m)=\varphi(m')$, then $\varphi(m-m')=0$ so $m=m'$; conversely injectivity forces $\ker\varphi=0$ since $\varphi(0)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
$M/N$ is already an abelian group (cosets of the subgroup $N$). We must check the action is \emph{well defined}: if $m+N=m'+N$ then $m-m'\in N$, and since $N$ is a submodule $r(m-m')\in N$, i.e.\ $rm+N=rm'+N$. The module axioms for $M/N$ follow coset-wise from those of $M$: e.g.\ $(r+s)(m+N)=(r+s)m+N=(rm+sm)+N=r(m+N)+s(m+N)$, and $1\cdot(m+N)=m+N$. The projection $\pi$ is additive and $\pi(rm)=rm+N=r(m+N)=r\pi(m)$, hence $R$-linear; it is surjective by construction, and $\pi(m)=0+N$ iff $m\in N$, so $\ker\pi=N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Well defined:} if $m+N=m'+N$ then $m-m'\in N\subseteq\ker\varphi$, so $\varphi(m)=\varphi(m')$; thus $\overline\varphi(m+N):=\varphi(m)$ is unambiguous. \emph{$R$-linear:} $\overline\varphi(r(m+N)+(m'+N))=\overline\varphi(rm+m'+N)=\varphi(rm+m')=r\varphi(m)+\varphi(m')=r\overline\varphi(m+N)+\overline\varphi(m'+N)$. \emph{Commutes:} $\overline\varphi(\pi(m))=\overline\varphi(m+N)=\varphi(m)$. \emph{Unique:} any $\psi$ with $\psi\circ\pi=\varphi$ satisfies $\psi(m+N)=\psi(\pi(m))=\varphi(m)$, forcing $\psi=\overline\varphi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{method}{Using the universal property to define maps out of $M/N$}
\textit{Trigger.} You want to define a map $\overline\varphi\colon M/N\to L$ on cosets, or prove two such maps agree.
\textit{Recipe.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Define $\varphi\colon M\to L$ on representatives.
\item Check $N\subseteq\ker\varphi$ (this is exactly the ``well-defined'' check).
\item Conclude $\overline\varphi$ exists, is unique, and $\overline\varphi(m+N)=\varphi(m)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

\begin{proof}
Let $K=\ker\varphi$, a submodule by Proposition~\ref{prop:kerim@c10}. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:up-quot@c10} with $L=N$ (using $K\subseteq\ker\varphi$ trivially): we get $\overline\varphi\colon M/K\to N$, $\overline\varphi(m+K)=\varphi(m)$, an $R$-homomorphism. Its image is $\im\varphi$, so restricting the codomain gives a surjection $M/K\to\im\varphi$. It is injective: $\overline\varphi(m+K)=0$ means $\varphi(m)=0$, i.e.\ $m\in K$, i.e.\ $m+K=0$ in $M/K$. A bijective $R$-homomorphism is an isomorphism (its set-theoretic inverse is automatically $R$-linear). The factorization $\varphi=\iota\circ\overline\varphi\circ\pi$ is read off the diagram.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Second (Diamond) Isomorphism Theorem]\label{thm:second@c10}
Let $A$ and $B$ be submodules of $M$. Then $A+B$ and $A\cap B$ are submodules, and
\[
(A+B)/B \cong A/(A\cap B).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$A+B=\{a+b:a\in A,b\in B\}$ and $A\cap B$ are submodules by the submodule criterion. Define $\varphi\colon A\to (A+B)/B$ by $\varphi(a)=a+B$; this is the composite of the inclusion $A\hookrightarrow A+B$ with the projection $A+B\to(A+B)/B$, hence $R$-linear. It is surjective: any coset $(a+b)+B=a+B=\varphi(a)$. Its kernel is $\{a\in A:a\in B\}=A\cap B$. By the First Isomorphism Theorem, $A/(A\cap B)\cong (A+B)/B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Third Isomorphism Theorem]\label{thm:third@c10}
Let $A\sgr B\sgr M$ be submodules. Then $B/A\sgr M/A$, and
\[
(M/A)/(B/A)\cong M/B.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define $\varphi\colon M/A\to M/B$ by $\varphi(m+A)=m+B$. This is well defined and $R$-linear since $A\subseteq B$: it is the map induced by $\pi_B\colon M\to M/B$ through Theorem~\ref{thm:up-quot@c10} (as $A\subseteq B=\ker\pi_B$). It is surjective, with kernel $\{m+A:m\in B\}=B/A$. The First Isomorphism Theorem gives $(M/A)/(B/A)\cong M/B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof (sketch)]
The projection $\pi\colon M\to M/N$ carries submodules to submodules and pulls submodules back to submodules containing $N=\ker\pi$. The maps $A\mapsto\pi(A)=A/N$ and $\overline A\mapsto\pi^{-1}(\overline A)$ are mutually inverse on the indicated sets (because every $A$ in the left family contains $N$, so $\pi^{-1}(A/N)=A$). Both maps clearly respect inclusion; surjectivity of $\pi$ and the modular law give preservation of $+$ and $\cap$.
\end{proof}

\begin{pitfall}
The Second Isomorphism Theorem requires $B$ to be a submodule so that $A+B$ makes sense as a submodule. The quotient in the Third Theorem, $(M/A)/(B/A)$, is a ``quotient of a quotient'': do not confuse $B/A$ (a submodule of $M/A$) with $B$ itself. And in the Fourth Theorem, only the submodules \emph{containing $N$} correspond to submodules of $M/N$ --- submodules of $M$ not containing $N$ have no counterpart.
\end{pitfall}

\begin{wex}{Group homs $\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ and their count}
Determine all $\mathbb{Z}$-module homomorphisms $\varphi\colon\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ and count them.
\wrec A $\mathbb{Z}$-module hom is an abelian-group hom; $\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z}=\langle \bar 1\rangle$ is cyclic, so $\varphi$ is determined by $\varphi(\bar 1)$, subject to the relation $m\bar 1=0$. \emph{Recognition:} ``maps out of a cyclic module'' $=$ choose the image of the generator, respecting its order.
\wsol Write $\varphi(\bar 1)=\bar a\in\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$. Then $\varphi(\bar k)=k\bar a$, so $\varphi$ is determined by $\bar a$. The single relation $m\bar 1=\bar 0$ in $\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z}$ forces $\varphi(m\bar 1)=m\bar a=\bar 0$ in $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$, i.e.\ $n\mid ma$. Conversely, any $\bar a$ with $n\mid ma$ yields a well-defined hom (the assignment respects the only relation). The condition $n\mid ma$ says $a$ is a multiple of $n/\gcd(m,n)$ modulo $n$. With $d=\gcd(m,n)$, the elements $\bar a\in\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ with $\tfrac{n}{d}\mid a$ are exactly $0,\ \tfrac{n}{d},\ 2\tfrac{n}{d},\dots,(d-1)\tfrac{n}{d}$, and there are $d$ of them. \textit{Check.} Count $=\gcd(m,n)$. For $m=4,n=6$: $d=2$, valid $\bar a\in\{\bar 0,\bar 3\}$, indeed $6\mid 4\cdot 0$ and $6\mid 4\cdot 3=12$; exactly $2$ homs. Matches $|\Hom_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(4)\mathbb{Z},\mathbb{Z}/(6)\mathbb{Z})|=2$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{$M/\ker\cong\im$ for a concrete map}
Let $\varphi\colon\mathbb{Z}^2\to\mathbb{Z}$ be the $\mathbb{Z}$-linear map $\varphi(a,b)=2a-4b$. Identify $\ker\varphi$, $\im\varphi$, and verify the First Isomorphism Theorem.
\wrec $\varphi$ is $\mathbb{Z}$-linear (additive, and $\mathbb{Z}$-scalars come from additivity). \emph{Recognition:} ``compute a quotient by realizing it as the kernel of an explicit map'' $=$ apply $M/\ker\cong\im$.
\wsol \emph{Image:} $\im\varphi=\{2a-4b:a,b\in\mathbb{Z}\}=2\mathbb{Z}$ since $\gcd(2,4)=2$ and $2a-4b$ ranges over all multiples of $2$ (take $a=1,b=0$ to get $2$). \emph{Kernel:} $2a-4b=0\iff a=2b$, so $\ker\varphi=\{(2b,b):b\in\mathbb{Z}\}=\mathbb{Z}\cdot(2,1)$, a rank-$1$ free submodule. \emph{First Theorem:} $\mathbb{Z}^2/\mathbb{Z}(2,1)\cong 2\mathbb{Z}\cong\mathbb{Z}$. \emph{Check via a basis:} $\{(2,1),(1,0)\}$ is a $\mathbb{Z}$-basis of $\mathbb{Z}^2$ (the matrix with these as columns has determinant $2\cdot 0-1\cdot 1=-1$, a unit), so modding by $\mathbb{Z}(2,1)$ leaves the free rank-$1$ quotient generated by the image of $(1,0)$, i.e.\ $\mathbb{Z}$. Consistent with $2\mathbb{Z}\cong\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{$\Hom_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z},\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/(\gcd(m,n))\mathbb{Z}$}
Prove $\Hom_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z},\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/(d)\mathbb{Z}$ where $d=\gcd(m,n)$, as abelian groups.
\wrec Maps out of the cyclic module $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ are determined by the image of $\bar 1$; the set of valid images is itself a subgroup of $\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z}$. \emph{Recognition:} ``$\Hom$ out of a cyclic group'' $=$ annihilator subgroup of the codomain.
\wsol By the previous analysis, $\varphi\mapsto\varphi(\bar 1)$ is a map $\Theta\colon\Hom_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z},\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z})\to\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z}$, and it is an injective group homomorphism: $\Theta(\varphi+\psi)=(\varphi+\psi)(\bar 1)=\varphi(\bar 1)+\psi(\bar 1)$, and $\varphi$ is recovered from $\varphi(\bar 1)$. Its image is $\{\bar a\in\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z}: m\mid na\}=\{\bar a: \tfrac{m}{d}\mid a\}=\tfrac{m}{d}\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z}=\langle \overline{m/d}\rangle$. This is the unique subgroup of $\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z}$ of order $d$, which is cyclic, hence $\cong\mathbb{Z}/(d)\mathbb{Z}$. Therefore $\Hom_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z},\mathbb{Z}/(m)\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/(d)\mathbb{Z}$. \textit{Check.} $n=4,m=6$ gives $d=2$ and $\Hom\cong\mathbb{Z}/(2)\mathbb{Z}$ (the two homs of the first example, $\bar 0$ and $\bar 3$, with $\bar 3=\overline{6/2}$ generating the order-$2$ subgroup inside $\mathbb{Z}/(6)\mathbb{Z}$). $n=m$ gives $d=n$ and $\Hom_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z},\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$, matching $\End_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{$\Hom_R(R,M)\cong M$}
For any $R$-module $M$, show evaluation at $1$ gives an isomorphism $\Hom_R(R,M)\cong M$ (of $R$-modules when $R$ is commutative).
\wrec $R$ is the free module on one generator $1$; a hom out of it is determined by $\varphi(1)$, with no relations. \emph{Recognition:} ``$\Hom$ out of the free rank-$1$ module'' $=$ evaluation at the basis element.
\wsol Define $\Phi\colon\Hom_R(R,M)\to M$ by $\Phi(\varphi)=\varphi(1)$. \emph{Homomorphism of abelian groups:} $\Phi(\varphi+\psi)=(\varphi+\psi)(1)=\varphi(1)+\psi(1)$. \emph{Injective:} if $\varphi(1)=0$ then $\varphi(r)=\varphi(r\cdot 1)=r\varphi(1)=0$ for all $r$, so $\varphi=0$. \emph{Surjective:} given $m\in M$, define $\varphi_m(r)=rm$; this is $R$-linear since $\varphi_m(r r'+r'')=(rr'+r'')m=r(r'm)+r''m=r\varphi_m(r')+\varphi_m(r'')$, and $\Phi(\varphi_m)=\varphi_m(1)=m$. Hence $\Phi$ is a bijective homomorphism. When $R$ is commutative, $\Hom_R(R,M)$ is an $R$-module (see below) and $\Phi(s\varphi)=(s\varphi)(1)=s\,\varphi(1)=s\Phi(\varphi)$, so $\Phi$ is $R$-linear. \textit{Check.} For $R=M=\mathbb{Z}$, $\Hom_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z},\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}$ (each hom is ``multiply by $\varphi(1)$''), as expected.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Quotient by a relation: $F[x]/(x^2)$ as a module map}
Let $R=F[x]$ and $M=F[x]$. Consider $\pi\colon F[x]\to F[x]/(x^2)$, the projection. Use the First Theorem to identify the quotient, and find $\im$ and $\ker$ of multiplication-by-$x$ on $F[x]/(x^2)$.
\wrec Projection onto a quotient ring viewed as a module: $\ker=(x^2)$. Multiplication by $x$ is an $F[x]$-endomorphism. \emph{Recognition:} ``identify a quotient module'' $=$ exhibit it as $\im$ of a surjection.
\wsol The projection $\pi\colon F[x]\to F[x]/(x^2)$ is surjective $F[x]$-linear with $\ker\pi=(x^2)$, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:first@c10}, $F[x]/(x^2)\cong\im\pi=F[x]/(x^2)$ (tautologically), and as an $F$-vector space it has basis $\{\bar 1,\bar x\}$. Now let $\mu_x\colon F[x]/(x^2)\to F[x]/(x^2)$, $\mu_x(\bar f)=x\bar f$. Then $\mu_x(\bar 1)=\bar x$ and $\mu_x(\bar x)=\overline{x^2}=\bar 0$. So $\im\mu_x=F\bar x=(x)/(x^2)$ and $\ker\mu_x=F\bar x=(x)/(x^2)$ as well. By the First Theorem applied to $\mu_x$: $\big(F[x]/(x^2)\big)/\ker\mu_x\cong\im\mu_x$, i.e.\ $\big(F[x]/(x^2)\big)\big/\big((x)/(x^2)\big)\cong (x)/(x^2)$; both sides are $1$-dimensional over $F$, consistent. \textit{Check.} $\dim_F\ker\mu_x+\dim_F\im\mu_x=1+1=2=\dim_F F[x]/(x^2)$ (rank--nullity).
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Second and Third Theorems in $\mathbb{Z}$}
Inside $M=\mathbb{Z}$, take $A=4\mathbb{Z}$ and $B=6\mathbb{Z}$. Verify the Second Isomorphism Theorem; then with $A=12\mathbb{Z}\sgr B=4\mathbb{Z}\sgr\mathbb{Z}$, verify the Third.
\wrec Submodules of $\mathbb{Z}$ are the ideals $n\mathbb{Z}$; sums and intersections are computed by $\gcd$ and $\operatorname{lcm}$. \emph{Recognition:} ``check an isomorphism theorem on a concrete lattice.''
\wsol \emph{Second:} $A+B=4\mathbb{Z}+6\mathbb{Z}=\gcd(4,6)\mathbb{Z}=2\mathbb{Z}$ and $A\cap B=4\mathbb{Z}\cap 6\mathbb{Z}=\operatorname{lcm}(4,6)\mathbb{Z}=12\mathbb{Z}$. The theorem predicts $(A+B)/B\cong A/(A\cap B)$, i.e.\ $2\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}\cong 4\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}$. Both are cyclic of order $3$: $2\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}=\{\bar 0,\bar 2,\bar 4\}\cong\mathbb{Z}/(3)\mathbb{Z}$ and $4\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}=\{\bar 0,\bar 4,\bar 8\}\cong\mathbb{Z}/(3)\mathbb{Z}$. Confirmed. \emph{Third:} with $12\mathbb{Z}\sgr 4\mathbb{Z}\sgr\mathbb{Z}$, the theorem gives $(\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z})/(4\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z}$. Now $\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}$ has order $12$, $4\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}$ has order $3$, so the quotient has order $12/3=4$, matching $|\mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z}|=4$. Confirmed.
\end{wex}

\begin{proposition}[$\Hom_R(M,N)$ is an abelian group]\label{prop:homgroup@c10}
Let $M,N$ be $R$-modules. Define $(\varphi+\psi)(m)=\varphi(m)+\psi(m)$. Then $\Hom_R(M,N)$ is an abelian group with this addition, identity the zero map, and inverse $(-\varphi)(m)=-\varphi(m)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
First, $\varphi+\psi$ is $R$-linear: $(\varphi+\psi)(rm+m')=\varphi(rm+m')+\psi(rm+m')=r\varphi(m)+\varphi(m')+r\psi(m)+\psi(m')=r(\varphi+\psi)(m)+(\varphi+\psi)(m')$, where commutativity of $N$ was used to regroup. The group axioms (associativity, commutativity, identity, inverses) are inherited pointwise from the abelian group $N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[$\Hom_R(M,N)$ is an $R$-module when $R$ is commutative]\label{prop:hommod@c10}
Suppose $R$ is commutative. For $r\in R$ and $\varphi\in\Hom_R(M,N)$ define $(r\varphi)(m)=r\big(\varphi(m)\big)$. Then $r\varphi\in\Hom_R(M,N)$ and this makes $\Hom_R(M,N)$ an $R$-module.
\end{proposition}

\begin{pitfall}
If $R$ is \emph{not} commutative, $(r\varphi)(m)=r\varphi(m)$ generally fails to be $R$-linear: the computation above breaks at $rs=sr$. So in general $\Hom_R(M,N)$ is only an abelian group, not an $R$-module. (It does carry actions by the center of $R$ and by $\End_R$, but not by $R$ itself in general.)
\end{pitfall}

\begin{theorem}[$\End_R(M)$ is a ring]\label{thm:endring@c10}
$\End_R(M)=\Hom_R(M,M)$ is a ring with $1$ under pointwise addition and composition as multiplication; the identity is $\id_M$. When $R$ is commutative, $\End_R(M)$ is an $R$-algebra, and $M$ is naturally a left $\End_R(M)$-module via $\varphi\cdot m=\varphi(m)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:homgroup@c10}, $(\End_R(M),+)$ is an abelian group. Composition of $R$-linear maps is $R$-linear and associative, with two-sided identity $\id_M$. Distributivity: $(\varphi\circ(\psi+\chi))(m)=\varphi(\psi(m)+\chi(m))=\varphi\psi(m)+\varphi\chi(m)$ (using additivity of $\varphi$), and $((\psi+\chi)\circ\varphi)(m)=\psi\varphi(m)+\chi\varphi(m)$. Hence $\End_R(M)$ is a ring with $1=\id_M$. When $R$ is commutative, $r\mapsto r\cdot\id_M$ maps $R$ into the center of $\End_R(M)$ (since $r\,\id_M$ commutes with every $R$-linear $\varphi$: $\varphi(rm)=r\varphi(m)$), making $\End_R(M)$ an $R$-algebra. The action $\varphi\cdot m=\varphi(m)$ satisfies $(\varphi\psi)\cdot m=\varphi(\psi(m))=\varphi\cdot(\psi\cdot m)$ and $\id_M\cdot m=m$, so $M$ is a left $\End_R(M)$-module.
\end{proof}

\begin{method}{Recognizing $\End_R(M)$}
\textit{Trigger.} Asked to compute or describe $\End_R(M)$ for a specific module.
\textit{Recipe.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $R$ is commutative and $M=R^n$ is free, $\End_R(R^n)\cong M_n(R)$ (matrices acting on column vectors). For noncommutative $R$, left multiplication by a matrix is not $R$-linear: the $R$-linear maps of the left module $R^n$ (row vectors) are $v\mapsto vB$, and $\End_R(R^n)\cong M_n(R^{\mathrm{op}})$, where the opposite ring $R^{\mathrm{op}}$ is $R$ with the product $a*b=ba$ (so $\End_R(R)\cong R^{\mathrm{op}}$).
\item If $M$ is an $F[x]$-module $(V,T)$, $\End_{F[x]}(V)$ is the \emph{commutant} of $T$: all $F$-linear maps commuting with $T$.
\item If $M=\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ over $\mathbb{Z}$, $\End_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ (each endomorphism is multiplication by a residue).
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

\begin{wex}{$\End_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ as a ring}
Show $\End_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ as rings.
\wrec Endomorphisms of the cyclic group $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ are determined by the image of $\bar 1$, which can be any element. \emph{Recognition:} ``endomorphism ring of a cyclic module.''
\wsol By the analysis of homs out of cyclic modules with $m=n$ (so $d=\gcd(n,n)=n$), \emph{every} $\bar a\in\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}$ arises as $\varphi(\bar 1)$, giving $\varphi_a(\bar k)=k\bar a=\overline{ka}$. The map $\bar a\mapsto\varphi_a$ is a bijection $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}\to\End_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})$. It respects addition: $\varphi_a+\varphi_b=\varphi_{a+b}$. It respects multiplication via composition: $(\varphi_a\circ\varphi_b)(\bar 1)=\varphi_a(\bar b)=\overline{ab}=\varphi_{ab}(\bar 1)$, so $\varphi_a\circ\varphi_b=\varphi_{ab}$. And $\varphi_1=\id$. Hence $\bar a\mapsto\varphi_a$ is a ring isomorphism $\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z}\cong\End_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})$. \textit{Check.} Units of $\End_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})$ $=$ automorphisms $\operatorname{Aut}(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})\cong(\mathbb{Z}/(n)\mathbb{Z})^\times$, matching the units on the ring side. For $n=6$: $|(\mathbb{Z}/(6)\mathbb{Z})^\times|=\phi(6)=2$, and indeed $\operatorname{Aut}(\mathbb{Z}/(6)\mathbb{Z})$ has order $2$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{$\Hom$ is additive: $\Hom_R(M,N_1\oplus N_2)\cong\Hom_R(M,N_1)\oplus\Hom_R(M,N_2)$}
Establish the natural isomorphism of abelian groups (of $R$-modules if $R$ commutative).
\wrec A map into a direct sum is the same as a pair of maps into the factors, via the projections. \emph{Recognition:} ``$\Hom$ into a direct sum splits.''
\wsol Let $p_i\colon N_1\oplus N_2\to N_i$ be the projections. Define $\Theta(\varphi)=(p_1\varphi,p_2\varphi)$. This is an additive map $\Hom_R(M,N_1\oplus N_2)\to\Hom_R(M,N_1)\oplus\Hom_R(M,N_2)$. \emph{Inverse:} given $(\alpha,\beta)$, set $\Psi(\alpha,\beta)(m)=(\alpha(m),\beta(m))$, which is $R$-linear into $N_1\oplus N_2$. Then $\Theta\Psi(\alpha,\beta)=(\alpha,\beta)$ and $\Psi\Theta(\varphi)=\varphi$ since $\varphi(m)=(p_1\varphi(m),p_2\varphi(m))$. Hence $\Theta$ is an isomorphism. \textit{Check.} $\Hom_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z},\mathbb{Z}/(2)\mathbb{Z}\oplus\mathbb{Z}/(3)\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/(2)\mathbb{Z}\oplus\mathbb{Z}/(3)\mathbb{Z}\cong\mathbb{Z}/(6)\mathbb{Z}$, matching $\Hom_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z},\mathbb{Z}/(6)\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}/(6)\mathbb{Z}$ and the CRT $\mathbb{Z}/(2)\mathbb{Z}\oplus\mathbb{Z}/(3)\mathbb{Z}\cong\mathbb{Z}/(6)\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{A non-example, then repaired to a linear map}
Decide whether $\varphi\colon\mathbb{R}^2\to\mathbb{R}$, $\varphi(x,y)=x^2+y$, is an $\mathbb{R}$-module homomorphism; then repair it.
\wrec Test the two axioms; squaring destroys additivity/scaling. \emph{Recognition:} ``is this a module map?'' $=$ check linearity, do not assume.
\wsol \emph{Scalar test:} $\varphi(r(x,y))=\varphi(rx,ry)=(rx)^2+ry=r^2x^2+ry$, whereas $r\varphi(x,y)=r(x^2+y)=rx^2+ry$. These differ (e.g.\ $r=2,(x,y)=(1,0)$: $\varphi(2,0)=4$ but $2\varphi(1,0)=2$). So $\varphi$ is \emph{not} $\mathbb{R}$-linear. \emph{Repair:} drop the square: $\psi(x,y)=ax+by$ for fixed $a,b\in\mathbb{R}$ is $\mathbb{R}$-linear, and these are \emph{all} of $\Hom_\mathbb{R}(\mathbb{R}^2,\mathbb{R})$ (the dual space), since $\psi$ is determined by $\psi(e_1)=a$, $\psi(e_2)=b$. \textit{Check.} $\Hom_\mathbb{R}(\mathbb{R}^2,\mathbb{R})\cong\mathbb{R}^2$ as $\mathbb{R}$-modules, consistent with $\Hom_R(R^2,M)\cong M^2$ at $M=\mathbb{R}$.
\end{wex}

\begin{definition}[Submodule generated by a subset]
Let $M$ be an $R$-module and $A\subseteq M$ a subset.
\begin{itemize}
\item $RA \;=\; \{\, r_1a_1+\cdots+r_na_n \;:\; n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0},\ r_i\in R,\ a_i\in A \,\}$ is the set of all (finite) $R$-linear combinations of elements of $A$ (with the empty sum equal to $0$). This is the \emph{submodule generated by $A$}; it is the smallest submodule of $M$ containing $A$, and equals the intersection of all submodules containing $A$.
\item If $RA=M$ we say $A$ \emph{generates} $M$, or $A$ is a \emph{set of generators}.
\item $M$ is \emph{finitely generated} if $M=RA$ for some finite set $A$.
\item $M$ is \emph{cyclic} if $M=Rm=\{rm:r\in R\}$ for a single $m\in M$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{proof}
For $r,s\in R$ and the module axioms, $\pi(r+s)=(r+s)m=rm+sm=\pi(r)+\pi(s)$ and $\pi(sr)=(sr)m=s(rm)=s\,\pi(r)$, so $\pi$ is an $R$-module map (here $R$ is viewed as a left module over itself). Its image is $\{rm:r\in R\}=Rm$. Its kernel is $\{r:rm=0\}=\ann(m)$, a submodule of the left $R$-module $R$, i.e.\ a left ideal. The First Isomorphism Theorem for modules gives $R/\ker\pi\cong\im\pi$, that is $R/\ann(m)\cong Rm$. If $M=Rm$ then $M\cong R/\ann(m)$; conversely if $M\cong R/I$ then the image of $1$ generates, so $M$ is cyclic.
\end{proof}

\begin{method}{Identifying and analyzing $RA$, finitely generated, cyclic}
\textit{Trigger.} You are asked what a subset \emph{generates}, whether a module is finitely generated/cyclic, or to identify a cyclic module up to isomorphism.
\begin{enumerate}
\item To compute $RA$: take \emph{all} finite $R$-linear combinations $\sum r_ia_i$. Verify it is closed under $+$ and under $R$-scaling (it is, by construction).
\item ``Cyclic'' means \emph{one} generator $m$: then $M=Rm\cong R/\ann(m)$. Compute $\ann(m)=\{r:rm=0\}$ as a left ideal; read off the quotient.
\item ``Finitely generated'' means \emph{some finite} $A$ works; to disprove, show no finite set generates (e.g.\ a divisibility/denominator obstruction).
\item Over a field $R=F$: modules are vector spaces; ``finitely generated'' $=$ ``finite-dimensional'', ``cyclic'' $=$ ``dimension $\le 1$''.
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

\begin{wex}{A cyclic module over a polynomial ring}
Let $R=\F[x]$ where $\F$ is a field, and let $M=R$, $m=x^2-1\in R$. Describe $Rm$ and its annihilator. Then redo with $M=R/(x^2-1)$ and $m=\overline{x}$.
\wrec $Rm\cong R/\ann(m)$. In an integral domain $\ann$ of a nonzero element is $0$; in a quotient ring use the quotient law.
\wsol (a) $R=\F[x]$ is a domain, so $\ann_R(x^2-1)=\{f: f\cdot(x^2-1)=0\}=0$. Hence $R(x^2-1)\cong R/0\cong R$, the principal ideal $(x^2-1)$, a free rank-$1$ submodule. (b) In $M=R/(x^2-1)$, $\ann_M(\overline x)=\{f:\ f\,\overline x=\overline 0\}=\{f:\ (x^2-1)\mid xf\}$. Since $\gcd(x,x^2-1)=1$, $(x^2-1)\mid xf$ iff $(x^2-1)\mid f$, so $\ann(\overline x)=(x^2-1)$ and $R\overline x\cong R/(x^2-1)=M$; thus $\overline x$ generates $M$ and $M$ is cyclic. \textit{Check.} $\dim_\F M=2$ with basis $\overline1,\overline x$; and $\overline x\cdot\overline x=\overline{x^2}=\overline1$, so $R\overline x\supseteq\{\overline1,\overline x\}$ spans, confirming $M=R\overline x$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Cyclic over a noncommutative ring: $\ann$ is only a left ideal}
Let $R=M_2(\F)$ (a noncommutative ring) acting on the column space $M=\F^2$. Take $m=\binom{1}{0}$. Show $M=Rm$ and compute $\ann(m)$.
\wrec $Rm\cong R/\ann(m)$ where $\ann(m)$ is a \emph{left} ideal (not two-sided when $R$ is noncommutative).
\wsol For $A=\begin{psmallmatrix}a&b\\ c&d\end{psmallmatrix}$, $Am=\binom{a}{c}$ ranges over all of $\F^2$ as $a,c$ vary, so $Rm=\F^2=M$ (cyclic). And $\ann(m)=\{A:Am=0\}=\{A:a=c=0\}=\left\{\begin{psmallmatrix}0&b\\0&d\end{psmallmatrix}\right\}$, the matrices with zero first column. \textit{Check.} This set is closed under left multiplication: $B\begin{psmallmatrix}0&b\\0&d\end{psmallmatrix}$ again has zero first column, confirming it is a left ideal. It is \emph{not} a right ideal (multiplying on the right by a permutation can move the nonzero column), as expected.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Generators need not be unique or minimal}
In $M=\mathbb{Z}/(6)\mathbb{Z}$ (a $\mathbb{Z}$-module), show $A=\{\overline2,\overline3\}$ generates $M$ but $M$ is also cyclic.
\wrec $RA$ is all $\mathbb{Z}$-combinations; cyclic means a single generator suffices.
\wsol $\mathbb{Z}\{\overline2,\overline3\}\ni \overline3-\overline2=\overline1$, and $\overline1$ generates $\mathbb{Z}/(6)\mathbb{Z}$, so $A$ generates $M$. But also $M=\mathbb{Z}\overline1$ is cyclic with the single generator $\overline1$. Thus $\{\overline2,\overline3\}$ is a (non-minimal) generating set. \textit{Check.} $\ann(\overline1)=6\mathbb{Z}$ so $\mathbb{Z}\overline1\cong\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}=M$; the two-element set is genuinely redundant.
\end{wex}

