\wsol $\Phi:R\to M$, $f\mapsto f(T)e_1$. Compute the orbit: $1\cdot e_1=e_1$, $x\cdot e_1=Te_1=e_2$, $x^2\cdot e_1=T^2e_1=-e_1$. So $\{e_1,xe_1\}=\{e_1,e_2\}$ spans $M$, hence $\Phi$ is onto and $Rv=M$. The relation $x^2 e_1=-e_1$ means $(x^2+1)e_1=0$, so $x^2+1\in\ann(v)$. Since $\F[x]$ is a PID and $\dim_\F M=2$ forces $\deg$ of the generator $=2$, $\ann(v)=(x^2+1)$. Thus $Rv\cong\F[x]/(x^2+1)$. \textit{Check.} $\dim_\F \F[x]/(x^2+1)=2=\dim_\F M$, and $T^2+I=0$ confirms $x^2+1$ annihilates. Consistent.
\end{wex}

\begin{remark}
The universal property characterizes $R^{(A)}$ \emph{up to unique isomorphism}: any module $F$ with a map $\iota:A\to F$ having the same universal extension property is canonically isomorphic to $R^{(A)}$. This is the module-theoretic instance of ``free objects are unique,'' and is the template for the analogous characterizations of the tensor product and of projective/injective modules later in the chapter.
\end{remark}

