\begin{pitfall}
Simple tensors can vanish and do not exhaust the group. Two traps:
\begin{itemize}
\item $m\otimes n=0$ does not imply $m=0$ or $n=0$. E.g. $\bar2\otimes\bar5=0$ in $Z/(6)Z\otimes_ZZ/(10)Z$, and every simple tensor is $0$ in $Q\otimes_ZZ/(n)Z$ or in $Z/(2)Z\otimes_ZZ/(3)Z$.
\item Not every element is a simple tensor; a general element is a finite sum $\sum_i m_i\otimes n_i$. In $R^2\otimes_RR^2$ the element $e_1\otimes e_1+e_2\otimes e_2$ is not equal to any single $u\otimes v$. Never define a map on $\otimes$ by a formula on simple tensors without checking balancedness --- consistency across the relations is exactly what the UP guarantees and a naive formula may not.
\end{itemize}
\end{pitfall}

