Section: Vector Spaces > Definitions and Basic Theory > Vector spaces and subspaces
Type: definition
Title: Vector space over $F$
Content:
\begin{definition}[Vector space over $F$]
A vector space over $F$ (or $F$-vector space) is an abelian group $(V,+)$ together with a scalar action $F\times V\to V$, $(\alpha,v)\mapsto \alpha v$, such that for all $\alpha,\beta\in F$ and all $v,w\in V$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(V1)] $(\alpha+\beta)v=\alpha v+\beta v$;
\item[(V2)] $(\alpha\beta)v=\alpha(\beta v)$;
\item[(V3)] $\alpha(v+w)=\alpha v+\alpha w$;
\item[(V4)] $1\,v=v$.
\end{enumerate}
Elements of $V$ are called vectors. A map $T\colon V\to W$ of $F$-vector spaces is $F$-linear (a linear transformation) if $T(\alpha v+\beta w)=\alpha T(v)+\beta T(w)$ for all $\alpha,\beta\in F$, $v,w\in V$. We write $\Hom_F(V,W)$ for the set of all such maps, $\End_F(V)=\Hom_F(V,V)$, and $\dual{V}=\Hom_F(V,F)$ for the dual space.
\end{definition}

