\begin{method}{Computing a dual basis}
Trigger. A basis $\{v_i\}$ is given and you need the functionals $v_i^{*}$ explicitly, or the coordinates of a given $f$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Form the matrix $P=[\,v_1\ \cdots\ v_n\,]$ whose columns are the $v_i$ in standard coordinates.
\item The rows of $P^{-1}$ are the dual basis functionals: $v_i^{*}(x)=($i$th row of P^{-1})\,x$. (Reason: $v_i^{*}(v_j)=(P^{-1}P)_{ij}=\delta_{ij}$.)
\item To get the coordinates of a functional $f$ in $\{v_i^{*}\}$, just evaluate: $f=\sum_i f(v_i)\,v_i^{*}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{method}

