Course: abstract algebra notes
Section: Vector Spaces > Dual Vector Spaces > Summary of the duality dictionary
Type: prose
Content:
For finite-dimensional $V$ over $F$ the constructions of this section assemble into one coherent dictionary, all anchored by the natural isomorphism $V\cong V^{**}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\dim\dual{V}=\dim V$, with dual basis $v_i^{*}(v_j)=\delta_{ij}$ (Theorem~ [REF:thm:dualbasis@c11] );
\item $V\xrightarrow{\ \Phi\ }V^{**}$, $\Phi(v)(f)=f(v)$, a basis-free isomorphism (Theorem~ [REF:thm:doubledual@c11] );
\item $\dim\operatorname{ann}(W)=\dim V-\dim W$ and $\operatorname{ann}(\operatorname{ann}(W))=W$ (Proposition~ [REF:prop:ann@c11] );
\item $\dual{T}(f)=f\circ T$ has matrix $A\trans$, with $\ker\dual{T}=\operatorname{ann}(\im T)$ and $\rank\dual{T}=\rank T$ (Theorem~ [REF:thm:transpose@c11] ).
\end{itemize}
The contravariant functor $\Hom_F(-,F)$ ties these together: it sends $V\rightsquigarrow\dual{V}$, $T\rightsquigarrow\dual{T}$, subspaces to their annihilators, and --- applied twice --- returns $V$ to itself.

