Title: Inverse via the adjugate over $Q$
Content:
\begin{wex}{Inverse via the adjugate over $Q$}
Find $A^{-1}$ for $A = \begin{psmallmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{psmallmatrix}$.
\wrec Theorem~ [REF:thm:laplace@c11] : $A^{-1} = (\det A)^{-1}\operatorname{adj} A$.
\wsol $\det A = -2$. Cofactors: $c_{11}=4,\ c_{12}=-3,\ c_{21}=-2,\ c_{22}=1$, so
$\operatorname{adj} A = \begin{psmallmatrix} c_{11} & c_{21} \\ c_{12} & c_{22}
\end{psmallmatrix} = \begin{psmallmatrix} 4 & -2 \\ -3 & 1 \end{psmallmatrix}$.
Hence $A^{-1} = -\tfrac12\begin{psmallmatrix} 4 & -2 \\ -3 & 1 \end{psmallmatrix}
= \begin{psmallmatrix} -2 & 1 \\ 3/2 & -1/2 \end{psmallmatrix}$.
Sanity check. $A A^{-1} = \begin{psmallmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{psmallmatrix}
\begin{psmallmatrix} -2 & 1 \\ 3/2 & -1/2 \end{psmallmatrix}
= \begin{psmallmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{psmallmatrix}$. \checkmark
\end{wex}

