\begin{wex}{Operator determinant is basis-free, over $Q$}
Let $T : Q^2 \to Q^2$, $T = \begin{psmallmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 3
\end{psmallmatrix}$ in the standard basis. Recompute $\det T$ in the basis
$\mathcal C = \{(1,1),(1,-1)\}$.
\wrec Theorem~ [REF:thm:det-operator@c11] : $\det$ is conjugation-invariant.
\wsol Standard-basis: $\det T = 6$. Change of basis $P = \begin{psmallmatrix}
1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{psmallmatrix}$, $\det P = -2$,
$P^{-1} = -\tfrac12\begin{psmallmatrix} -1 & -1 \\ -1 & 1 \end{psmallmatrix}
= \begin{psmallmatrix} 1/2 & 1/2 \\ 1/2 & -1/2 \end{psmallmatrix}$. Then
$A' = P^{-1}AP$. Compute $AP = \begin{psmallmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 3 \end{psmallmatrix}
\begin{psmallmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{psmallmatrix}
= \begin{psmallmatrix} 3 & 1 \\ 3 & -3 \end{psmallmatrix}$, so
$A' = \begin{psmallmatrix} 1/2 & 1/2 \\ 1/2 & -1/2 \end{psmallmatrix}
\begin{psmallmatrix} 3 & 1 \\ 3 & -3 \end{psmallmatrix}
= \begin{psmallmatrix} 3 & -1 \\ 0 & 2 \end{psmallmatrix}$.
$\det A' = 3\cdot 2 - (-1)\cdot 0 = 6$. Sanity check. Matches the
standard-basis value $6$, as Theorem~ [REF:thm:det-operator@c11] guarantees.
\end{wex}

