\begin{remark}[The two pictures, side by side]
Diagonalization solves both the continuous problem $\frac{dX}{dt}=AX$ and the discrete one $X_{n+1}=QX_n$ by the same move: change to an eigenbasis, where the dynamics decouple into independent scalar channels $e^{\lambda t}$ or $\lambda^n$, then change back. The exponential $e^{tA}=Pe^{tD}P^{-1}$ and the power $Q^n=PD^nP^{-1}$ are literally the same formula with $e^{t(\,\cdot\,)}$ replaced by $(\,\cdot\,)^n$. When the eigenvalues are complex, $e^{\lambda t}$ supplies the $\cos,\sin$ of oscillation and $\lambda^n$ the rotating powers; when an eigenvalue is defective, the nilpotent part injects a polynomial factor ($t$ in the flow, $n$ in the power). Everything downstream --- stability, growth rate, periodicity --- is read directly from the eigenvalues of $A$.
\end{remark}

\begin{proof}
Write $v=\sum_i v_i x_i$ and $w=\sum_j v_j y_j$. By bilinearity,
\[
\langle v,w\rangle=\Bigl\langle\sum_i v_i x_i,\ \sum_j v_j y_j\Bigr\rangle=\sum_{i,j}x_i\langle v_i,v_j\rangle y_j=\sum_{i,j}x_i a_{ij}y_j=X\trans A Y. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Symmetric and skew-symmetric]
A bilinear form is \emph{symmetric} if $\langle v,w\rangle=\langle w,v\rangle$ for all $v,w$, and \emph{skew-symmetric} if $\langle v,w\rangle=-\langle w,v\rangle$ for all $v,w$. (``Symmetric form'' always means a \emph{bilinear} symmetric form, and likewise for skew-symmetric.)
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Symmetry of form $\leftrightarrow$ symmetry of matrix, Artin 8.1.6]
Let $A$ be $n\times n$. The form $X\trans A Y$ is symmetric --- that is, $X\trans A Y=Y\trans A X$ for all $X,Y$ --- if and only if $A\trans=A$. Consequently a bilinear form is symmetric iff its matrix with respect to one (equivalently every) basis is a symmetric matrix. The analogous statement holds with ``symmetric'' replaced by ``skew-symmetric'' and $A\trans=-A$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $A\trans=A$, view the scalar $X\trans A Y$ as a $1\times1$ matrix equal to its own transpose: $X\trans A Y=(X\trans A Y)\trans=Y\trans A\trans X=Y\trans A X$, so the form is symmetric. Conversely, taking $X=e_i$, $Y=e_j$ gives $e_i\trans A e_j=a_{ij}$ and $e_j\trans A e_i=a_{ji}$; symmetry of the form forces $a_{ij}=a_{ji}$, i.e.\ $A\trans=A$. The second sentence is then immediate from $\langle v,w\rangle=X\trans A Y$. The skew case is identical with signs reversed.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Operators versus forms]
Under $\mathcal{B}'=\mathcal{B}P$, the matrix of the bilinear form $X\trans A Y$ changes to $P\trans A P$, while the matrix of the linear operator $Y=AX$ changes to $P^{-1} A P$. Since $P\trans\neq P^{-1}$ in general, the two new matrices usually differ. Congruence preserves symmetry ($\,(P\trans A P)\trans=P\trans A\trans P=P\trans A P$ when $A\trans=A$) and rank, but not eigenvalues; conjugation preserves eigenvalues. The two equivalences coincide exactly when $P$ is orthogonal, $P\trans=P^{-1}$.
\end{remark}

