\begin{remark}
A clean uniform statement: $T$ is diagonalizable in an orthonormal basis if and
only if $T$ is normal. The three named cases impose where the eigenvalues live ---
the real line (Hermitian/symmetric) or the unit circle (unitary) --- but they do
not change the diagonalization mechanism, which is normality alone.
\end{remark}

\begin{proof}
The eigenvalues of a real symmetric matrix are real: if $A v=\lambda v$ with $v\ne 0$ a complex eigenvector, then $\overline{v}\trans A v = \lambda\,\overline{v}\trans v$, while taking conjugate transpose and using $A\trans=A$, $\overline{A}=A$ gives $\overline{v}\trans A v=\overline{\lambda}\,\overline{v}\trans v$; since $\overline{v}\trans v>0$ we get $\lambda=\overline{\lambda}$. Now argue by induction on $n$. Pick a real unit eigenvector $v_1$, $Av_1=\lambda_1 v_1$. Its orthogonal complement $W=v_1^{\perp}$ is $A$-invariant: for $w\perp v_1$, $\langle v_1, Aw\rangle=\langle Av_1, w\rangle=\lambda_1\langle v_1,w\rangle=0$, so $Aw\in W$. The restriction of $A$ to $W$ is again symmetric, so by induction $W$ has an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors; adjoining $v_1$ gives an orthonormal eigenbasis of $\mathbb{R}^n$. Stacking these as the columns of $P$ makes $P$ orthogonal and $P\trans A P$ diagonal. Substituting $X=PX'$, $q=X\trans A X=(PX')\trans A(PX')=X'^{\trans}(P\trans A P)X'=\sum_i\lambda_i x_i'^{\,2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{method}{Classifying a conic $f=0$ in the plane}
\textit{Trigger.} A quadratic polynomial $f(x_1,x_2)=a_{11}x_1^2+2a_{12}x_1x_2+a_{22}x_2^2+b_1x_1+b_2x_2+c$ is given.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Form the symmetric matrix $A=\begin{psmallmatrix}a_{11}&a_{12}\\a_{12}&a_{22}\end{psmallmatrix}$ and read off $\det A$. \emph{Sign of $\det A$ already decides the type:} $\det A>0$ (definite) gives an ellipse, $\det A<0$ (indefinite) a hyperbola, $\det A=0$ a parabola --- up to degenerate cases.
\item Diagonalize: by Theorem~\ref{art:conics:spectral@c11} an orthogonal $P$ kills the cross term, leaving $\lambda_1 x_1^2+\lambda_2 x_2^2+b_1'x_1+b_2'x_2+c=0$.
\item Complete the square in each variable with $\lambda_i\ne 0$; this is a translation (see the proof of Theorem~\ref{art:conics:conics@c11}) and removes the corresponding linear term.
\item Rescale by a nonzero constant to normalize the constant term to $-1$ (or, in the parabolic case, to put the equation in the form $a_{11}x_1^2-x_2=0$). Match against the list in Theorem~\ref{art:conics:conics@c11}.
\end{enumerate}
\textbf{Shortcut for type only:} if you only need the \emph{type}, arbitrary (not necessarily orthogonal) changes of variable are allowed --- they distort an ellipse but never turn it into a hyperbola or a parabola.
\end{method}

 egin{proof}
By Theorem~ef{art:conics:spectral@c11} an orthogonal change of variable diagonalizes the quadratic part, so we may assume $f=a_{11}x_1^2+a_{22}x_2^2+b_1x_1+b_2x_2+c$. If $a_{ii}
e 0$ the substitution $x_i\mapsto x_i-	frac{b_i}{2a_{ii}}$ (a translation) eliminates $b_i$ and absorbs the change into $c$; this is completing the square. When both $a_{ii}
e 0$ we reach $a_{11}x_1^2+a_{22}x_2^2+c=0$. If $c=0$ the locus is degenerate (a point or a pair of lines); otherwise scale so $c=-1$. If both $a_{ii}<0$ the locus is empty (degenerate), so at least one is positive; arranging $a_{11}>0$ gives the ellipse ($a_{22}>0$) or the hyperbola ($a_{22}<0$). If instead one eigenvalue vanishes, say $a_{22}=0$, then $f$ is genuinely quadratic forces $a_{11}
e0$; if $b_2
e 0$ the substitution $x_2\mapsto x_2-c/b_2$ removes the constant and, after scaling, leaves the parabola $a_{11}x_1^2-x_2=0$; if $b_2=0$ the locus degenerates to two lines, one line, or empty.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The two operations are different in kind. The orthogonal step (principal axes) \emph{rotates} so the axes of the conic align with the coordinate axes; the completing-the-square step \emph{translates} so the center sits at the origin. Together they exhaust the isometry group of the plane up to reflection, which is why congruence classes match the list above.
\end{remark}

