\begin{wex}{Classify a conic by its discriminant}
Classify the locus $x_1^2+2x_1x_2-x_2^2+2x_1+2x_2-1=0$ (Artin's example).
\wrec Method ``Classifying a conic'': the matrix $A$ and $\det A$ decide the type; for type alone an arbitrary change of variable is legal.
\wsol Here $A=\begin{psmallmatrix}1&1\\1&-1\end{psmallmatrix}$, $B=(2\ 2)$, $c=-1$, and $\det A=-1-1=-2<0$, so $A$ is indefinite and the conic is a hyperbola or degenerate. To confirm, use the nonorthogonal change $x_1\mapsto x_1-x_2$, $x_2\mapsto x_2$ to clear the cross term; the polynomial becomes $x_1^2-2x_2^2+2x_1-1=0$, and completing the square in $x_1$ ($x_1^2+2x_1=(x_1+1)^2-1$) gives, after a translation and dropping primes, $x_1^2-2x_2^2-2=0$, i.e.\ $\tfrac12 x_1^2-x_2^2-1=0$. \textit{Check.} This is the hyperbola form $a_{11}x_1^2-a_{22}x_2^2-1=0$ with $a_{11}=\tfrac12$, $a_{22}=1$, matching the prediction $\det A<0$. The locus is a hyperbola.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{From eigenvalues to principal axes}
Put $q(x_1,x_2)=5x_1^2-4x_1x_2+5x_2^2$ into principal-axis form and name the conic $q=9$.
\wrec Theorem~\ref{art:conics:spectral@c11}: diagonalize the symmetric matrix; the diagonal entries are the eigenvalues and the conic type follows from their signs.
\wsol $A=\begin{psmallmatrix}5&-2\\-2&5\end{psmallmatrix}$ has characteristic polynomial $(5-\lambda)^2-4=\lambda^2-10\lambda+21=(\lambda-3)(\lambda-7)$, so $\lambda_1=3$, $\lambda_2=7$. Orthonormal eigenvectors are $\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(1,1)\trans$ (for $3$) and $\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(1,-1)\trans$ (for $7$). With $P=\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\begin{psmallmatrix}1&1\\1&-1\end{psmallmatrix}$ orthogonal, $P\trans A P=\operatorname{diag}(3,7)$ and $q=3x_1'^{\,2}+7x_2'^{\,2}$. \textit{Check.} $q=9$ becomes $\tfrac{x_1'^{\,2}}{3}+\tfrac{x_2'^{\,2}}{9/7}=1$; both coefficients positive, so an ellipse with semi-axes along the lines $x_1=x_2$ and $x_1=-x_2$. Eigenvalues both positive $\Rightarrow\det A=21>0$, consistent with ``ellipse.''
\end{wex}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{art:conics:spectral@c11} an orthogonal $P$ gives $P\trans A P=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n)$. For each $i$ replace the $i$th basis vector by a scalar multiple: under $v_i\mapsto c_i v_i$ the entry $\lambda_i$ becomes $c_i^2\lambda_i$. Choosing $c_i=1/\sqrt{|\lambda_i|}$ when $\lambda_i\ne0$ normalizes it to $\operatorname{sign}(\lambda_i)=\pm1$, and $\lambda_i=0$ stays $0$. Folding this diagonal rescaling $D$ into $Q=PD$ yields the stated normal form. Nondegeneracy means $\det A\ne0$, i.e.\ no zero eigenvalue, i.e.\ $z=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sylvester's Law of Inertia]\label{art:conics:sylvester@c11}
The signature $(p,m)$ in Theorem~\ref{art:conics:normal@c11} depends only on the congruence class of $A$, not on the diagonalizing matrix $Q$. Equivalently, two real symmetric matrices are congruent ($A'=Q\trans A Q$ for invertible $Q$) iff they have the same rank $p+m$ and the same $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The number $z$ of zeros is congruence-invariant because $\rank(Q\trans A Q)=\rank A$ for invertible $Q$, so $p+m=\rank A$ is fixed; it remains to show $p$ is fixed. Realize $A$ as a symmetric form $\beta(X,Y)=X\trans A Y$. Let $p_0$ be the largest dimension of a subspace $U\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n$ on which $\beta$ is \emph{positive definite}, i.e.\ $\beta(u,u)>0$ for all $u\ne0$ in $U$; this number is defined without reference to any basis, hence is a congruence invariant. In the normal-form basis $v_1,\dots,v_n$, the span $U_+$ of the $p$ vectors with $\beta(v_i,v_i)=+1$ is positive definite, so the invariant is $\ge p$. Conversely, if $U$ has dimension $>p$, then $U$ meets the span $V_-$ of the $m+z$ vectors with $\beta(v_i,v_i)\le0$ nontrivially (dimensions add to more than $n$), giving a nonzero $u\in U\cap V_-$ with $\beta(u,u)\le0$, contradicting positive-definiteness on $U$. Hence the invariant equals $p$, so $p$ is determined by the congruence class.
\end{proof}

\begin{pitfall}
Signature is a \emph{congruence} ($Q\trans A Q$) invariant, not a \emph{similarity} ($Q^{-1} A Q$) invariant. Congruence preserves the signs of the eigenvalues but not their magnitudes; similarity preserves the eigenvalues themselves. The two notions agree only when $Q$ is orthogonal, where $Q\trans=Q^{-1}$.
\end{pitfall}

\begin{wex}{Find a signature}
Find the signature of $A=\begin{psmallmatrix}0&1&0\\1&0&0\\0&0&2\end{psmallmatrix}$.
\wrec Theorem~\ref{art:conics:normal@c11}: signature counts the positive and negative eigenvalues; either diagonalize or reduce by congruence.
\wsol The eigenvalues come from the block structure: the $2\times2$ block $\begin{psmallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{psmallmatrix}$ has eigenvalues $\pm1$, and the last block contributes $2$. So the eigenvalues are $1,-1,2$: one is negative, two positive, none zero. Hence $p=2$, $m=1$, $z=0$, signature $(2,1)$, and $A$ is congruent to $\operatorname{diag}(1,1,-1)=I_{2,1}$. \textit{Check.} $\det A=2\cdot(0\cdot0-1\cdot1)=-2\ne0$ confirms $z=0$; the sign of $\det A=\lambda_1\lambda_2\lambda_3=(1)(-1)(2)=-2$ matches an odd number ($m=1$) of negative eigenvalues.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Signature by completing squares}
Determine the signature of $q(x,y,z)=x^2+2xy+2y^2+4yz+5z^2$ without computing eigenvalues.
\wrec Theorem~\ref{art:conics:sylvester@c11} lets us use \emph{any} invertible change of variable; completing squares is a congruence and reads off the signs directly.
\wsol Group around $x$: $x^2+2xy=(x+y)^2-y^2$, so $q=(x+y)^2+y^2+4yz+5z^2$. Now group the $y$ terms: $y^2+4yz=(y+2z)^2-4z^2$, giving $q=(x+y)^2+(y+2z)^2+z^2$. Set $u=x+y$, $v=y+2z$, $w=z$ (an invertible substitution). Then $q=u^2+v^2+w^2$, all coefficients $+1$. \textit{Check.} Signature $(3,0)$, so $q$ is positive definite; indeed $q$ is a sum of three real squares, hence $\ge0$ and zero only when $u=v=w=0$, i.e.\ $x=y=z=0$. Congruent to $I_3$.
\end{wex}

\begin{definition}[Skew-symmetric form]
A bilinear form $\langle\,,\,\rangle$ on $V$ is \textbf{skew-symmetric} (alternating) if $\langle v,v\rangle=0$ for all $v\in V$. Over a field of characteristic $\ne2$ this is equivalent to $\langle u,v\rangle=-\langle v,u\rangle$ for all $u,v$. Its matrix $A$ satisfies $A\trans=-A$ with zero diagonal.
\end{definition}

