\begin{definition}[The real classical groups]
Inside $\GL_n=\GL_n(\mathbb{R})$ or $\GL_n(\mathbb{C})$ one singles out:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(SL)] the \emph{special linear group} $\SL_n=\{P\in\GL_n(\mathbb{R}) : \det P=1\}$;
\item[(O)] the \emph{orthogonal group} $\mathrm{O}_n=\{P\in\GL_n(\mathbb{R}) : P\trans P=I\}$;
\item[(U)] the \emph{unitary group} $\mathrm{U}_n=\{P\in\GL_n(\mathbb{C}) : \dual{P}P=I\}$;
\item[(Sp)] the \emph{symplectic group} $\mathrm{Sp}_{2n}=\{P\in\GL_{2n}(\mathbb{R}) : P\trans S\,P=S\}$, where $S=\begin{psmallmatrix}0&I_n\\-I_n&0\end{psmallmatrix}$ is the standard skew-symmetric form on $\mathbb{R}^{2n}$.
\end{enumerate}
Adding the word \emph{special} imposes $\det P=1$ in addition: the \emph{special orthogonal group} $\mathrm{SO}_n=\mathrm{O}_n\cap\SL_n$ and the \emph{special unitary group} $\mathrm{SU}_n=\mathrm{U}_n\cap\{\det P=1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
Each relation says the matrix preserves a form, so each is a group automatically. An orthogonal $P$ preserves the dot product $X\trans Y$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$, since $(PX)\trans(PY)=X\trans P\trans P\,Y=X\trans Y$. A unitary $P$ preserves the standard Hermitian product $\dual{X}Y$ on $\mathbb{C}^n$. A symplectic $P$ preserves the skew form $X\trans S\,Y$ on $\mathbb{R}^{2n}$. The condition $\det P=1$ likewise picks out the maps preserving signed volume. There are also indefinite analogues: the \emph{Lorentz group} $\mathrm{O}_{3,1}=\{P : P\trans I_{3,1}P=I_{3,1}\}$, and more generally $\mathrm{O}_{p,m}$ for any signature, preserve an indefinite form rather than the positive-definite dot product. The complex orthogonal group ($P\trans P=I$ over $\mathbb{C}$) is \emph{not} the unitary group ($\dual{P}P=I$); the defining relations differ.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[The defining relations are closed under the group operations]
For each of $\SL_n,\mathrm{O}_n,\mathrm{U}_n,\mathrm{Sp}_{2n}$, the defining set is a subgroup of the ambient $\GL_n$. Moreover every orthogonal, unitary, or symplectic matrix is invertible, and every orthogonal or unitary matrix has $\det$ of absolute value $1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
We argue uniformly; take the orthogonal case, the others being identical with $\trans$ replaced by $\dual{\,\cdot\,}$ or with the form $S$ inserted. The identity satisfies $I\trans I=I$. If $P\trans P=I$ and $Q\trans Q=I$, then $(PQ)\trans(PQ)=Q\trans P\trans P\,Q=Q\trans Q=I$, so the product is orthogonal; and from $P\trans P=I$ we get $P^{-1}=P\trans$, whence $P$ is invertible and $(P^{-1})\trans P^{-1}=P\,P\trans=I$ (using $P\trans P=I\Rightarrow PP\trans=I$ for square $P$), so the inverse is orthogonal. For determinants, $P\trans P=I$ gives $\det(P)^2=\det(P\trans)\det(P)=\det I=1$, so $\det P=\pm1$; in the unitary case $\dual{P}P=I$ gives $\overline{\det P}\,\det P=\abs{\det P}^2=1$. The symplectic case uses $P\trans S P=S$, and since $S$ is invertible, $\det(P)^2\det S=\det S$ forces $\det(P)^2=1$; in fact one can show $\det P=1$ always, so the two uses of the letter ``S'' in $\mathrm{SO},\mathrm{SU},\mathrm{Sp}$ do not conflict.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Dimensions and compactness]
The \emph{dimension} of a linear group $G$ is, roughly, the number of degrees of freedom of a matrix in $G$. The full space of real $n\times n$ matrices has dimension $n^2$; each independent scalar relation cuts the count by one. Thus $\SL_2$ has dimension $3$ (the single equation $\det P=1$ removes one degree of freedom from the four entries). The orthogonal group $\mathrm{O}_n$ has dimension $\tfrac12 n(n-1)$: the $n^2$ equations of $P\trans P=I$ are symmetric, giving $\binom{n+1}{2}$ independent conditions. The unitary group $\mathrm{U}_n$ has dimension $n^2$ as a real manifold. The groups $\mathrm{O}_n,\mathrm{SO}_n,\mathrm{U}_n,\mathrm{SU}_n$ are \emph{compact}: they are closed (defined by polynomial equations) and bounded (an orthogonal or unitary matrix has columns of length $1$, so all entries lie in $[-1,1]$ or the unit disk). By contrast $\GL_n$, $\mathrm{Sp}_{2n}$ and, for $n\ge2$, $\SL_n$ are \emph{not} compact ($\SL_1=\{1\}$ is trivial) --- $\SL_2$ contains $\begin{psmallmatrix}t&0\\0&t^{-1}\end{psmallmatrix}$ with $t\to\infty$.
\end{remark}

\begin{proof}
The map $\theta\mapsto R(\theta)$ from the additive group $\mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z}$ of angles to $\mathrm{SO}(2)$ is a homomorphism, since the angle-addition formulas give $R(\theta)R(\phi)=R(\theta+\phi)$; it is surjective because every $2\times2$ real orthogonal matrix of determinant $1$ has this form (orthonormal columns with the right orientation), and injective modulo $2\pi$. The map $\theta\mapsto e^{i\theta}=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta$ is a homomorphism onto the unit-modulus complex numbers, with the same kernel. A unit complex number is exactly a $1\times1$ unitary matrix: $\mathrm{U}(1)=\{p\in\mathbb{C}^{\times} : \overline{p}\,p=1\}=\{p : \abs{p}=1\}$. Composing, $R(\theta)\mapsto e^{i\theta}$ is a well-defined isomorphism. The single parameter $\theta$ shows the dimension is $1$.
\end{proof}

