\begin{wex}{Degree $6$ and an immediate non-membership}
Let $K=\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]2,\zeta_3)$ (the splitting field of $x^3-2$). Compute $[K:\mathbb{Q}]$ two ways and deduce $\sqrt[3]2\notin\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_3)$.
\wrec Build the tower in two orders; the tower law forces the two products to agree, and a degree that does not divide the total rules out membership.
\wsol Order 1: $[\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]2):\mathbb{Q}]=3$ ($x^3-2$, Eisenstein). Then $\zeta_3$ has degree $\le2$ over $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]2)$; since $\zeta_3\notin\mathbb{R}\supseteq\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]2)$, its degree is exactly $2$ (minimal poly $x^2+x+1$). So $[K:\mathbb{Q}]=2\cdot3=6$. Order 2: $[\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_3):\mathbb{Q}]=2$ ($\Phi_3=x^2+x+1$); then $[K:\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_3)]\le3$ (as $\sqrt[3]2$ is a root of $x^3-2$) and the product must equal $6$, forcing $[K:\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_3)]=3$, i.e.\ $x^3-2$ is irreducible over $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_3)$ and $\sqrt[3]2\notin\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_3)$. Sanity check: $6=2\cdot3=3\cdot2$ both ways; and $3\nmid2=[\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_3):\mathbb{Q}]$ already shows $\sqrt[3]2$ (degree $3$) cannot lie in $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_3)$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Degree forces irreducibility}
