\wsol If $\sqrt[3]2\in\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt2)$ then $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]2)\subseteq\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt2)$, so by the tower law $3=[\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]2):\mathbb{Q}]$ divides $[\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt2):\mathbb{Q}]=2$, false; hence $\sqrt[3]2\notin\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt2)$. General statement: if $\alpha\in E$ then $F(\alpha)\subseteq E$, so $p=[F(\alpha):F]$ divides $[E:F]<p$, impossible for a prime $p$ unless $[E:F]=0$, which cannot happen; contradiction. Sanity check: this is the standard ``prime degree blocks proper subfields'' argument --- a degree-$p$ simple extension has no intermediate fields strictly between $F$ and $F(\alpha)$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{A tower over a finite field}
