$x^{12}-1=\Phi_1\Phi_2\Phi_3\Phi_4\Phi_6\Phi_{12}.$

Reading the divisor lattice top-down, the degrees add: $\sum_{d\mid 12}\varphi(d)=1+1+2+2+2+4=12=\deg(x^{12}-1)$.

The divisor lattice of $12$ governs the factorization of $x^{12}-1$, but the subfield lattice of $Q(\zeta_{12})$ is a different object. Since $\varphi(12)=4$ and $\zeta_{12}=e^{2\pi i/12}=\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}+\tfrac{i}{2}$ gives $Q(\zeta_{12})=Q(i,\sqrt3)$, the Galois group is Klein four, so there are three quadratic subfields: $Q(i)=Q(\zeta_4)$, $Q(\sqrt{-3})=Q(\zeta_3)$, and $Q(\sqrt3)$. (Note $i\cdot\sqrt3=\sqrt{-3}$, so any two of these three square roots generate the third.) Each covering edge has relative degree $2$:

