 \node[ltlab] at (-0.17,1.5) {$2$};
 \node[ltlab] at (0.93,1.5) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture} 

\begin{dgx}
\dgi{Objects:} Five nodes arranged as a diamond with an extra middle level: the base field $Q$ (bottom), three intermediate quadratic subfields $Q(i)=Q(\zeta_4)$, $Q(\sqrt{-3})=Q(\zeta_3)$, and $Q(\sqrt3)$ (middle row), and the top field $Q(\zeta_{12})=Q(i,\sqrt3)$ (top). All are subfields of $C$ and finite extensions of $Q$.
\dgi{Arrows:} Each edge represents an inclusion of fields (a field extension of degree $2$). The six edges divide into two levels: three lower edges from $Q$ up to each quadratic subfield, and three upper edges from each quadratic subfield up to $Q(\zeta_{12})$. Every edge carries degree label $2$, expressing that each extension is quadratic.
\dgi{What commutes:} The tower law: for each quadratic subfield $E$, $[Q(\zeta_{12}):Q]=[Q(\zeta_{12}):E]\cdot[E:Q]=2\cdot2=4$. All three paths from $Q$ to $Q(\zeta_{12})$ through a middle node give the same total degree $4$.
\dgi{Why it commutes:} Since $\varphi(12)=4$ and $Q(\zeta_{12})=Q(i,\sqrt3)$, the Galois group $Gal(Q(\zeta_{12})/Q)\cong(Z/12Z)^\times\congZ/2Z\timesZ/2Z$ (Klein four), which has exactly three subgroups of index $2$, each corresponding to one of the three quadratic subfields by the Galois correspondence. The degree product $2\cdot2=4$ follows from the tower law applied to any such subgroup.
\dgi{Why this matters here:} This diagram visually separates two distinct lattice structures: the divisor lattice of $12$ (which organizes the factorization of $x^{12}-1$) and the subfield lattice of $Q(\zeta_{12})$ (which is controlled by the Galois group). The point made in the text is that these are different objects, and the subfield lattice has only three intermediate fields at level $2$ (not six divisors).
\dgi{Concrete example:} The relation $i\cdot\sqrt3=\sqrt{-3}$ explains why knowing any two of $i$, $\sqrt3$, $\sqrt{-3}$ gives the third: $Q(i,\sqrt3)$ already contains $\sqrt{-3}=i\sqrt3$, and each pair of the three quadratic subfields generates $Q(\zeta_{12})$ over $Q$.
\dgi{Common pitfall:} Students confuse this subfield lattice with the divisor lattice of $12$. The divisor lattice has six nodes ($1,2,3,4,6,12$) and represents factors $\Phi_d$ of $x^{12}-1$; the subfield lattice shown here has five nodes and represents actual field extensions, organized by the Galois group, not by divisibility.
\dgi{Takeaway:} The point of this diagram is that $Q(\zeta_{12})$ sits above three distinct quadratic subfields simultaneously, each reachable by a degree-$2$ step, reflecting the Klein-four Galois group rather than the richer divisor lattice of $12$.
\end{dgx}

