\begin{proposition}[{discriminant test, Artin 16.9.5}]
Let $G$ be the Galois group of an irreducible quartic $f$. Then $D$ is a square in $F$ if and only if $G$ contains no odd permutation. Consequently:
\begin{itemize}
\item If $D$ is a square in $F$, then $G$ is $A_4$ or $V_4$.
\item If $D$ is not a square in $F$, then $G$ is $S_4$, $D_4$, or $C_4$.
\end{itemize}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$D=\delta^2$ is a square in $F$ exactly when $\delta\in F$, i.e.\ when $\delta$ is fixed by every element of $G$. The permutations fixing $\delta$ are precisely the even ones, so $D$ is a square iff $G\sgr A_4$. Among the five transitive subgroups, those lying in $A_4$ are $A_4$ itself and $V_4$ (its elements $(ij)(kl)$ are even); the ones containing an odd permutation are $S_4$, $D_4$ (which contains $4$-cycles and transpositions, both odd), and $C_4$ (a $4$-cycle is odd). Reading off the list of five transitive subgroups above gives the two cases.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The same argument works in any degree: for an irreducible separable $f$ of degree $n$ over a field $F$ of characteristic $\neq2$, the group $\Gal(K/F)\sgr A_n$ if and only if $\disc(f)$ is a square in $F$ (Artin 16.9.6). The discriminant detects ``even versus odd,'' nothing finer.
\end{remark}

\begin{lemma}[{the $\beta_i$ are distinct}]
If the roots of $f$ are distinct, then $\beta_1,\beta_2,\beta_3$ are distinct, so $g$ has no repeated root.
\end{lemma}

