\item Let $X$ be a Noetherian topological space (Chapter 6, Exercise 5) and let $E \subseteq X$. Show that $E \in \mathcal{F}$ if and only if, for each irreducible closed subset $X_0$ of $X$, either $\overline{E \cap X_0} \neq X_0$ or else $E \cap X_0$ contains a non-empty open subset of $X_0$. [Suppose $E \notin \mathcal{F}$. Then the collection of closed sets $X' \subseteq X$ such that $E \cap X' \notin \mathcal{F}$ is not empty and therefore has a minimal element $X_0$. Show that $X_0$ is irreducible and then that each of the alternatives leads to the conclusion that $E \cap X_0 \in \mathcal{F}$.] The sets belonging to $\mathcal{F}$ are called the \emph{constructible} subsets of $X$.

\item Let $X$ be a Noetherian topological space and let $E$ be a subset of $X$. Show that $E$ is open in $X$ if and only if, for each irreducible closed subset $X_0$ in $X$, either $E \cap X_0 = \varnothing$ or else $E \cap X_0$ contains a non-empty open subset of $X_0$. [The proof is similar to that of Exercise 21.]

\item Let $A$ be a Noetherian ring, $f : A \to B$ a ring homomorphism of finite type (so that $B$ is Noetherian). Let $X = \Spec(A)$, $Y = \Spec(B)$, and let $f^* : Y \to X$ be the mapping associated with $f$. Then the image under $f^*$ of a constructible subset $E$ of $Y$ is a constructible subset of $X$.

[By Exercise 20 it is enough to take $E = U \cap C$ where $U$ is open and $C$ is closed in $Y$; thus, replacing $B$ by a homomorphic image, we reduce to the case where $E$ is open in $Y$. Since $Y$ is Noetherian, $E$ is quasi-compact, and therefore $E$ is the finite union of open sets of the form $\Spec(B_g)$. Hence reduce to the case $E = Y$. To show that $f^*(Y)$ is constructible, use the criterion of Exercise 21. Let $X_0$ be an irreducible closed subset of $X$ such that $f^*(Y) \cap X_0$ is dense in $X_0$. We have $f^*(Y) \cap X_0 = f^*(f^{*-1}(X_0))$, and $f^{*-1}(X_0) = \Spec((A/\fp) \otimes_A B)$, where $X_0 = \Spec(A/\fp)$ with $\fp$ prime. Hence reduce to the case where $A$ is an integral domain and $f$ is injective. If $Y_1, \ldots, Y_n$ are the irreducible components of $Y$, it is enough to show that some $f^*(Y_i)$ contains a non-empty open set in $X$. So finally we are brought down to the situation in which $A, B$ are integral domains and $f$ is injective (and still of finite type); now use Chapter 5, Exercise 21 to complete the proof.]

\item With the notation and hypotheses of Exercise 23, $f^*$ is an open mapping $\Leftrightarrow$ $f$ has the going-down property (Chapter 5, Exercise 10). [Suppose $f$ has the going-down property. As in Exercise 23, reduce to proving that $E = f^*(Y)$ is open in $X$.

\begin{proposition}[{$\rad\fa$ is an ideal; $\nil(R/\fa)=(\rad\fa)/\fa$}]
For any ideal $\fa$ of $R$, $\rad\fa$ is an ideal containing $\fa$, and $(\rad\fa)/\fa$ is the nilradical of $R/\fa$. In particular $R/\fa$ is reduced if and only if $\fa$ is a radical ideal.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Clearly $\fa\subseteq\rad\fa$. The nilradical of $R/\fa$ consists of cosets some power of which is $0$, which under the Lattice Isomorphism Theorem corresponds exactly to elements of $R$ some power of which lies in $\fa$, i.e.\ to $\rad\fa$. So it suffices to see $\nil(R)$ is an ideal. It is closed under multiplication by $R$ since $(ra)^n=r^na^n$, and closed under addition by the Binomial Theorem: if $a^n=0$ and $b^m=0$ then in $(a+b)^{n+m}=\sum_i\binom{n+m}{i}a^ib^{n+m-i}$ every term has $i\ge n$ (so $a^i=0$) or $n+m-i\ge m$ (so $b^{n+m-i}=0$), forcing $(a+b)^{n+m}=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Passing to $R/\fa$ reduces to the case $\fa=0$: show $\nil(R)=\bigcap_{\fp}\fp$. If $a$ is nilpotent, $a^k=0\in\fp$ for every prime $\fp$; taking the least $n$ with $a^n\in\fp$ and factoring $a^{n-1}\cdot a\in\fp$ forces $a\in\fp$ by primality, so $a$ lies in every prime. Conversely if $a$ is \emph{not} nilpotent, the multiplicative set $\{1,a,a^2,\dots\}$ misses $0$; the family of ideals meeting no power of $a$ is nonempty ($0$ is in it) and closed under unions of chains, so Zorn's Lemma yields a maximal such ideal $\fp$, which one checks is prime and avoids $a$. Hence $a\notin\bigcap_\fp\fp$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[{primes are radical}]
Every prime ideal $\fp$ is radical (it is the intersection of the primes containing it, the smallest of which is $\fp$). Hence maximal ideals are radical too.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The geometric reading is the heart of A\&M's philosophy: a prime ideal is simultaneously a ``prime of arithmetic'' (in $\mathbb{Z}$ the primes containing $(a)$ are the $(p)$ with $p\mid a$) and a ``point of geometry'' (for $k$ algebraically closed, the maximal ideals of $k[V]$ are the points of $V$). The radical collects exactly the information visible at every point: $a\in\rad\fa$ iff $a$ vanishes at every prime over $\fa$. Over $k[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ with $k$ algebraically closed this will become ``$a$ vanishes on the zero set $V(\fa)$,'' the Nullstellensatz.
\end{remark}

\begin{wex}{Radical of a principal ideal in $\mathbb{Z}$}
Compute $\rad(180)$ in $\mathbb{Z}$ and identify the primes over $(180)$.
\wrec In a U.F.D., if $a=p_1^{e_1}\cdots p_r^{e_r}$ then $\rad(a)=(p_1\cdots p_r)$; the minimal primes over $(a)$ are the $(p_i)$ (in $\mathbb{Z}$ these are all the primes over $(a)$).
\wsol Factor $180=2^2\cdot3^2\cdot5$, so the distinct prime divisors are $2,3,5$ and $\rad(180)=(2\cdot3\cdot5)=(30)$. The primes containing $(180)$ are $(2),(3),(5)$, and $(2)\cap(3)\cap(5)=(\lcm(2,3,5))=(30)$, matching the theorem. Sanity check: $30^2=900=5\cdot180\in(180)$ so $30\in\rad(180)$; and $\rad(180)$ is itself radical since $30=2\cdot3\cdot5$ is squarefree, so $\rad\rad(180)=(30)$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{A radical ideal need not be prime}
In $k[x,y]$ show $\rad(xy)=(xy)$ is radical but not prime.
\wrec An ideal is radical iff the quotient has no nilpotents; it is prime iff the quotient is a domain.
\wsol Since $xy$ is squarefree (product of the distinct irreducibles $x,y$), $(xy)=(x)\cap(y)$ and $\rad(xy)=(x)\cap(y)=(xy)$, a radical ideal. But it is not prime: $x\cdot y\in(xy)$ while neither $x$ nor $y$ lies in $(xy)$ (degree reasons). Sanity check: $k[x,y]/(xy)$ has no nilpotents (a polynomial whose square is divisible by $xy$ is itself divisible by both $x$ and $y$ off the axes), confirming radical, yet it has the zero divisors $\bar x,\bar y$, confirming non-prime.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Nilradical of $\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}$}
Find the nilpotent elements of $R=\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}$ and exhibit $\nil(R)$ as an intersection of primes.
\wrec $\nil(R)=\rad(0)=\bigcap\fp$; in $\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$ the primes are $(p)/n\mathbb{Z}$ for $p\mid n$.
\wsol $12=2^2\cdot3$, so $\rad(0)$ in $\mathbb{Z}$ pulled to the quotient is $(6)/12\mathbb{Z}=\{0,6\}$. Indeed $6^2=36\equiv0$, while $2,3,4$ are not nilpotent ($2^k$ cycles through $2,4,8,4,\dots$). The primes of $\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}$ are $(2)$ and $(3)$, and $(2)\cap(3)=(6)$. Sanity check: $\{0,6\}$ is closed under addition and absorbs multiplication, and $(\bar6)^2=\bar0$, so it is the nilradical; the quotient $\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}$ is not reduced precisely because $12$ is not squarefree.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Powers of the radical: the Noetherian case}
If $R$ is Noetherian, show some power of $\rad\fa$ lies in $\fa$; deduce the nilradical is nilpotent.
\wrec In a Noetherian ring $\rad\fa$ is finitely generated, so finitely many of its generators' powers land in $\fa$.
\wsol Write $\rad\fa=(a_1,\dots,a_m)$ with $a_i^{k_i}\in\fa$, and put $k=\max_i k_i$. Then $(\rad\fa)^{km}$ is generated by products $a_1^{d_1}\cdots a_m^{d_m}$ with $\sum d_i=km$, and by pigeonhole some $d_i\ge k$, so each generator has a factor $a_i^{d_i}\in\fa$; hence $(\rad\fa)^{km}\subseteq\fa$. Taking $\fa=0$ gives $\nil(R)^{km}=0$. Sanity check: in $\mathbb{Z}/p^e\mathbb{Z}$ the nilradical is $(p)$ and $(p)^e=0$, exactly a finite power vanishing; over a non-Noetherian ring this can fail, e.g.\ $k[x_1,x_2,\dots]/(x_1,x_2^2,x_3^3,\dots)$ has a non-nilpotent nilradical.
\end{wex}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Order-reversal and the unit inequalities $\fa\subseteq IV(\fa)$, $A\subseteq VI(A)$ are immediate from the definitions; these are exactly the axioms of a (contravariant) Galois connection, and the two ``triangle'' identities follow formally by applying $V$ to $A\subseteq VI(A)$ and using order-reversal both ways. That $I(A)$ is radical: if $f^k$ vanishes on $A$ then $f(P)^k=0$ in the field $k$ forces $f(P)=0$, so $f\in I(A)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{wex}{$V$ collapses an ideal and its radical}
Show $V(\fa)=V(\rad\fa)$ for every ideal $\fa$, with the explicit case $\fa=(x^2)$ in $k[x]$.
\wrec A point kills $f$ iff it kills $f^k$; so passing to radicals does not change zero sets.
\wsol If $a\in\rad\fa$ then $a^k\in\fa$, and $a^k(P)=0$ on $V(\fa)$ gives $a(P)^k=0$, hence $a(P)=0$; so $V(\fa)\subseteq V(\rad\fa)$, and the reverse holds since $\fa\subseteq\rad\fa$ reverses. For $(x^2)\subseteq k[x]$: $\rad(x^2)=(x)$ and $V(x^2)=V(x)=\{0\}$. Sanity check: the ``double point'' $(x^2)$ and the reduced point $(x)$ have the same underlying set $\{0\}$; the radical forgets the multiplicity, exactly the redundancy the radical was invented to remove.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{$I$ of a finite point set}
With $k$ infinite, compute $I(\{(0,0),(1,0)\})$ in $k[x,y]$.
\wrec $I(A)$ is the set of polynomials vanishing at every listed point; for finitely many points it is an intersection of the corresponding maximal ideals.
\wsol A point $(a,b)$ has $I(\{(a,b)\})=(x-a,y-b)$, the maximal ideal of functions vanishing there. So $I(\{(0,0),(1,0)\})=(x,y)\cap(x-1,y)$. A polynomial lies here iff it vanishes at both points; one checks this intersection equals $(y,\ x^2-x)=(y,\ x(x-1))$. Sanity check: $y$ vanishes on both, and $x(x-1)$ vanishes at $x=0$ and $x=1$; conversely modulo $(y,x(x-1))$ every class is represented by $c_0+c_1x$, a $2$-dimensional $k$-space, matching the $2$ points -- the coordinate ring of two reduced points is $k\times k$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{The closure operator $V\!I$ in the non-closed case}
Over $k=\mathbb{R}$, show $I(V((x^2+1)))$ strictly exceeds $\rad(x^2+1)$.
\wrec $V$ over a non-algebraically-closed field can be empty even for a proper radical ideal; $I(\varnothing)$ is everything.
\wsol In $\mathbb{R}[x]$ the ideal $(x^2+1)$ is maximal (hence radical), but $V(x^2+1)=\varnothing$ since $x^2+1$ has no real root. Then $I(V(x^2+1))=I(\varnothing)=\mathbb{R}[x]\supsetneq(x^2+1)=\rad(x^2+1)$. Sanity check: this is precisely the obstruction A\&M and D\&F flag -- a one-variable polynomial with no zeros. Over $\mathbb{C}$ no such obstruction exists, and the Nullstellensatz below restores $I(V(\fa))=\rad\fa$.
\end{wex}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
(1) If $V=V_1\cup V_2$ properly, pick $f_1\in I(V_1)\setminus I(V)$ and $f_2\in I(V_2)\setminus I(V)$; then $f_1f_2$ vanishes on $V$ so $f_1f_2\in I(V)$ with neither factor in $I(V)$, so $I(V)$ is not prime. Conversely if $f_1f_2\in I(V)$ with $f_i\notin I(V)$, set $V_i=V\cap V(f_i)$: both are proper closed subsets and $V=V_1\cup V_2$, so $V$ is reducible. The translation to $k[V]$ is the standard fact that $R/\fp$ is a domain iff $\fp$ is prime. (2) Existence: were the family of algebraic sets with no finite decomposition into varieties nonempty, a maximal $I(V_0)$ (Noetherian!) gives a minimal bad $V_0$ which, being reducible, splits into strictly smaller pieces that \emph{do} decompose -- contradiction. Uniqueness: intersect one decomposition against the other and use irreducibility to match components.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is the order-reversing bijection of the Galois connection made precise on the irreducible level: varieties in $\Aff^n$ correspond to prime ideals that arise as $I(V)$. It refines the radical theorem -- writing a radical ideal as $\bigcap\fp$ over its minimal primes is the algebraic shadow of decomposing $V$ into components. (The general module/ideal version, primary decomposition, is developed in Section~6.)
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}
For a variety $V$ the domain $k[V]$ has a fraction field $k(V)$, the field of \emph{rational functions} on $V$. The \emph{dimension} of $V$ is $\dim V=\trdeg_k k(V)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{wex}{The cuspidal cubic is a variety}
With $k$ infinite, show $V=V(y^2-x^3)\subseteq\Aff^2$ is irreducible and compute its coordinate ring and dimension.
\wrec $V$ is a variety iff $I(V)$ is prime iff $k[V]$ is a domain; $\dim V=\trdeg_k k(V)$.
\wsol $y^2-x^3$ is irreducible in $k[x,y]$ (it is monic of degree $2$ in $y$ with no root in $k[x]$, as $x^3$ is not a square), so $(y^2-x^3)$ is prime. It equals $I(V)$: if $f$ vanishes on $V$, write $f\equiv a(x)+y\,b(x)\pmod{y^2-x^3}$; then $a(t^2)+t^3b(t^2)=0$ for every $t$ in the infinite field $k$, so this polynomial in $t$ is $0$, and comparing even and odd powers of $t$ gives $a=b=0$. Then $k[V]=k[x,y]/(y^2-x^3)$ is a domain, so $V$ is a variety. Setting $t=y/x$ in $k(V)$ gives $x=t^2,\ y=t^3$, so $k(V)=k(t)$ and $\dim V=\trdeg_k k(t)=1$. Sanity check: $V$ is the image of the parametrization $t\mapsto(t^2,t^3)$, a curve, consistent with dimension $1$; the cusp at the origin does not affect irreducibility or dimension.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{A union of axes is reducible}
With $k$ infinite, decompose $V(xy)\subseteq\Aff^2$ into components and read off the coordinate ring's defect.
\wrec $V(fg)=V(f)\cup V(g)$; the components are the $V(\fp_i)$ for the minimal primes $\fp_i$ over $I(V)$.
\wsol $V(xy)=V(x)\cup V(y)$, the union of the two coordinate axes. Since $(xy)=(x)\cap(y)$ with $(x),(y)$ prime and incomparable, the irredundant decomposition is into the $y$-axis $V(x)$ and the $x$-axis $V(y)$. The coordinate ring $k[x,y]/(xy)$ has the zero divisors $\bar x\bar y=0$, so it is not a domain -- exactly why $V(xy)$ is not a variety. Sanity check: two distinct minimal primes $(x),(y)$ over the radical ideal $(xy)$ give exactly two components; the ring is reduced (radical ideal) but not a domain (reducible).
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Points are zero-dimensional varieties}
Show a single point $P=(a_1,\dots,a_n)$ is a variety and $\dim\{P\}=0$.
\wrec $I(\{P\})=(x_1-a_1,\dots,x_n-a_n)$ is maximal, hence prime; dimension is the transcendence degree of the residue field.
\wsol The ideal $\fm_P=(x_1-a_1,\dots,x_n-a_n)$ is maximal with $k[x]/\fm_P\cong k$, so it is prime and $\{P\}$ is irreducible. Then $k[\{P\}]=k$ and $k(\{P\})=k$, giving $\dim=\trdeg_k k=0$. Sanity check: a finite set $\{P_1,\dots,P_r\}$ decomposes into its singletons, each a $0$-dimensional component, and its coordinate ring is $k^r$ -- $r$ minimal primes, $r$ points, dimension $0$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Linear subspaces are varieties}
With $k$ infinite, show the zero set $V$ of linear polynomials $\ell_1,\dots,\ell_m$, when nonempty, is a variety.
\wrec An ideal generated by linear forms (after translating to the origin) has domain quotient -- a polynomial ring in the remaining coordinates.
\wsol After an affine change of coordinates, $V$ is the linear subspace $x_{d+1}=\cdots=x_n=0$, with $I(V)=(x_{d+1},\dots,x_n)$, a prime ideal (the quotient is the domain $k[x_1,\dots,x_d]$). So $V$ is a variety with $k[V]\cong k[x_1,\dots,x_d]$ and $\dim V=\trdeg_k k(x_1,\dots,x_d)=d$. Sanity check: $\dim V=d$ matches the geometric dimension of a $d$-plane, and $k(V)=k(x_1,\dots,x_d)$ is a rational function field, the prototype of dimension $d$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{The hyperbola and its rational function field}
With $k$ infinite, show $V=V(xy-1)\subseteq\Aff^2$ is a variety with $k[V]\cong k[x,x^{-1}]$.
\wrec $V(xy-1)$ is a variety iff $xy-1$ is irreducible; the coordinate ring is the localization adjoining $x^{-1}$.
\wsol $xy-1$ is irreducible (degree $1$ in $y$, primitive), so $(xy-1)$ is prime. It equals $I(V)$: if $f$ vanishes on $V$ then $f(t,t^{-1})=0$ for the infinitely many $t\in k^\times$, so $f(x,x^{-1})=0$ in $k[x,x^{-1}]$, i.e.\ $f\in\ker\bigl(k[x,y]\to k[x,x^{-1}],\ y\mapsto x^{-1}\bigr)=(xy-1)$. Hence $V$ is a variety. On $V$, $y=x^{-1}$, so $k[V]=k[x,y]/(xy-1)\cong k[x,x^{-1}]$, a domain. Its fraction field is $k(x)$, so $\dim V=1$. Sanity check: every point of $V$ has $x\ne0$, consistent with inverting $x$; and although over $\mathbb{R}$ the hyperbola has two visible ``branches,'' they are \emph{not} subvarieties -- the Zariski topology cannot separate them since $V$ is irreducible.
\end{wex}

\begin{remark}
The Zariski topology is coarse and almost never Hausdorff. On $\Aff^1$ over an infinite field the only proper closed sets are the finite ones, so any two nonempty opens meet; points are closed ($T_1$) but no two points are separated by disjoint opens. This coarseness is the source of the slogan ``nonempty open $\Rightarrow$ dense'' and is why irreducibility (a topological condition) is so strong. In Section~8 the same topology is placed on $\Spec R$, with prime ideals as points; the present picture is its $k$-rational shadow.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[{Zariski closure}]
For any subset $A\subseteq\Aff^n$ the Zariski closure (smallest algebraic set containing $A$) is $\overline{A}=V(I(A))$. Hence $A$ is dense in a variety $V$ iff $V(I(A))=V$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
$A\subseteq V(I(A))$, and if $W=V(\fb)\supseteq A$ is any algebraic set then $I(W)\subseteq I(A)$ so $V(I(A))\subseteq V(I(W))=W$; thus $V(I(A))$ is the smallest such. This is the closure operator of the Galois connection again, now read topologically.
\end{proof}

\begin{wex}{The Zariski closure of the parabola-points}
Over $\mathbb{R}$, find the Zariski closure of $A=\{(a,a^2):a\in\mathbb{R}\}\subseteq\Aff^2$.
\wrec $\overline A=V(I(A))$; compute $I(A)$ as the polynomials vanishing along the parametrized set.
\wsol A polynomial $f$ vanishes on $A$ iff $f(a,a^2)=0$ for all $a$, i.e.\ $f(x,y)\equiv0\pmod{y-x^2}$ since substituting $y=x^2$ kills it for the infinitely many $a$ (and $\mathbb{R}$ is infinite). So $I(A)=(y-x^2)$ and $\overline A=V(y-x^2)$, the whole parabola. Sanity check: $y-x^2$ is irreducible, so $\overline A$ is a variety of dimension $1$; the infinite set $A$ is Zariski dense in it, illustrating that an infinite subset of a curve already determines the curve.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Zariski versus Euclidean closed sets}
Over $\mathbb{R}$, show the interval $[0,1]\subseteq\Aff^1$ is Euclidean-closed but not Zariski-closed.
\wrec Zariski-closed sets in $\Aff^1$ over an infinite field are only $\varnothing$, all of $\Aff^1$, and finite sets.
\wsol $[0,1]$ is infinite but is not all of $\Aff^1$, so it is not a Zariski-closed set; its Zariski closure is $V(I([0,1]))=V(0)=\Aff^1$, since the only polynomial vanishing on an infinite set is $0$. Yet $[0,1]$ is closed in the Euclidean topology. Sanity check: this confirms the Euclidean topology is strictly finer; every Zariski-closed set (finite, or all of $\mathbb{R}$) is Euclidean-closed, but not conversely.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Nonempty open sets are dense in a variety}
Let $V$ be a variety and $U_1,U_2$ nonempty Zariski-open subsets of $V$. Show $U_1\cap U_2\ne\varnothing$, hence every nonempty open is dense.
\wrec Irreducibility says $V$ is not a union of two proper closed sets; complements translate this to opens.
\wsol If $U_1\cap U_2=\varnothing$ then $V=(V\setminus U_1)\cup(V\setminus U_2)$ writes $V$ as a union of two closed sets, each proper since $U_i\ne\varnothing$. This contradicts irreducibility. Taking $U_2$ arbitrary nonempty open shows $U_1$ meets every nonempty open, i.e.\ $\overline{U_1}=V$. Sanity check: on $\Aff^1$ over $\mathbb{R}$ a nonempty open is $\mathbb{R}$ minus finitely many points; any two such overlap in cofinitely many points, exactly density. Reducible sets fail this: on $V(xy)$ the punctured axes give disjoint nonempty opens.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{An irreducible set is connected}
Deduce that a variety is connected in the Zariski topology.
\wrec Connected means not a disjoint union of two nonempty opens; use that nonempty opens are dense.
\wsol If $V=U_1\sqcup U_2$ with both $U_i$ nonempty open and disjoint, then $U_1\cap U_2=\varnothing$ contradicts the previous example. So no such splitting exists and $V$ is connected. Sanity check: connectedness is weaker than irreducibility -- $V(xy)$ is connected (the axes meet at the origin) but reducible -- so irreducible $\Rightarrow$ connected is the expected one-way implication.
\end{wex}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
The full proof is deferred to Section~7, where it follows from integral dependence and the ``weak'' Nullstellensatz (that the maximal ideals of $E[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ are exactly the $\fm_P=(x_1-a_1,\dots,x_n-a_n)$ for $P\in\Aff^n_E$). Granting the weak form, the equality $I(V(\fa))=\rad\fa$ is obtained by the Rabinowitsch trick: a function $g$ vanishing on $V(\fa)$ is shown to satisfy $g^N\in\fa$ by introducing an extra variable $t$ and the relation $1-tg$, whose vanishing set forces $g$ into the radical.
\end{proof}

\begin{method}{Testing equality of algebraic sets and ideals over $\overline{E}$.}
To decide whether $V(\fa)=V(\fb)$, compare radicals: $V(\fa)=V(\fb)\iff\rad\fa=\rad\fb$. To decide whether a function $g$ vanishes on $V(\fa)$, test whether $g\in\rad\fa$ (e.g.\ via a Gr\"obner-basis radical-membership computation, or by exhibiting $g^N\in\fa$). To find the components of $V(\fa)$, compute the minimal primes over $\rad\fa$.
\end{method}

