e m\in M$, then $\Ann(m)$ is a proper ideal, so it lies in some maximal $ m$. In $M_{ m}$, the class $m/1=0$ would give $s m=0$ for some $s
otin m$ -- but then $s\in\Ann(m)\subseteq m$, a contradiction. So $M_{ m}
e0$, against (3). Hence $M=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[{injectivity and surjectivity are local}]
An $R$-module map $\varphi\colon M\to N$ is injective (resp.\ surjective, resp.\ an isomorphism) iff $\varphi_{\fm}\colon M_{\fm}\to N_{\fm}$ is injective (resp.\ surjective, iso) for every maximal ideal $\fm$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
By exactness, $(\ker\varphi)_{\fm}=\ker(\varphi_{\fm})$ and $(\coker\varphi)_{\fm}=\coker(\varphi_{\fm})$. Apply the theorem to $\ker\varphi$ (resp.\ $\coker\varphi$): it is $0$ iff all its localizations vanish iff each $\varphi_{\fm}$ is injective (resp.\ surjective).
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[{flatness is local too}]
A\&M record (3.10) that flatness of $M$ is equivalent to flatness of every $M_{\fp}$, equivalently every $M_{\fm}$. The proof is the same localize-the-kernel argument applied to the criterion ``$N\to P$ injective $\Rightarrow M\otimes N\to M\otimes P$ injective,'' using that $\otimes$ commutes with localization. The pattern -- \emph{a property is global iff local at every maximal ideal} -- is the organizing principle of commutative algebra.
\end{remark}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Clearly $R\subseteq\bigcap_{\fm}R_{\fm}$. If $a\in F$ lies in every $R_{\fm}$, set $\fa=\{d\in R:\ da\in R\}$, an ideal with $a\in R\iff\fa=R$. If $\fa\ne R$, pick a maximal $\fm\supseteq\fa$; since $a\in R_{\fm}$, $a=r/d$ with $d\notin\fm$, so $d\in\fa\setminus\fm$, contradiction. Hence $\fa=R$ and $a\in R$.
\end{proof}

\begin{wex}{Detecting $M=0$ locally}
Let $R=\mathbb{Z}$ and $M$ an abelian group with $M_{(p)}=0$ for all primes $p$ and $M_{(0)}=0$. Conclude $M=0$.
\wrec The local-global principle: $M=0\iff M_{\fm}=0$ for all maximal $\fm$; the maximal ideals of $\mathbb{Z}$ are the $(p)$.
\wsol Every maximal ideal of $\mathbb{Z}$ is $(p)$ for a prime $p$, and by hypothesis each $M_{(p)}=0$. By the theorem, $M=0$. Sanity check: contrapositively, if $M\ne0$ pick $0\ne m$; $\Ann(m)=(n)$ for some $n\ge0$; choose a prime $p\mid n$ (or $p$ arbitrary if $n=0$) with $(p)\supseteq\Ann(m)$ -- then $m/1\ne0$ in $M_{(p)}$, contradicting $M_{(p)}=0$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Local-global for injectivity}
Show $\varphi\colon\mathbb{Z}\xrightarrow{2}\mathbb{Z}$ is injective by checking it locally.
\wrec Injectivity is local: $\varphi$ injective iff $\varphi_{(p)}$ injective for all primes $p$.
\wsol At $(2)$: $\varphi_{(2)}\colon\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}\to\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}$ is multiplication by $2$, injective on the domain $\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}$ (no zero divisors). At $(p)$, $p\ne2$: $2$ is a unit in $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$, so $\varphi_{(p)}$ is an isomorphism, hence injective. At $(0)$: multiplication by $2$ on $\mathbb{Q}$ is injective. All localizations injective $\Rightarrow\varphi$ injective. Sanity check: $\coker\varphi=\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ is \emph{not} zero, so $\varphi$ is not surjective -- and indeed $\coker(\varphi_{(2)})=\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}/2\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}\cong\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}\ne0$, correctly detecting non-surjectivity at the single prime $(2)$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{$\mathbb{Z}=\bigcap_p\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ concretely}
Verify that a rational $a/b$ (in lowest terms) lies in every $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ iff $a/b\in\mathbb{Z}$.
\wrec $\mathbb{Z}=\bigcap_{\fm}\mathbb{Z}_{(\fm)}$; $a/b\in\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}\iff p\nmid b$.
\wsol If $a/b$ in lowest terms has $b>1$, take a prime $p\mid b$; then $a/b\notin\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$, so $a/b$ fails to be in the intersection. If $b=1$, then $a/b=a\in\mathbb{Z}\subseteq\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ for all $p$. Hence $\bigcap_p\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}=\mathbb{Z}$. Sanity check: $1/6\notin\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}$ and $\notin\mathbb{Z}_{(3)}$ (caught at the primes dividing the denominator), but $1/6\in\mathbb{Z}_{(5)}$ -- so $1/6$ is excluded from the intersection exactly because it is not an integer.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Normality is local}
State and use the criterion: a domain $R$ is integrally closed iff every $R_{\fm}$ is.
\wrec Like $M=0$, integral closure is detected at maximal ideals, via $R=\bigcap_{\fm}R_{\fm}$.
\wsol If $R$ is normal and $y\in F$ is integral over $R_{\fp}$, clearing the (finitely many) denominators $d_i\notin\fp$ shows $y'=y(d_0\cdots d_{n-1})^n$ is integral over $R$, hence $y'\in R$, hence $y\in R_{\fp}$: so $R_{\fp}$ is normal. Conversely if each $R_{\fm}$ is normal and $y\in F$ is integral over $R$, then $y$ is integral over each $R_{\fm}$, so $y\in R_{\fm}$ for all $\fm$, whence $y\in\bigcap_{\fm}R_{\fm}=R$: $R$ is normal. Sanity check: $R=k[t^2,t^3]$ is not normal; its non-normality localizes to the single maximal ideal $\fm=(t^2,t^3)$, where $R_{\fm}$ fails to be integrally closed ($t=t^3/t^2$ is integral but not in $R_{\fm}$), pinpointing the singular point.
\end{wex}

\begin{pitfall}
Localization need not be surjective on rings (e.g.\ $\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}$ misses $1/3$) and need not be injective: $R\to S^{-1}R$ has kernel the $S$-torsion. And ``local property'' is one-directional in subtlety: $M=0$, injectivity, flatness localize correctly, but properties like ``$R$ is a domain'' do \emph{not} -- every $R_{\fm}$ can be a domain while $R$ is not (e.g.\ $R=\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}$: $R_{(2)}\cong\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ and $R_{(3)}\cong\mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}$ are fields, yet $R$ has zero divisors). Always check whether the property you want is genuinely local.
\end{pitfall}

\begin{wex}{Reading the spectrum of $R_f$ as a distinguished open}
For $R=k[x,y]$, $f=x$, describe $\Spec R_x$ as an open subset of $\Spec R$ and find its dimension.
\wrec $\Spec R_f=D(f)=\{\fq:\ f\notin\fq\}$; inverting $x$ removes exactly the primes containing $x$.
\wsol The primes containing $x$ are those of $k[x,y]$ with $x\in\fq$, i.e.\ the primes of $k[x,y]/(x)\cong k[y]$ pulled back -- the line $V(x)$. So $\Spec R_x=\Spec k[x,y]\setminus V(x)=\Aff^2\setminus\{x=0\}$, the complement of a line. Its dimension is still $2$ (an open dense subset of the plane). Sanity check: $\dim R_x\le\dim k[x,y]=2$ because the primes of $R_x$ correspond to primes of $k[x,y]$ (preserving inclusions), and the chain $(0)\subsetneq(y)\subsetneq(x-1,y)$ of primes not containing $x$ gives $\dim R_x\ge2$; so $\dim R_x=2$. (For a general domain, inverting one nonzero element can lower the dimension: $\dim\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}=1$ but $\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}[1/2]=\mathbb{Q}$ has dimension $0$.)
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Factoring $R_{\fp}$ through $R_f$}
Show that for $f\notin\fp$, $R_{\fp}=(R_f)_{\fp R_f}$, i.e.\ localizing at $\fp$ is unchanged by first inverting an $f\notin\fp$.
\wrec Both rings invert exactly $R\setminus\fp$: in $R_f$, the prime $\fp R_f$ has complement $S_f=\{g/f^n:\ g\notin\fp\}$, and re-localizing inverts these.
\wsol The composite $R\to R_f\to(R_f)_{\fp R_f}$ inverts every element of $R\setminus\fp$ (since $f\notin\fp$ is already inverted, and everything outside $\fp R_f$ is inverted in the second step). By the universal property there is a unique map $R_{\fp}\to(R_f)_{\fp R_f}$; conversely $(R_f)_{\fp R_f}$ inverts only elements that are units in $R_{\fp}$, giving the inverse. Hence the two coincide. Sanity check: take $R=\mathbb{Z}$, $\fp=(2)$, $f=3$. Then $R_f=\mathbb{Z}[1/3]$ and $(\mathbb{Z}[1/3])_{(2)}=\{a/b:\ b\text{ odd}\}=\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}$, since inverting $3$ then localizing at $2$ recovers exactly the integers localized at $2$. Matches.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Primary ideals under localization}
Let $\fq$ be $\fp$-primary in $R$ and $S$ a multiplicative set. Describe $S^{-1}\fq$ in the two cases $S\cap\fp=\varnothing$ and $S\cap\fp\ne\varnothing$.
\wrec Primary ideals correspond to primary ideals when their radical misses $S$; if the radical meets $S$, the extension is the whole ring.
\wsol If $S\cap\fp\ne\varnothing$, pick $s\in S\cap\fp$; some power $s^n\in\fq$ (as $\fp=\rad\fq$), and $s^n\in S$, so $S^{-1}\fq=S^{-1}R$. If $S\cap\fp=\varnothing$, then $S^{-1}\fp$ is prime and $S^{-1}\fq$ is $S^{-1}\fp$-primary with contraction back to $R$ equal to $\fq$ (using that $dr\in\fq$, $d\notin\fp$ force $r\in\fq$ by primariness). Sanity check: $R=\mathbb{Z}$, $\fq=(4)$ (which is $(2)$-primary), $S=\mathbb{Z}\setminus(2)$ gives $S^{-1}(4)=4\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}$, still $(2)\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}$-primary, contracting to $(4)$; whereas $S=\mathbb{Z}\setminus(3)$ meets $(2)$ at $2$, and $2\in S$ inverts, so $S^{-1}(4)=\mathbb{Z}_{(3)}$, the whole ring.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Localization and minimal primary decomposition}
For a minimal primary decomposition $\fa=\fq_1\cap\dots\cap\fq_m$ in a Noetherian $R$ with $\fq_i$ being $\fp_i$-primary, describe $S^{-1}\fa$ when $S$ misses $\fp_1,\dots,\fp_t$ and meets $\fp_{t+1},\dots,\fp_m$.
\wrec $S^{-1}$ commutes with finite intersection; components whose radical meets $S$ extend to the unit ideal and drop out.
\wsol Since $S^{-1}(\fq_i)=S^{-1}R$ for $i>t$ (their primes meet $S$) and these are units in the intersection, $S^{-1}\fa=S^{-1}\fq_1\cap\dots\cap S^{-1}\fq_t$, a minimal primary decomposition in $S^{-1}R$ with $S^{-1}\fq_i$ being $S^{-1}\fp_i$-primary. Contracting back, $(S^{-1}\fa)^c=\fq_1\cap\dots\cap\fq_t$. Sanity check: taking $S=R\setminus\fp$ for a \emph{minimal} prime $\fp$ of $\fa$ keeps only the single component $\fq$ belonging to $\fp$, which proves the primary component at an isolated prime is uniquely determined by $\fa$ -- the local characterization of the isolated components.
\end{wex}

Prove that if $P \in \Ass_R(M)$ then $D^{-1}P \in \Ass_{D^{-1}R}(D^{-1}M)$. [Use Proposition 38(3) and Proposition 42.]

\item Suppose $D^{-1}P \in \Ass_{D^{-1}R}(D^{-1}M)$ where $P = (a_1, \ldots, a_n)$ is a finitely generated prime ideal in $R$ with $P \cap D = \emptyset$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Suppose $m/d \in D^{-1}M$ has annihilator $D^{-1}P$ in $D^{-1}R$. Show that $d_i a_i m = 0 \in R$ for some $d_1, \ldots, d_n \in D$.
\item[(b)] Let $d' = d_1 d_2 \cdots d_n$. Show that $P = \Ann(d'm)$ and conclude that $P \in \Ass_R(M)$. [The inclusion $P \subseteq \Ann(d'm)$ is immediate. For the reverse inclusion, show that $b \in \Ann(d'm)$ implies that $b/1$ annihilates $m/d$ in $D^{-1}M$, hence $b/1 \in D^{-1}P$, and conclude $b \in P$.]
\end{enumerate}

\item Suppose $M$ is a module over the Noetherian ring $R$. Use the previous two exercises to show that under the bijection of Proposition 38(3) the prime ideals $P$ of $\Ass_R(M)$ with $P \cap D = \emptyset$ correspond bijectively with the prime ideals of $\Ass_{D^{-1}R}(D^{-1}M)$.

\item Suppose $M$ is a module over the Noetherian ring $R$ and $D$ is a multiplicatively closed subset of $R$. Let $S$ be the subset of prime ideals $P$ in $\Ass_R(M)$ with $P \cap D \neq \emptyset$. This exercise proves that the kernel $N$ of the localization map $M \to D^{-1}M$ is the unique submodule $N$ of $M$ with $\Ass_R(N) = S$ and $\Ass_R(M/N) = \Ass_R(M) - S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $N'$ is a submodule of $M$ with $\Ass_R(N') = S$ and $\Ass_R(M/N') = \Ass_R(M) - S$ as in Exercise 35 in Section 1, prove that the diagram
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
M \arrow[r, "\pi"] \arrow[d, "\varphi"'] & M/N' \arrow[d, "\varphi'"] \\
D^{-1}M \arrow[r, "\pi'"'] & D^{-1}(M/N')
\end{tikzcd}
\]
is commutative, where $\pi$ and $\pi'$ are the natural projections (cf. Proposition 42(6)) and $\varphi, \varphi'$ are the localization homomorphisms.
\item[(b)] Show that $\Ass_{D^{-1}R}(D^{-1}N') = \emptyset$ and conclude that $D^{-1}N' = 0$ and that $\pi'$ is injective. [Use the previous exercise, the definition of $S$, and Exercise 34 in Section 1.]
\item[(c)] If $x$ is in the kernel $K$ of $\varphi'$ show that $\Ann(x) \cap D \neq \emptyset$ and that $\Ass_R(K) \subseteq S$. Show that $\Ass_R(K) \subseteq \Ass_R(M/N')$ implies that $\Ass_R(K) = \emptyset$, and deduce that $K = 0$.
\item[(d)] Prove $\varphi$ and $\pi$ have the same kernel, i.e., $N = N'$, and this submodule of $M$ is unique.
\end{enumerate}

\item Prove that $\Ass_R(M) \subseteq \Supp(M)$. [If $Rm \cong R/P$ use Proposition 42(4) and Proposition 46(1) to show that $0 \neq (Rm)_P \subseteq M_P$.]

\item Suppose that $R$ is Noetherian and $M$ is an $R$-module.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $P \in \Supp(M)$ prove that $P$ contains a prime ideal $Q$ with $Q \in \Ass_R(M)$.
\item[(b)] If $P$ is a minimal prime in $\Supp(M)$, show that $P \in \Ass_R(M)$. [Use Exercise 33 in Section 1 to show that $\Ass_{R_P}(M_P) \neq \emptyset$ and then use Exercise 37.]
\item[(c)] Conclude that $\Ass_R(M) \subseteq \Supp(M)$ and that these two sets have the same minimal elements.
\end{enumerate}
\end{exers}

\begin{proposition}[{the radical of a primary ideal}]
Let $\fq$ be primary. Then $\fp=\rad\fq$ is prime, and is the \emph{smallest} prime ideal containing $\fq$. We then say $\fq$ is \emph{$\fp$-primary} and call $\fp$ the prime \emph{belonging to} $\fq$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
If $xy\in\rad\fq$ then $(xy)^m=x^my^m\in\fq$; since $\fq$ is primary, either $x^m\in\fq$ (so $x\in\rad\fq$) or $(y^m)^n\in\fq$ for some $n$ (so $y\in\rad\fq$). Hence $\rad\fq$ is prime. It is the smallest prime over $\fq$ because $\rad\fq$ is the intersection of all such primes.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
(2) Pass to $A=R/\fq$; then $\rad(0)=\fm/\fq$ is maximal, hence is the \emph{unique} prime of $A$ (the nilradical sits in every prime). So every non-unit of $A$ lies in this maximal ideal, i.e.\ is nilpotent; thus every zero-divisor is nilpotent and $\fq$ is primary. (3) $\fm^n\subseteq\fq$ gives $\fm\subseteq\rad\fq$ and $\fq\subseteq\fm$ gives $\rad\fq\subseteq\fm$, so $\rad\fq=\fm$; apply (2).
\end{proof}

\begin{pitfall}
Two converses fail, and both failures are the heart of the theory.
\emph{(i) A primary ideal need not be a prime power.} In $k[x,y]$ the ideal $\fq=(x^2,y)$ is $(x,y)$-primary (its radical is the maximal ideal $(x,y)$) yet is not $\fp^n$ for any prime $\fp$: if $(x^2,y)=\fp^n$ then $x^2,y\in\fp$ force $\fp=(x,y)$, but $y\notin(x,y)^2$ rules out $n\ge2$ and $x\notin(x^2,y)$ rules out $n=1$.
\emph{(ii) A prime power need not be primary.} In $R=k[x,y,z]/(xy-z^2)$ the ideal $\fp=(\bar x,\bar z)$ is prime, but $\fp^2$ is not primary: $\bar x\bar y=\bar z^2\in\fp^2$ while $\bar x\notin\fp^2$ and no power of $\bar y$ lies in $\fp^2$. So ``primary'' is genuinely between ``prime'' and ``prime power.''
\end{pitfall}

\begin{wex}{The primary ideals of $\mathbb{Z}$}
Determine all primary ideals of $\mathbb{Z}$ and identify the prime each belongs to.
\wrec In a PID/UFD, $(\pi^n)$ should be $(\pi)$-primary; conversely every primary ideal of $\mathbb{Z}$ should be a prime power.
\wsol The proper ideals of $\mathbb{Z}$ are $(0)$ and $(m)$ for $m\ge2$. $(0)$ is prime, hence primary. For $(m)$ with $m=p_1^{e_1}\cdots p_r^{e_r}$: if $r\ge2$ then $p_1^{e_1}\cdot(m/p_1^{e_1})\in(m)$ but neither factor nor any power of the second lies in $(m)$, so $(m)$ is not primary. If $m=p^e$, then $\rad(p^e)=(p)$ is maximal, so $(p^e)$ is $(p)$-primary by the criterion. Thus the primary ideals of $\mathbb{Z}$ are exactly $(0)$ and the prime powers $(p^e)$, $p$ prime, $e\ge1$. Sanity check: $(12)=(2^2)\cap(3)$ is the intersection of the primary ideals $(4)$ and $(3)$ --- a primary decomposition mirroring $12=2^2\cdot3$, exactly the arithmetic prototype.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{$(x^2,y)$ is primary but not a prime power}
Show $\fq=(x^2,y)\subseteq k[x,y]$ is primary and find its prime.
\wrec $\rad\fq$ should be the maximal ideal $(x,y)$; an ideal whose radical is maximal is automatically primary.
\wsol The quotient is $k[x,y]/(x^2,y)\cong k[x]/(x^2)$, a local ring with maximal ideal $(\bar x)$ and $\bar x$ nilpotent. Every element is $a+b\bar x$; it is a zero-divisor iff $a=0$ iff it is nilpotent. So $\fq$ is primary. Its radical: $\rad\fq=\rad(x^2,y)=(x,y)$ since $x\in\rad$ ($x^2\in\fq$) and $y\in\fq$, and $(x,y)$ is maximal. Hence $\fq$ is $(x,y)$-primary. Sanity check: the inclusions $(x,y)^2=(x^2,xy,y^2)\subseteq(x^2,y)\subseteq(x,y)$ exhibit $\fm^2\subseteq\fq\subseteq\fm$, confirming primary via the maximal-radical criterion.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Two minimal decompositions of $(x^2,xy)$}
Find primary decompositions of $\fa=(x^2,xy)\subseteq k[x,y]$ and show minimality.
\wrec $\fa$ is not primary ($xy\in\fa$, $x\notin\fa$, no power of $y$ in $\fa$); look to write it as $(\text{prime})\cap(\text{maximal-radical primary})$.
\wsol Factor out the common $x$: $\fa=(x)\cap(x^2,y)=(x)\cap(x^2,xy,y^2,\dots)$. Check: $(x)\cap(x^2,y)$ consists of multiples of $x$ that also lie in $(x^2,y)$; $f=xg\in(x^2,y)$ means $xg\equiv0$ in $k[x,y]/(x^2,y)=k[x]/(x^2)$, i.e.\ $g\in(x,y)$, so $f\in(x^2,xy)=\fa$. Both pieces are primary: $(x)$ is prime, $(x^2,y)$ is $(x,y)$-primary. A \emph{different} minimal decomposition is $\fa=(x)\cap(x,y)^2$, since $(x)\cap(x,y)^2=(x^2,xy)$ likewise. Sanity check: radicals are $(x)$ and $(x,y)$ in both, distinct, and neither component contains the other, so both are minimal --- yet the $(x,y)$-primary component differs ($(x^2,y)$ vs.\ $(x,y)^2$). Non-uniqueness of components is real.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{A three-component decomposition}
Give a minimal primary decomposition of $\fa=(x^2,xy,xz,yz)\subseteq k[x,y,z]$ and list its primes.
\wrec Look for the irreducible pieces of $Z(\fa)$: where does $x^2=xy=xz=yz=0$ vanish? On $\{x=y=0\}\cup\{x=z=0\}$ plus possibly an embedded point.
\wsol One checks $\fa=(x,y)\cap(x,z)\cap(x,y,z)^2$. Indeed $(x,y)\cap(x,z)=(x,yz)$ (modulo $x$ this is $(y)\cap(z)=(yz)$ in $k[y,z]$), so an element of the right side is $f=xg+yzh$; since $f\in(x,y,z)^2$ has no linear term, $g$ has zero constant term, i.e.\ $g\in(x,y,z)$, and $f\in x(x,y,z)+(yz)=\fa$. Conversely each generator of $\fa$ lies in all three. The radicals are $(x,y)$ (prime), $(x,z)$ (prime), and $\rad(x,y,z)^2=(x,y,z)$ (maximal). Sanity check: $Z(\fa)$ is the union of the two lines $\{x=y=0\}$ (the $z$-axis) and $\{x=z=0\}$ (the $y$-axis); these are the loci of the isolated primes $(x,y)$ and $(x,z)$, while $(x,y,z)$ is the embedded origin where the two lines cross --- three primes, two of dimension $1$ and one embedded point.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Decomposing a principal ideal in a UFD}
For $a=p_1^{e_1}\cdots p_m^{e_m}$ in a UFD $R$ (distinct irreducibles $p_i$), give the minimal primary decomposition of $(a)$.
\wrec Each $(p_i^{e_i})$ is $(p_i)$-primary; their intersection should be $(\mathrm{lcm})=(a)$.
\wsol $(a)=(p_1^{e_1})\cap\cdots\cap(p_m^{e_m})$. Since the $p_i$ are pairwise non-associate, the intersection of the $(p_i^{e_i})$ is generated by their lcm, which is $a$. Each $(p_i^{e_i})$ is $(p_i)$-primary (its radical $(p_i)$ is prime, indeed maximal in $\mathbb{Z}$; in a general UFD one checks directly that $\pi^n\mid ab$, $\pi^n\nmid a$ force $\pi\mid b$), the radicals $(p_i)$ are distinct, and no factor is redundant (dropping $(p_i^{e_i})$ enlarges the intersection to $(a/p_i^{e_i})\ne(a)$). All primes $(p_i)$ are isolated --- none contains another. Sanity check: with $R=\mathbb{Z}$, $a=180=2^2\cdot3^2\cdot5$ gives $(180)=(4)\cap(9)\cap(5)$, $\rad(180)=(2)\cap(3)\cap(5)=(30)$, matching $\rad(180)=(2\cdot3\cdot5)$.
\end{wex}

\begin{theorem}[{First Uniqueness Theorem}]
Let $\fa$ be decomposable, $\fa=\bigcap_{i=1}^n\fq_i$ a minimal primary decomposition, $\fp_i=\rad\fq_i$. Then the set $\{\fp_1,\dots,\fp_n\}$ is exactly the set of prime ideals occurring among the ideals $\rad(\fa\colonid x)$ as $x$ ranges over $R$. Consequently $\Ass(\fa)$ is independent of the chosen minimal decomposition.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
For $x\in R$, $(\fa\colonid x)=\bigcap_i(\fq_i\colonid x)$, so by the colon lemma $\rad(\fa\colonid x)=\bigcap_{x\notin\fq_j}\fp_j$. If this radical is prime it must equal one of the $\fp_j$ (a prime containing a finite intersection contains a factor; primeness pins it to a single $\fp_j$). Conversely, minimality (b) lets us pick, for each $i$, an $x_i\in\bigcap_{j\ne i}\fq_j\setminus\fq_i$; then $\rad(\fa\colonid x_i)=\fp_i$. So the primes among $\{\rad(\fa\colonid x)\}$ are precisely $\{\fp_i\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is the module-theoretic associated-prime set in disguise. Recall $\fp$ is associated to an $R$-module $M$ if $\fp=\Ann(m)$ for some $m\in M$, i.e.\ $M$ contains a copy of $R/\fp$. Applying this to $M=R/\fa$: since $\Ann(x+\fa)=(\fa\colonid x)$, the first uniqueness theorem says that $\Ass(\fa)$ consists of the primes that are \emph{radicals} of annihilators of elements of $R/\fa$. When $R$ is Noetherian each of these primes is itself such an annihilator (A\&M Proposition 7.17), so $\Ass(\fa)=\Ass_R(R/\fa)$; for non-Noetherian $R$ the two sets can differ. The geometric reading: $\Ass$ records the ``components and embedded data'' that $\fa$ remembers about its zero locus.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[{isolated and embedded primes}]
A prime in $\Ass(\fa)$ is \emph{isolated} (or \emph{minimal}) if it is minimal in $\Ass(\fa)$ with respect to inclusion; otherwise it is \emph{embedded}. The isolated primes are exactly the minimal primes over $\fa$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
(1) $\fp\supseteq\fa=\bigcap\fq_i$ gives $\fp\supseteq\fq_i$ (a prime containing a finite intersection contains a factor), hence $\fp\supseteq\rad\fq_i=\fp_i$; the minimal such $\fp$ are the minimal $\fp_i$. (2) Radical commutes with finite intersection. (3) For $\fa=0$ each $\fq_i$ has $\rad\fq_i=\fp_i$ and $0=\bigcap\fq_i$; the colon description $D=\bigcup_{x\ne0}\rad(0\colonid x)=\bigcup\fp_i$ identifies zero-divisors, while nilpotents form $\rad(0)=\bigcap\fp_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{wex}{Associated primes via colon ideals}
For $\fa=(x^2,xy)=(x)\cap(x,y)^2$ in $k[x,y]$, compute $\Ass(\fa)$ and classify each prime.
\wrec Use $\rad(\fa\colonid f)$ for cleverly chosen $f$; the isolated prime is the minimal one.
\wsol The decomposition has radicals $(x)$ and $(x,y)$. Take $f=x$: $(\fa\colonid x)=\{g:xg\in(x^2,xy)\}=\{g:g\in(x,y)\}=(x,y)$, so $\rad(\fa\colonid x)=(x,y)$, an associated prime. Take $f=y$: $(\fa\colonid y)=\{g:yg\in(x^2,xy)\}$; $yg\in(x^2,xy)=x(x,y)$ forces $x\mid g$, giving $(\fa\colonid y)=(x)$, prime. So $\Ass(\fa)=\{(x),(x,y)\}$. Since $(x)\subsetneq(x,y)$, $(x)$ is isolated (minimal) and $(x,y)$ is embedded. Sanity check: $\rad\fa=(x)\cap(x,y)=(x)$, matching ``radical = intersection of isolated primes,'' and $Z(\fa)=$ the $y$-axis (locus of $(x)$) with the origin (locus of $(x,y)$) embedded in it.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Associated primes of $\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$ as a module}
For $R=\mathbb{Z}$ and $M=\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$, compute $\Ass_R(M)$, and reconcile with $\Ass$ of the ideal $(n)$.
\wrec $\fp=\Ann(m)$ prime; cyclic submodules of $\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$ are $\mathbb{Z}/d\mathbb{Z}$ for $d\mid n$, with annihilator $(d)$, which is prime iff $d=p$.
\wsol An element $\bar m\in\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$ of additive order $d$ generates a copy of $\mathbb{Z}/d\mathbb{Z}$ with $\Ann(\bar m)=(d)$. This is prime iff $d$ is prime. For each prime $p\mid n$ there is an element of order $p$ (namely $\overline{n/p}$), giving $(p)\in\Ass_R(M)$; and these are the only primes arising. So $\Ass_\mathbb{Z}(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})=\{(p):p\mid n\}$. Sanity check: this matches $\Ass$ of the ideal $(n)$, whose minimal primary decomposition $(n)=\bigcap_p(p^{e_p})$ has radicals $(p)$ for $p\mid n$ --- all isolated, no embedded primes, consistent with $(n)$ being far from radical yet having a ``square-free'' set of primes.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Minimal primes of an ideal in $k[x,y,z]$}
Find the minimal primes over $\fa=(xy,xz)\subseteq k[x,y,z]$ and hence $\rad\fa$.
\wrec Minimal primes over $\fa$ = isolated associated primes, matching (for $k$ algebraically closed) the irreducible components of $Z(\fa)$. Factor $\fa=(x)\cap(y,z)$.
\wsol $xy,xz\in\fa$ vanish where $x=0$ or where $y=z=0$. Indeed $\fa=(x)\cap(y,z)$: an element divisible by $x$ and lying in $(y,z)$\,---\,check generators $xy=x\cdot y\in(x)\cap(y,z)$, etc.; conversely $(x)\cap(y,z)\subseteq(xy,xz)$ since $f\in(x)$ means $f=xg$, and $f\in(y,z)$ forces $g\in(y,z)$, so $f\in x(y,z)=(xy,xz)$. Both $(x)$ and $(y,z)$ are prime, incomparable, hence both isolated; there are no embedded primes here. Thus the minimal primes are $(x),(y,z)$ and $\rad\fa=(x)\cap(y,z)=\fa$ (so $\fa$ is radical). Sanity check: $Z(\fa)$ is the plane $x=0$ together with the line $y=z=0$ (the $x$-axis), the two irreducible components matching the two minimal primes; $\fa$ radical $\iff$ all components prime, consistent.
\end{wex}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
(1) If $s\in S\cap\fp$ then $s^n\in\fq$ for some $n$, and $s^n$ becomes a unit in $S^{-1}R$, so $S^{-1}\fq$ contains a unit. (2) Standard localization compatibilities: $\rad$ and primary survive, and for $\fp\cap S=\varnothing$ no element of $S$ is a zero-divisor mod $\fq$, so contraction recovers $\fq$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Localize at $S=R\setminus\bigcup_k\fp_{i_k}$. By the localization proposition, $S^{-1}\fq_j=S^{-1}R$ exactly when $\fp_j\not\subseteq\bigcup\fp_{i_k}$ (prime avoidance: $\fp_j\subseteq\bigcup\fp_{i_k}\iff\fp_j\subseteq$ some $\fp_{i_k}\iff\fp_j$ in the isolated set). So contracting $S^{-1}\fa$ kills all components outside the set and recovers $\bigcap_k\fq_{i_k}$, an expression in terms of $\fa$ and the $\fp_{i_k}$ alone --- hence independent of the decomposition. For a single minimal prime $\fp_i$ take $S_i=R\setminus\fp_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Together the two theorems give the precise statement of ``unique factorization of ideals'': the \emph{set} of associated primes is unique (first), and the primary components at \emph{minimal} primes are unique (second), while embedded components carry the only genuine ambiguity. This is the exact ideal-theoretic analogue of: the primes dividing $n$ are unique, and so are the exact prime-power factors --- but in the ideal world the ``higher-order'' embedded data can wobble.
\end{remark}

\begin{wex}{Recovering the isolated component by localization}
For $\fa=(x^2,xy)$ in $k[x,y]$, recover the $(x)$-primary (isolated) component as $S^{-1}\fa$ contracted, $S=k[x,y]\setminus(x)$.
\wrec The isolated prime is $(x)$; localizing at $S=R\setminus(x)$ inverts everything outside $(x)$, in particular makes $y$ a unit, blowing away the $(x,y)$-component.
\wsol In $S^{-1}R=R_{(x)}$, the element $y\notin(x)$ becomes a unit. The component $(x,y)^2$ (or $(x^2,y)$) localizes to the unit ideal because its radical $(x,y)$ meets $S$ (indeed $y\in S\cap(x,y)$). Hence $S^{-1}\fa=S^{-1}(x)=(x)R_{(x)}$, and contracting back: $(x)R_{(x)}\cap R=(x)$ (since $(x)$ is prime, its contraction from the localization at $(x)$ is itself). So $S_{(x)}(\fa)=(x)$, the unique isolated component. Sanity check: this matches both decompositions $(x)\cap(x,y)^2$ and $(x)\cap(x^2,y)$ --- the $(x)$-component is $(x)$ in each, exactly as the second uniqueness theorem predicts.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Embedded component is not recoverable}
Explain, using the same $\fa=(x^2,xy)$, why localizing cannot pin down the embedded $(x,y)$-component.
\wrec Localize at $S=R\setminus(x,y)$ (i.e.\ pass to $R_{(x,y)}$) and watch \emph{neither} component get inverted: both radicals $(x)$ and $(x,y)$ lie inside $(x,y)$, so the isolated component survives alongside the embedded one.
\wsol At $\fp=(x,y)$ the set $S=R\setminus(x,y)$ inverts elements outside the maximal ideal. The isolated component $(x)$ has radical $(x)$, and here $(x)\subseteq(x,y)$, so $S\cap(x)=\varnothing$ and $(x)$ does \emph{not} get blown away; we get $S^{-1}\fa=S^{-1}\bigl((x)\cap\fq_{(x,y)}\bigr)$ involving \emph{both} components. So localization at the embedded prime does not isolate $\fq_{(x,y)}$. Concretely $(S^{-1}\fa)^c$ recovers $\fa$ itself, not a single primary ideal, because $(x,y)$ is not minimal. Sanity check: consistent with the explicit ambiguity $\fq_{(x,y)}\in\{(x,y)^2,(x^2,y)\}$ --- there is no canonical choice, so no localization could return one.
\end{wex}

\begin{definition}[{irreducible ideal}]
A proper ideal $\fa$ is \emph{irreducible} if $\fa=\fb\cap\fc$ (ideals) implies $\fa=\fb$ or $\fa=\fc$.
\end{definition}

