We have to show that there is a maximal ideal of $B$ which does not contain $v$. By applying Exercise 21 to the ring $B_v$ and its subring $A$, we obtain an element $s \neq 0$ in $A$. Let $\fm$ be a maximal ideal of $A$ such that $s \notin \fm$, and let $k = A/\fm$. Then the canonical mapping $A \to k$ extends to a homomorphism $g$ of $B_v$ into an algebraic closure $\Omega$ of $k$. Show that $g(v) \neq 0$ and that $\Ker(g) \cap B$ is a maximal ideal of $B$.]

\item Let $A$ be a ring. Show that the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] Every prime ideal in $A$ is an intersection of maximal ideals.
\item[(ii)] In every homomorphic image of $A$ the nilradical is equal to the Jacobson radical.
\item[(iii)] Every prime ideal in $A$ which is not maximal is equal to the intersection of the prime ideals which contain it strictly.
\end{enumerate}
[The only hard part is (iii) $\Rightarrow$ (ii). Suppose (ii) false; then there is a prime ideal which is not an intersection of maximal ideals. Passing to the quotient ring, we may assume that $A$ is an integral domain whose Jacobson radical $\fR$ is not zero. Let $f$ be a non-zero element of $\fR$. Then $A_f \neq 0$, hence $A_f$ has a maximal ideal, whose contraction in $A$ is a prime ideal $\fp$ such that $f \notin \fp$, and is maximal with respect to this property. Then $\fp$ is not maximal and is not equal to the intersection of the prime ideals strictly containing $\fp$ (all of which contain $f$).] A ring $A$ with the equivalent properties above is called a Jacobson ring.

\item Let $A$ be a Jacobson ring (Exercise 23) and $B$ an $A$-algebra. Show that if $B$ is either (i) integral over $A$ or (ii) finitely generated as an $A$-algebra, then $B$ is Jacobson. [Use Exercise 22 for (ii).] In particular, every finitely generated ring, and every finitely generated algebra over a field, is a Jacobson ring.

\item Let $A$ be a ring. Show that the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $A$ is a Jacobson ring;
\item[(ii)] Every finitely generated $A$-algebra $B$ which is a field is finite over $A$.
\end{enumerate}
[(i) $\Rightarrow$ (ii). Reduce to the case where $A$ is a subring of $B$, and use Exercise 21. If $s \in A$ is as in Exercise 21, then there exists a maximal ideal $\fm$ of $A$ not containing $s$, and the homomorphism $A \to A/\fm = k$ extends to a homomorphism $g$ of $B$ into the algebraic closure of $k$. Since $B$ is a field, $g$ is injective, and $g(B)$ is algebraic over $k$, hence finite algebraic. (ii) $\Rightarrow$ (i). Use criterion (iii) of Exercise 23. Let $\fp$ be a prime ideal of $A$ which is not maximal, and let $B = A/\fp$. Let $f$ be a non-zero element of $B$. Then $B_f$ is a finitely generated $A$-algebra. If $B_f$ is a field it is finite over $B$, hence integral over $B$ and therefore $B$ is a field by (5.7), which is false. Hence $B_f$ is not a field and therefore $B_f$ has a non-zero prime ideal, whose contraction in $B$ is a non-zero prime ideal $\fp'$ such that $f \notin \fp'$.]

\item Let $X$ be a topological space. A subset $X_0$ of $X$ is locally closed if it is the intersection of an open set and a closed set, or equivalently if it is open in its closure. The following conditions on a subset $X_0$ of $X$ are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] Every non-empty locally closed subset of $X$ meets $X_0$;
\item[(2)] For every closed set $E$ in $X$ we have $\overline{E \cap X_0} = E$;
\item[(3)] The mapping $U \mapsto U \cap X_0$ of the collection of open sets of $X$ onto the collection of open sets of $X_0$ is bijective.
\end{enumerate}
A subset $X_0$ satisfying these conditions is said to be very dense in $X$. If $A$ is a ring, show that the following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $A$ is a Jacobson ring;
\item[(ii)] The set of maximal ideals of $A$ is very dense in $\Spec(A)$;
\item[(iii)] Every locally closed subset of $\Spec(A)$ consisting of a single point is closed.
\end{enumerate}
[(ii) and (iii) are geometrical formulations of conditions (ii) and (iii) of Exercise 23.]

\item Let $A$, $B$ be two local rings. $B$ is said to dominate $A$ if $A$ is a subring of $B$ and the maximal ideal $\fm$ of $A$ is contained in the maximal ideal $\fn$ of $B$ (or, equivalently, if $\fm = \fn \cap A$). Let $K$ be a field and let $\Sigma$ be the set of all local subrings of $K$. If $\Sigma$ is ordered by the relation of domination, show that $\Sigma$ has maximal elements and that $A \in \Sigma$ is maximal if and only if $A$ is a valuation ring of $K$. [Use (5.21).]

\item Let $A$ be an integral domain, $K$ its field of fractions. Show that the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] $A$ is a valuation ring of $K$;
\item[(2)] If $\fa, \fb$ are any two ideals of $A$, then either $\fa \subseteq \fb$ or $\fb \subseteq \fa$.
\end{enumerate}
Deduce that if $A$ is a valuation ring and $\fp$ is a prime ideal of $A$, then $A_{\fp}$ and $A/\fp$ are valuation rings of their fields of fractions.

\item Let $A$ be a valuation ring of a field $K$. Show that every subring of $K$ which contains $A$ is a local ring of $A$, i.e.\ of the form $A_{\fp}$ for a prime ideal $\fp$ of $A$.

\item Let $A$ be a valuation ring of a field $K$. The group $U$ of units of $A$ is a subgroup of the multiplicative group $K^*$ of $K$. The group $\Gamma = K^*/U$ is totally ordered as follows: if $\xi, \eta \in \Gamma$ are represented by $x, y \in K^*$, then $\xi \geq \eta$ is to mean $xy^{-1} \in A$. Show that this defines a total ordering on $\Gamma$ which is compatible with the group structure (i.e., $\xi \geq \eta \Rightarrow \xi \omega \geq \eta \omega$ for all $\omega \in \Gamma$). In other words, $\Gamma$ is a totally ordered abelian group. It is called the value group of $A$. Let $v : K^* \to \Gamma$ be the canonical homomorphism.

