Type: exercise_set
Title: Exercises (Atiyah \& Macdonald).
Content:
\begin{exers}{Exercises (Atiyah \& Macdonald).}
\item Let $\fq_1 \cap \cdots \cap \fq_n = 0$ be a minimal primary decomposition of the zero ideal in a Noetherian ring, and let $\fq_i$ be $\fp_i$-primary. Let $\fp_i^{(r)}$ be the $r$th symbolic power of $\fp_i$ (Chapter 4, Exercise 13). Show that for each $i = 1, \ldots, n$ there exists an integer $r_i$ such that $\fp_i^{(r_i)} \subseteq \fq_i$.

Suppose we allow $i$ to range over those suffixes such that $\fp_i$ is minimal (i.e., $\fq_i$ is an isolated primary component). Then $A_{\fp_i}$ is an Artin local ring, hence if $\fm_i$ is its maximal ideal we have $\fm_i^r = 0$ for all sufficiently large $r$, hence $\fq_i = \fp_i^{(r)}$ for all large $r$.

If $\fq_i$ is an embedded primary component, then $A_{\fp_i}$ is not Artinian, hence the powers $\fm_i^r$ are all distinct, and so the $\fp_i^{(r)}$ are all distinct. Hence in the given primary decomposition we can replace $\fq_i$ by any of the infinite set of $\fp_i$-primary ideals $\fp_i^{(r)}$ where $r \geq r_i$, and so there are infinitely many minimal primary decompositions of $0$ which differ only in the $\fp_i$-component.

\item Let $A$ be a Noetherian ring. Prove that the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[i)] $A$ is Artinian;
\item[ii)] $\Spec(A)$ is discrete and finite;
\item[iii)] $\Spec(A)$ is discrete.
\end{enumerate}

\item Let $k$ be a field and $A$ a finitely generated $k$-algebra. Prove that the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[i)] $A$ is Artinian;
\item[ii)] $A$ is a finite $k$-algebra.
\end{enumerate}
[To prove that i) $\Rightarrow$ ii), use (8.7) to reduce to the case where $A$ is an Artin local ring. By the Nullstellensatz, the residue field of $A$ is a finite extension of $k$. Now use the fact that $A$ is of finite length as an $A$-module. To prove ii) $\Rightarrow$ i), observe that the ideals of $A$ are $k$-vector subspaces and therefore satisfy d.c.c.]

\item Let $f \colon A \to B$ be a ring homomorphism of finite type. Consider the following statements:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[i)] $f$ is finite;
\item[ii)] the fibres of $f^*$ are discrete subspaces of $\Spec(B)$;
\item[iii)] for each prime ideal $\fp$ of $A$, the ring $B \otimes_A k(\fp)$ is a finite $k(\fp)$-algebra ($k(\fp)$ is the residue field of $A_{\fp}$);
\item[iv)] the fibres of $f^*$ are finite.
\end{enumerate}
Prove that i) $\Rightarrow$ ii) $\Rightarrow$ iii) $\Rightarrow$ iv). [Use Exercises 2 and 3.]

If $f$ is integral and the fibres of $f^*$ are finite, is $f$ necessarily finite?

\item In Chapter 5, Exercise 16, show that $X$ is a finite covering of $L$ (i.e., the number of points of $X$ lying over a given point of $L$ is finite and bounded).

\item Let $A$ be a Noetherian ring and $\fq$ a $\fp$-primary ideal in $A$. Consider chains of primary ideals from $\fq$ to $\fp$. Show that all such chains are of finite bounded length, and that all maximal chains have the same length.
\end{exers}

