\wrec Units of $k[[t]]$ are the series with nonzero constant term; the non-units form the ideal $(t)$.
\wsol A series $\sum a_i t^i$ is a unit iff $a_0\neq0$ (invert by geometric series). Thus the non-units are exactly $(t)$, so $k[[t]]$ is local with maximal ideal $\fm=(t)$, principal and nonzero. It is a Noetherian domain (e.g.\ it is the $t$-adic completion of $k[t]$, or directly each ideal is $(t^n)$). By characterization (4) it is a DVR with uniformizer $t$. Sanity check: every nonzero series is $t^n\cdot(\text{unit})$ where $n$ is the lowest exponent, giving the valuation $v=n$ and recovering characterization (1).
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{A non-example: $k[x,y]_{(x,y)}$ is not a DVR}
Explain why the localization of $k[x,y]$ at the maximal ideal $(x,y)$ fails to be a DVR.
\wrec A DVR is dimension $1$ with a principal maximal ideal; here both fail.
\wsol The local ring $R=k[x,y]_{(x,y)}$ has $\dim R=2$ (chain $0\subsetneq(x)\subsetneq(x,y)$), violating characterization (5). Equivalently $\fm=(x,y)$ needs two generators: $\dim_{R/\fm}\fm/\fm^2=2\neq1$, violating (6). So $R$ is a regular local ring but of dimension $2$, not a DVR. Sanity check: $R$ is integrally closed and Noetherian, so it satisfies every condition \emph{except} dimension $1$ / principal maximal ideal -- pinpointing exactly which hypotheses are doing the work.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{A non-example: a non-integrally-closed dimension-$1$ local ring}
Show $R=k[t^2,t^3]_{(t^2,t^3)}$ is a Noetherian local domain of dimension $1$ that is \emph{not} a DVR.
\wrec Characterization (5) requires integral closure; the cusp coordinate ring fails it.
\wsol Let $A=k[t^2,t^3]\subseteq k[t]$, the coordinate ring of the cuspidal cubic $y^2=x^3$, and $R=A_\fm$ at $\fm=(t^2,t^3)$. Then $\dim R=1$ (it is a curve), $R$ is Noetherian and local. But $u=t^3/t^2=t\in K=\Frac(R)$ satisfies $u^2=t^2\in R$, so $u$ is integral over $R$ yet $u=t\notin R$. Thus $R$ is not integrally closed, so not a DVR. Sanity check: $\dim_{R/\fm}\fm/\fm^2=2$ here (generators $t^2,t^3$ are independent mod $\fm^2$), so (6) also fails -- the cusp is a singular point of the curve, the geometric face of ``not a DVR.''
\end{wex}

\begin{definition}[{$v$-adic metric}]
A discrete valuation $v$ on $K$ gives, for any fixed real $\beta>1$, an absolute value $\lVert a\rVert_v=\beta^{-v(a)}$ (with $\lVert0\rVert_v=0$) and metric $d_v(a,b)=\lVert a-b\rVert_v$. Axiom (iii) yields the \emph{ultrametric} inequality $d_v(a,b)\le\max\{d_v(a,c),d_v(c,b)\}$. The completion $K_v$ is again a field with a discrete valuation extending $v$, and $\{v\ge0\}$ inside it is a complete DVR.
\end{definition}

\begin{wex}{The $p$-adic integers $\mathbb{Z}_p$ as a DVR}
Show $\mathbb{Z}_p=\varprojlim\mathbb{Z}/p^n\mathbb{Z}$ is a DVR and identify its uniformizer and residue field.
\wrec $\mathbb{Z}_p$ is the completion of $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$; it is a PID with unique maximal ideal $p\mathbb{Z}_p$.
\wsol Every nonzero $\alpha\in\mathbb{Z}_p$ is uniquely $p^n u$ with $u\in\units{\mathbb{Z}_p}$ a unit (constant $p$-adic digit nonzero), so $v_p(\alpha)=n$ is a discrete valuation and $\mathbb{Z}_p$ is its ring -- a DVR by characterization (1). The uniformizer is $t=p$, the maximal ideal is $p\mathbb{Z}_p$, and the residue field is $\mathbb{Z}_p/p\mathbb{Z}_p\cong\F_p$. Its fraction field is $\mathbb{Q}_p$, in which every nonzero element is $p^n u$ with $n\in\mathbb{Z}$. Sanity check: the sequence $1,p,p^2,\dots$ has $v_p\to+\infty$, so $\lVert p^k\rVert_p=p^{-k}\to0$; it converges to $0$ in the $p$-adic metric, the hallmark that ``divisible by a high power of $p$'' means ``small.''
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{$\mathbb{Z}_p$ as the completion of $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$, and $\mathbb{Q}_p$ from $\mathbb{Q}$}
Explain the sense in which $\mathbb{Z}_p$ and $\mathbb{Q}_p$ are completions, via $p$-adic Laurent expansions.
\wrec Completing $\mathbb{Q}$ in the $p$-adic metric gives $\mathbb{Q}_p$; each element has a digit expansion $\sum_{i\ge n_0}a_i p^i$.
\wsol Cauchy sequences in $(\mathbb{Q},d_{v_p})$ have limits represented as $\alpha=\sum_{i\ge n_0}a_i p^i$ with digits $a_i\in\{0,\dots,p-1\}$ and $v_p(\alpha)=n_0$ when $a_{n_0}\neq0$. The subset with $n_0\ge0$ (no negative powers of $p$) is the completion of $\mathbb{Z}$, namely $\mathbb{Z}_p$; allowing finitely many negative powers gives $\mathbb{Q}_p=\Frac(\mathbb{Z}_p)$. This mirrors the power-series case $F[[x]]\subseteq F((x))$ exactly, with $p$ in place of $x$. Sanity check: truncating the series at $p^n$ recovers $\mathbb{Z}/p^n\mathbb{Z}$, so $\mathbb{Z}_p=\varprojlim\mathbb{Z}/p^n\mathbb{Z}$ and $\bigcap_n p^n\mathbb{Z}_p=0$, consistent with $v_p$ of a nonzero element being finite.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Laurent series as the completion of $k[x]_{(x)}$}
Identify $\Frac(k[[x]])$ and relate $k[[x]]$ to the localization $k[x]_{(x)}$.
\wrec Completing $k(x)$ at the valuation $v_x$ (order at $x=0$) gives $k((x))$ with ring $k[[x]]$.
\wsol The valuation ring $k[x]_{(x)}$ (rational functions defined at $0$) is dense in its $v_x$-completion $k[[x]]$, and $\Frac(k[[x]])=k((x))$, the formal Laurent series. Every nonzero Laurent series is $x^n\cdot(\text{unit})$, recovering $v_x=n$. Sanity check: $k[x]_{(x)}$ and $k[[x]]$ have the same residue field $k$ and the same uniformizer $x$, but only the completion is complete -- e.g.\ the partial sums of $\sum_{i\ge0}x^{i^2}$ form a Cauchy sequence in $k[x]_{(x)}$ with no limit there, since this series is not a rational function (its coefficients satisfy no linear recurrence), whereas $\sum_{i\ge0}x^i=1/(1-x)$ already lies in $k[x]_{(x)}$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{The ultrametric inequality sharpened}
In a DVR's fraction field, prove that $v(x)<v(y)\Rightarrow v(x+y)=\min\{v(x),v(y)\}=v(x)$.
\wrec Equality holds in the ultrametric law whenever the two valuations differ; write $x+y=x(1+y/x)$.
\wsol Suppose $v(x)<v(y)$, so $v(y/x)=v(y)-v(x)>0$, i.e.\ $y/x\in\fm$. Then $1+y/x$ is a unit (it is $1$ plus a non-unit in a local ring), so $v(1+y/x)=0$, giving $v(x+y)=v(x)+v(1+y/x)=v(x)=\min\{v(x),v(y)\}$. Sanity check: in $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$, $v_p(p+p^2)=v_p\bigl(p(1+p)\bigr)=1+0=1=\min(1,2)$; the only way the strict inequality $v(x+y)>\min$ can occur is when $v(x)=v(y)$ and leading terms cancel, e.g.\ $v_p(p-p)=+\infty$.
\end{wex}

\begin{definition}[{fractional ideal}]
For a domain $R$ with fraction field $K$, a \emph{fractional ideal} is a nonzero $R$-submodule $A\subseteq K$ with $dA\subseteq R$ for some nonzero $d\in R$ (equivalently $A=d^{-1} I$ for an ordinary ideal $I$). Products are defined by $AB=\{\sum a_ib_i\}$; $A$ is \emph{invertible} if $AB=R$ for some fractional ideal $B$, necessarily $B=A^{-1}=\{x\in K:xA\subseteq R\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[{DVRs via fractional ideals}]
Let $R$ be a local domain that is not a field. Then $R$ is a DVR if and only if every nonzero fractional ideal of $R$ is invertible.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
If $R$ is a DVR with uniformizer $t$, every nonzero fractional ideal is $t^N R$ for some $N\in\mathbb{Z}$ (clear denominators, then use that ordinary ideals are $\fm^n=(t^n)$), hence principal and invertible. Conversely, if every nonzero fractional ideal is invertible then every ideal is finitely generated (invertible $\Rightarrow$ finitely generated), so $R$ is Noetherian. With $\fm$ the maximal ideal, $\fm\neq\fm^2$ (else $\fm=0$ by Nakayama and $R$ is a field), so pick $t\in\fm\setminus\fm^2$. Then $t\fm^{-1}$ is a nonzero ideal of $R$; it cannot lie in $\fm$ (that would force $t\in\fm^2$), so $t\fm^{-1}=R$, i.e.\ $\fm=(t)$ is principal. By characterization (4), $R$ is a DVR.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Invertibility is the same as being projective: a nonzero fractional ideal $A$ of a domain is invertible iff $A$ is a projective $R$-module. Over a DVR every fractional ideal is even \emph{free} of rank $1$ (it is principal), so the class group is trivial -- a DVR is a PID. Relaxing ``local'' while keeping ``every fractional ideal invertible'' gives, for domains that are not fields, exactly the Dedekind domains of the next section (Theorem~15(3) there; the definition itself is Noetherian, integrally closed, of dimension $1$); the class group then measures the failure to be a PID.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[{nonsingularity $=$ DVR}]
Let $C$ be an irreducible affine curve over an algebraically closed field $k$, and $v\in C$ a point with local ring $\mathcal{O}_{v,C}$ and maximal ideal $\fm_v$. Then $C$ is nonsingular at $v$ (i.e.\ $\dim_k(\fm_v/\fm_v^2)=1$) if and only if $\mathcal{O}_{v,C}$ is a discrete valuation ring.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
$\mathcal{O}_{v,C}$ is a Noetherian local domain of dimension $1$ (the curve has $\trdeg_k k(C)=1$). Nonsingularity says $\dim_k(\fm_v/\fm_v^2)=1$, which by characterization (6) is exactly the DVR condition for a one-dimensional Noetherian local ring. The residue field is $k$ (Nullstellensatz, $k=\bar k$), so the $R/\fm$-dimension is the $k$-dimension.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[{smoothness $=$ integral closure}]
An irreducible affine curve $C$ over $k=\bar k$ is smooth (nonsingular at every point) iff its coordinate ring $k[C]$ is integrally closed.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
$C$ is smooth iff every local ring $\mathcal{O}_{v,C}$ is a DVR iff (characterization (5), using $\dim=1$) every $\mathcal{O}_{v,C}$ is integrally closed iff $k[C]$ itself is integrally closed (integral closure is a local property).
\end{proof}

\begin{wex}{Order of a zero/pole on a smooth curve}
At a smooth point $v$ with uniformizer $t$, define and compute the order of $f$.
\wrec Every $f\in\mathcal{O}_{v,C}$ (and every $f$ in the function field) is $u\,t^n$; set $\mathrm{ord}_v(f)=n=v_v(f)$.
\wsol The valuation $v_v$ on $k(C)$ attached to the DVR $\mathcal{O}_{v,C}$ assigns $\mathrm{ord}_v(f)=n$ where $f=u\,t^n$, $u$ a unit. Then $n>0$ means a zero of order $n$, $n<0$ a pole of order $-n$, $n=0$ a nonzero finite value. For $C=\Aff^1$ with coordinate $x$ and $v$ the point $x=a$: $t=x-a$, and $f=(x-a)^2/(x-b)$ has $\mathrm{ord}_v f=2$ at $a$ and $\mathrm{ord}=-1$ at $b$. Sanity check: $\mathrm{ord}_v(fg)=\mathrm{ord}_v f+\mathrm{ord}_v g$ by additivity of $v_v$, so orders add under multiplication and the ``divisor'' $\sum_v\mathrm{ord}_v(f)\cdot v$ is well defined -- the entry point to the theory of divisors on curves.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{A singular point fails to be a DVR}
For the cusp $C:\,y^2=x^3$ at the origin, show $\dim_k(\fm_v/\fm_v^2)=2$, hence singular.
\wrec Compute $\fm_v/\fm_v^2$ in $\mathcal{O}_{0,C}$; if it has dimension $>1$ the local ring is not a DVR.
\wsol With $k[C]=k[x,y]/(y^2-x^3)$ and $\fm=(x,y)$ at the origin, in $\fm/\fm^2$ the relation $y^2=x^3$ lives in $\fm^3\subseteq\fm^2$ and imposes nothing, so $x,y$ are linearly independent mod $\fm^2$: $\dim_k(\fm/\fm^2)=2>1$. Hence $\mathcal{O}_{0,C}$ is not a DVR and the origin is singular. Sanity check: this is the cusp $R=k[t^2,t^3]_{(t^2,t^3)}$ from earlier, where $t=y/x$ is integral but not in $R$ -- non-integral-closure and the dimension-$2$ cotangent space are two views of the same singularity.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{Localizing a PID at a prime always gives a DVR}
Show every localization $R_\fp$ of a PID $R$ at a nonzero prime $\fp=(p)$ is a DVR.
\wrec A PID is integrally closed (it is a UFD) and its nonzero primes are maximal, so the corollary on minimal primes applies.
\wsol $R$ is Noetherian, integrally closed, a domain; $\fp=(p)$ is a minimal nonzero prime (in a PID all nonzero primes are minimal $=$ maximal). By the corollary, $R_\fp$ is a DVR, with valuation $v_p(p^n a/b)=n$ for $a,b$ prime to $p$. This unifies the $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ and $F[x]_{(f)}$ examples. Sanity check: the fraction field of $R_\fp$ equals $\Frac(R)$, so each nonzero prime $p$ of $R$ yields one valuation on the common field $K$ -- exactly the family of DVRs that reassembles into the Dedekind domain $R$ in Section 16.3.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{From a Dedekind domain to its DVRs (forward link)}
If $R$ is Noetherian, integrally closed, of dimension $1$, describe the family $\{R_\fp\}$ and why each is a DVR.
\wrec Each nonzero prime $\fp$ is minimal; localizing gives a one-dimensional integrally closed Noetherian local domain.
\wsol For each nonzero prime $\fp$ of $R$, the localization $R_\fp$ satisfies characterization (5): Noetherian, integrally closed, local with unique nonzero prime $\fp R_\fp$, dimension $1$. So $R_\fp$ is a DVR with a valuation $v_\fp$ on $K=\Frac(R)$, and the maximal ideals of $R$ are in bijection with these valuations. Sanity check: this is condition (2) of the upcoming Dedekind characterization ($R$ Noetherian with every $R_\fp$ a DVR), so a Dedekind domain is precisely a ``global'' object whose every local piece is a DVR -- the local-global principle that restores unique factorization at the level of ideals.
\end{wex}

\begin{definition}[{fractional ideal}]
A \emph{fractional ideal} of $R$ is an $R$-submodule $A\subseteq K$ for which there is a nonzero $d\in R$ with $dA\subseteq R$. Equivalently, $A=d^{-1} I$ for some nonzero $d\in R$ and some (ordinary) ideal $I\subseteq R$. The ordinary ideals of $R$ are exactly the fractional ideals contained in $R$; to distinguish them we call them \emph{integral ideals}. For $x\in K$ the cyclic module $Rx=\{rx:r\in R\}$ is the \emph{principal fractional ideal} generated by $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[{product and inverse}]
If $A,B$ are fractional ideals their \emph{product} $AB$ is the set of finite sums $\sum a_ib_i$ with $a_i\in A$, $b_i\in B$. If $A=d^{-1} I$ and $B=(d')^{-1} J$ then $AB=(dd')^{-1} IJ$, so $AB$ is again a fractional ideal. We call $A$ \emph{invertible} if $AB=R$ for some fractional ideal $B$; such a $B$ is unique (if $AB=AC=R$ then $B=B(AC)=(BA)C=C$) and is written $A^{-1}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
(1) is immediate. For (2): $A'$ is an $R$-module; picking a nonzero $a\in A\cap R$ gives $aA'\subseteq R$, so $A'$ is fractional. If $AB=R$ then $B\subseteq A'$, whence $R=AB\subseteq AA'\subseteq R$, forcing $AA'=R$. For (3): from $AA'=R$ write $1=\sum_{i=1}^n a_ia_i'$ with $a_i\in A$, $a_i'\in A'$; then any $a\in A$ is $a=\sum_i(aa_i')a_i$ with $aa_i'\in R$, so $a_1,\dots,a_n$ generate $A$. (4) is formal, using $(xR)(yR)=(xy)R$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[{Prop.~11: local invertibility detects a DVR}]
Let $R$ be a local domain, not a field. Then $R$ is a DVR iff every nonzero fractional ideal of $R$ is invertible.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
If $R$ is a DVR with uniformizer $t$, every nonzero fractional ideal is $t^N R$ for some $N\in\mathbb{Z}$, hence principal, hence invertible. Conversely, invertibility of ideals makes every ideal finitely generated (Prop.~9(3)), so $R$ is Noetherian; if $\fm$ is the maximal ideal then $\fm\ne\fm^2$ (else $\fm=0$ by Nakayama and $R$ is a field), so pick $t\in\fm\setminus\fm^2$. Then $t\fm^{-1}$ is an integral ideal; it cannot lie in $\fm$ (that would give $t\in\fm^2$), so $t\fm^{-1}=R$, i.e.\ $(t)=\fm$. A Noetherian local domain with principal maximal ideal is a DVR.
\end{proof}

\begin{wex}{The standard fractional ideal $\tfrac12\mathbb{Z}$ of $\mathbb{Z}$}
Exhibit $A=\tfrac12\mathbb{Z}$ as a fractional ideal of $\mathbb{Z}$ and compute $A'$ and $AA'$.
\wrec A fractional ideal of $\mathbb{Z}$ is $d^{-1} I$ for an integer $d$ and ideal $I=(n)$; the inverse of a principal fractional ideal $xR$ is $x^{-1} R$.
\wsol Take $d=2$, $I=(1)=\mathbb{Z}$: then $A=2^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\tfrac12\mathbb{Z}$ is an $R$-submodule of $\mathbb{Q}$ with $2A=\mathbb{Z}\subseteq\mathbb{Z}$, so it is fractional. It is principal, $A=(\tfrac12)\mathbb{Z}$, so $A'=A^{-1}=2\mathbb{Z}$ and $AA'=(\tfrac12\cdot2)\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Z}=R$. Sanity check: $A$ is invertible because every nonzero fractional ideal of the PID $\mathbb{Z}$ is principal; here $A^{-1}=2\mathbb{Z}$ and indeed $\tfrac12\mathbb{Z}\cdot 2\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{A non-invertible fractional ideal in a non-normal ring}
In $R=k[t^2,t^3]\subseteq k[t]$ (the cusp ring) let $\fm=(t^2,t^3)$. Show $\fm$ is not invertible.
\wrec For an integral ideal, $\fm$ invertible $\Rightarrow$ $\fm\fm'=R$; one tests whether $\fm'=(R:\fm)$ is large enough to recover $R$.
\wsol Here $K=k(t)$ and $\fm'=(R:\fm)=\{x\in K:x\fm\subseteq R\}$. The element $t=t^3/t^2$ satisfies $t\cdot t^2=t^3\in R$ and $t\cdot t^3=t^4=(t^2)^2\in R$, so $t\in\fm'$; thus $R[t]\subseteq\fm'$ and in fact $\fm'=k[t]$. Then $\fm\fm'=(t^2,t^3)k[t]=(t^2)k[t]=t^2k[t]$, which contains $t^2,t^3,t^4,\dots$ but \emph{not} $1$, so $\fm\fm'\ne R$ and $\fm$ is not invertible. Sanity check: $R$ is not integrally closed ($t$ is integral over $R$ but $t\notin R$), so $R$ is not a Dedekind domain, and Theorem~15 below predicts that some ideal fails to be invertible -- exactly what we found.
\end{wex}

\begin{wex}{The inverse of $(2,1+\sqrt{-5})$ in $\mathbb{Z}[\sqrt{-5}]$}
In $R=\mathbb{Z}[\sqrt{-5}]$ let $\fp=(2,1+\sqrt{-5})$. Verify $\fp$ is invertible by computing $\fp\fp'$.
\wrec In a Dedekind domain every nonzero ideal is invertible; one verifies it by producing $A'$ with $AA'=R$. Here $\fp'$ should be $\tfrac12\fp$ (the conjugate scaled).
\wsol Compute $\fp^2=(2,1+\sqrt{-5})^2$, generated by $4$, $2(1+\sqrt{-5})$, and $(1+\sqrt{-5})^2=-4+2\sqrt{-5}$. Each lies in $(2)$, so $\fp^2\subseteq(2)$. Conversely $\fp^2$ contains $4$ and $-4+2\sqrt{-5}$, hence their sum $2\sqrt{-5}$; and it contains $2(1+\sqrt{-5})=2+2\sqrt{-5}$, so $(2+2\sqrt{-5})-2\sqrt{-5}=2\in\fp^2$, giving $(2)\subseteq\fp^2$. Hence $\fp^2=(2)$, the statement that the prime $2$ is ramified with $N(\fp)=\abs{R/\fp}=2$. From $\fp^2=(2)$ we get $\fp\cdot\bigl(\tfrac12\fp\bigr)=\tfrac12\fp^2=\tfrac12(2)=R$, so $\fp$ is invertible with $\fp^{-1}=\tfrac12\fp$. Sanity check: $\fp$ is not principal (no element of $R$ has norm $2$, since $a^2+5b^2=2$ is insoluble), yet it is invertible -- the hallmark of a Dedekind domain that is not a PID, and the source of a nontrivial class group.
\end{wex}

\begin{definition}[{Dedekind domain}]
A \emph{Dedekind domain} is a Noetherian, integrally closed integral domain of Krull dimension $1$. Equivalently: $R$ is a Noetherian, integrally closed domain, not a field, in which every nonzero prime ideal is maximal.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[{Prop.~14: the two motivating classes}]
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\arabic*)},leftmargin=*]
\item Every PID that is not a field is a Dedekind domain.
\item The ring of integers $\mathcal{O}_K$ of an algebraic number field $K$ is a Dedekind domain.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
A PID is Noetherian, is integrally closed (being a UFD), and has every nonzero prime maximal. For $\mathcal O_K=$ integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}$ in $K$: it is integrally closed by construction, Noetherian because it is a finitely generated $\mathbb{Z}$-module (hence a Noetherian $\mathbb{Z}$-module), and dimension $1$ because a nonzero prime $\fp$ contracts to a nonzero prime $\fp\cap\mathbb{Z}=(p)$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, which is maximal, forcing $\fp$ maximal by integrality (lying-over / going-up).
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
(1)$\Rightarrow$(2): localizing preserves Noetherian, integrally closed, and dimension $1$, so $R_P$ is a local Noetherian integrally closed domain of dimension $1$, i.e.\ a DVR. (2)$\Rightarrow$(1): integral closure and "primes are maximal" are local properties, and a domain that is locally a DVR is integrally closed of dimension $1$. (1)$\Leftrightarrow$(3): invertibility $AA'=R$ is a local statement, $(AA')_P=A_P(A_P)'$; each $R_P$ being a DVR makes $A_P$ invertible (Prop.~11), so $AA'=R$ globally. Conversely invertible fractional ideals make $R$ Noetherian and each $R_P$ a local ring with all fractional ideals invertible, hence a DVR (Prop.~11). (3)$\Leftrightarrow$(4) is the projective $=$ invertible dictionary (the paragraph after Prop.~9). (1)$\Rightarrow$(5): a minimal primary decomposition $I=Q_1\cap\cdots\cap Q_n$ has pairwise comaximal radicals (primes are maximal), so $\bigcap Q_i=\prod Q_i$, and each $P$-primary $Q_i$ is a prime power $P_i^{m_i}$ because $R_{P_i}$ is a DVR. (5)$\Rightarrow$(3): one shows invertible primes are maximal and that the factorization forces every nonzero prime to be invertible, whence every ideal -- and then every fractional ideal -- is invertible. Uniqueness in (5) is the cancellation argument: in $P_1\cdots P_n=P_1'\cdots P_m'$ a minimal $P_1'$ contains some $P_i$, which in turn contains some $P_j'$; minimality of $P_1'$ forces $P_j'=P_i=P_1'$, allowing cancellation of the invertible $P_1'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The logic of (5)$\Rightarrow$(3) is purely multiplicative and reverses the historical order. Dedekind \emph{defined} his domains by property (5) -- unique factorization of ideals -- precisely to repair the failure of unique factorization of \emph{elements} in rings of integers (Section 8.3). It was Noether who recognized the structural characterization (1). The two viewpoints, "ideals factor" and "Noetherian $+$ integrally closed $+$ dimension $1$," are the same world seen from arithmetic and from geometry respectively.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[{Cor.~16: unique factorization in $\mathcal O_K$}]
If $\mathcal O_K$ is the ring of integers of a number field $K$ then every nonzero ideal $I$ factors uniquely as $I=P_1^{e_1}\cdots P_n^{e_n}$ with distinct primes $P_i$ and $e_i\ge1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{wex}{$\mathbb{Z}$ as a Dedekind domain, all five conditions at once}
Check that $\mathbb{Z}$ satisfies each clause of Theorem~15 directly.
\wrec $\mathbb{Z}$ is a PID, hence Dedekind; the five conditions should each be visible from elementary number theory.
\wsol (1) $\mathbb{Z}$ is Noetherian, integrally closed (UFD), and its nonzero primes $(p)$ are maximal. (2) $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ is the DVR of $p$-adic valuation. (3) every fractional ideal is $\tfrac{a}{b}\mathbb{Z}$, invertible with inverse $\tfrac{b}{a}\mathbb{Z}$. (4) it is principal, hence free of rank $1$, hence projective. (5) $(n)=\prod(p_i)^{e_i}$ from $n=\prod p_i^{e_i}$. Sanity check: the prime factorization of the integer $n$ and the prime-ideal factorization of $(n)$ literally coincide because $\mathbb{Z}$ is a PID -- the class group is trivial.
\end{wex}

