\begin{method}{Factoring by norm bookkeeping}
To factor $\alpha\in R$ or test irreducibility: (i) compute $N=\Nm(\alpha)=a^2-b^2 d$;
(ii) list the divisors $m\mid N$ with $1<m<N$ -- a proper factor $\beta$ must have $\Nm(\beta)=m$;
(iii) for each such $m$, solve $\Nm(a'+b'\delta)=m$ (a finite search on the lattice); (iv) if no
$m$ is hit, $\alpha$ is irreducible; otherwise $\beta=a'+b'\delta$ is a candidate and
$\gamma=\alpha/\beta$ completes the split. Norms multiply, so a clash $\Nm(\beta)\nmid N$ rules
$\beta$ out instantly.
\end{method}

\begin{pitfall}
Irreducible is not the same as prime here. In $\mathbb{Z}[\sqrt{-5}]$ the element $2$ is irreducible
(its norm $4$ admits no proper factor) yet not prime (it divides the product
$(1+\sqrt{-5})(1-\sqrt{-5})$ but neither factor). Factorization into irreducibles always
\emph{terminates} -- the norm descends -- but it need not be \emph{unique}. Uniqueness is the
strictly stronger UFD condition, and Artin's theorem (13.2.5) records that for imaginary
$\mathbb{Q}[\sqrt d]$ it holds for only nine values
$d=-1,-2,-3,-7,-11,-19,-43,-67,-163$. The repair, pursued next, is to factor \emph{ideals}
rather than elements.
\end{pitfall}

\begin{definition}[{ideal as a sublattice}]
A nonzero subset $A\subseteq R$ is an \emph{ideal} iff it is a subgroup closed under multiplication by every element of $R$. When $d\equiv2,3\pmod4$ (as in the running example), $R=\mathbb{Z}\oplus\mathbb{Z}\delta$ is a lattice in $\mathbb{C}$, and any subgroup of $R$ closed under multiplication by $\delta$ is already an ideal: closure under $\mathbb{Z}$ is automatic, and $a+b\delta$ then acts because $\delta$ does. (When $d\equiv1\pmod4$, $R=\mathbb{Z}\oplus\mathbb{Z}\eta$ and one tests closure under $\eta$ instead.)
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[{Artin 13.3.1: every nonzero ideal is a full lattice}]
Let $R$ be the ring of integers in an imaginary quadratic field, $d\equiv2,3\pmod4$. Every nonzero ideal $A$ of $R$ is a sublattice of $R$ of full rank $2$. Conversely a sublattice $A\subseteq R$ is an ideal if and only if $\delta A\subseteq A$.
\end{proposition}

 egin{proof}
If $A
eq0$ pick $\alpha\in A$, $\alpha
eq0$; then $\{\alpha,\alpha\delta\}$ is $\mathbb{R}$-independent (as $\delta
otin\mathbb{R}$), so $A$ contains two independent vectors. Being a subgroup of the lattice $R$, $A$ is discrete, hence itself a lattice (a discrete subgroup containing two independent vectors is a full lattice). For the converse, a sublattice is closed under addition and under multiplication by $\mathbb{Z}$ automatically; if in addition $\delta A\subseteq A$, then $A$ is closed under multiplication by every $a+b\delta\in R$, so $A$ is an ideal.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Sketch, following Artin]
Since $\alpha\in A$, the principal ideal $(\alpha)\subseteq A$ has lattice basis $\{\alpha,\alpha\delta\}$. If $A=(\alpha)$ we are in case (i). Otherwise choose $\beta\in A\setminus(\alpha)$ lying in the fundamental rectangle of $(\alpha)$, i.e.\ $\beta=r\alpha+s\alpha\delta$ with $0\le r,s\le1$ (the rectangle, since $\delta$ is purely imaginary, has side ratio $1:\sqrt5$). The geometric lemma -- disks of radius $r=\abs{\alpha}$ about the four vertices together with disks of radius $\tfrac12 r$ about the three half-lattice points $\tfrac12\alpha\delta$, $\tfrac12(\alpha+\alpha\delta)$, $\alpha+\tfrac12\alpha\delta$ cover the rectangle, and the only lattice points interior to such disks are their centers -- forces $\beta$ to be one of the three half-lattice points. If $\beta=\alpha+\tfrac12\alpha\delta$ then $\tfrac12\alpha\delta\in A$, reducing to that case. If $\beta=\tfrac12\alpha\delta\in A$, multiplying by $\delta$ gives $\tfrac12\alpha\delta^2=-\tfrac52\alpha\in A$, and subtracting integer multiples of $\alpha$ shows $\tfrac12\alpha\in A$, contradicting minimality of $\abs\alpha$. The only survivor is $\beta=\tfrac12(\alpha+\alpha\delta)$, the rectangle center, giving case (ii); the resulting lattice is the non-principal shape.
\end{proof}

