\begin{definition}[{split, ramified, inert}]
Let $p$ be a rational prime. By part (b) of the theorem, either $(p)$ is prime, or
$(p)=P\overline P$ for a prime ideal $P$. We say:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item $p$ remains prime (is inert) if $(p)=pR$ is itself a prime ideal of $R$;
\item $p$ splits if $(p)=P\overline P$ with $P\neq\overline P$;
\item $p$ ramifies if $(p)=P\overline P$ with $P=\overline P$, i.e. $(p)=P^2$.
\end{itemize}
When $p$ splits or ramifies, $P\overline P=(p)$, so the norm of $P$ is $N(P)=p$; when $p$ is inert,
the prime ideal $(p)$ itself has norm $N\big((p)\big)=p^2$.
\end{definition}

