Given $K/L/F$, we could consider instead the automorphism group $\Aut_L F$ (instead of $\Aut_L K$). However, this is not a subgroup of $\Aut_F K$. \\

\begin{example}
Consider the extension $Q(\sqrt[4]{2})$. There is a very nice extension $Q \subseteq Q(\sqrt{2}) \subseteq Q(\sqrt[4]{2})$. Furthermore, we know that $Q(\sqrt{2})$ is Galois with a Galois group of $\{\id_Q, \sigma\}$, and $Q(\sqrt[4]{2})$ is Galois \emph{over $Q(\sqrt{2})$} with Galois group $\{1, \tau\}$ where $\tau(\sqrt[4]{2}) = -\sqrt[4]{2}$ and this group fixed $Q(\sqrt{2})$. However, $\Aut_{Q} Q(\sqrt[4]{2}) = \{1, \tau\}$ as well, so there is no way to think of $\sigma$ as an automorphism of $Q(\sqrt[4]{2})$.
\end{example}
\end{warning}

