Let $p$ be prime. Recall the following definitions:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\Pi(x) = \sum_{p \leq x} 1$;
\item $\vartheta(x) = \sum_{p \leq x} \log p$;
\item $% RAG-CORRECTION M06 (see audit/corrections.json)
\psi(x) = \sum_{\substack{p^k\leq x\\p\text{ prime},\ k\geq1}} \log p = \sum_{1\leq n\leq x} \Lambda(n)$.
\end{itemize}
Here $\Lambda(n)=\log p$ when $n=p^k$ for a prime $p$ and $k\geq1$,
and $\Lambda(n)=0$ otherwise. Hence the two expressions for $\psi$ count
exactly the same prime powers, with weight $\log p$.


We had two important results from these definitions.

\begin{theorem}
$\abs{\vartheta(x) - \psi(x)} \leq O(\sqrt{x}\log^2x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
$\Pi(x) = \vartheta(x)/\log x - \int_1^x \vartheta(x)/t\log^2x \dd{t}$.
\end{theorem}

