\label{tmua-2-subsection-002}
\textbf{Polynomial division/remainder theorem} (Euclidean division) states that for any polynomial \(P\) (the polynomial to be divided) and \(D \neq 0\) (the \textbf{divisor}), there are unique polynomials \(Q\) (the \textbf{quotient}) and \(R\) (the \textbf{remainder}), such that
\[
    P \pqty{x} = Q \pqty{x} D\pqty{x} + R \pqty{x}
\]
where \(\deg R < \deg D\).

\begin{problem}\label{tmua-2-unit-001}
    Can you try proving this? Which other number system allows for division with remainder like this? (There is a very deep reason behind this.)
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}\label{tmua-2-unit-002}
