Course: TMUA Lecture 3 -- Euclidean and Coordinate Geometry
Section: Geometry in Triangles > Area Formulae, Sine Rule and Cosine Rule
Type: prose
Content:
Sine rule:
\[
 \frac{a}{\sin A} = \frac{b}{\sin B} = \frac{c}{\sin C}.
\]

Solving \(\sin B=t\) for \(0<B<\pi\) gives two candidates when \(0<t<1\), one when \(t=1\), and none when \(t\notin(0,1]\). The angle sum and the given sides can exclude candidates.

When to use sine rule:
\begin{itemize}
 \item given two angles and one side, find the remaining angle and any other side;
 \item given two sides and one angle but not between them, find another angle.
\end{itemize}

Note: the second case gives rise to ``ambiguity'', see below.

Cosine rule:
\begin{align*}
 a^2 &= b^2 + c^2 - 2bc \cos A \\
 b^2 &= c^2 + a^2 - 2ca \cos B \\
 c^2 &= a^2 + b^2 - 2ab \cos C
\end{align*}

A useful rearrangement of the cosine rule:
\begin{align*}
 \cos A &= \frac{b^2 + c^2 - a^2}{2bc} \\
 \cos B &= \frac{c^2 + a^2 - b^2}{2ca} \\
 \cos C &= \frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}
\end{align*}

When to use cosine rule:
\begin{itemize}
 \item given three sides, find any angle; (rearrangement)
 \item given two sides and the angle between them, find the third side. (original form)
\end{itemize}

