Content:
When given the length of two sides and an angle that is not between them (the SSA) scenario, there are multiple cases for the dimensions of the triangle. The best way is to draw a diagram, but here is an overview of the possibilities.

Let \(r,a>0\) be given sides, with \(r\) opposite the given angle \(0<\theta<180\degree\). We casework on the value of \(\theta\).

\begin{itemize}
 \item \(\theta < 90\degree\).
 \begin{itemize}
 \item If \(r < a \sin \theta\), the triangle is impossible.
 \item If \(r = a \sin \theta\), the triangle is unique (in which case it is a right-angle triangle).
 \item If \(a \sin \theta < r < a\), there are two possibilities for the triangle, with the angle opposite side \(a\) acute in one and obtuse in the other; the entire first triangle need not be acute.
 \item If \(r = a\), the triangle is unique (in which case it is an isosceles triangle).
 \item If \(r > a\), the triangle is unique.
 \end{itemize}

 \item \(\theta = 90 \degree\). This reduces to RHS.
 \begin{itemize}
 \item If \(r \leq a\), the triangle is impossible.
 \item If \(r > a\), the triangle is unique.
 \end{itemize}

 \item \(\theta > 90 \degree\).
 \begin{itemize}
 \item If \(r \leq a\), the triangle is impossible.
 \item If \(r > a\), the triangle is unique.
 \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

