\label{tmua-3-subsection-010}

For a convex polygon, the sum of its \textbf{external} turning angles is \(360 \degree\).

The sum of \textbf{internal} angles of a simple \(n\)-sided polygon is \(\pqty{n - 2} \cdot 180 \degree\), which means the internal angle of a regular \(n\)-sided polygon (whose sides and angles are all equal) is \(\frac{n - 2}{n} \cdot 180 \degree\).

\begin{example}[TMUA 2016 P2 Q20]\label{tmua-3-unit-021}
    Each interior angle of a regular polygon with \(n\) sides is \(\flatfrac{3}{4}\) of each interior angle of a second regular polygon with \(m\) sides. How many pairs of positive integers \(n\) and \(m\) are there for which this statement is true?
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2016 P1 Q12]\label{tmua-3-unit-022}
    A right circular cylinder is contained within a sphere of radius \(\SI{5}{\centi\metre}\) in such a way that the whole of the circumferences of both ends of the cylinder are in contact with the sphere.

    The diagram in \hyperref[fig:tmua-2016-p1-q12]{the textual figure description} shows a planar cross section through the centre of the sphere and the cylinder.

    Find, in cubic centimetres, the maximum possible volume of the cylinder.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2016 P2 Q19; solid-column interpretation]\label{tmua-3-unit-023}
    Some identical unit cubes are used to construct a three-dimensional object by gluing them together face to face.

    Sketches of this object are made by looking at it from the right-hand side, from the front and from above. These sketches are called the side elevation, the front elevation, and the plan view respectively.

    How many cubes were used to construct the object?
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2017 P1 Q11]\label{tmua-4-unit-002}
    The sequence \(x_n\) is defined by the rules
    \begin{align*}
        x_1 &= 7, \\
        x_{n + 1} &= \frac{23 x_n - 53}{5 x_n + 1}.
    \end{align*}

    The first three terms in this sequence are \(7, 3, 1\). What is the value of \(x_{100}\)?
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2017 P2 Q6]\label{tmua-4-unit-003}
    A sequence \(u_0, u_1, u_2, \ldots\) is defined as follows:
    \begin{align*}
        u_0 &= 1 \\
        u_n &= \int_{0}^{1} 4x u_{n - 1} \dd{x} \qfor n \geq 1
    \end{align*}

    What is the value of \(u_{1000}\)?
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2017 P2 Q15]\label{tmua-4-unit-004}
    The function \(f\) is defined on the positive integers as follows:
    \[
        f\pqty{1} = 5, \text{ and for } n \geq 1: \quad
        \begin{aligned}
            f\pqty{n + 1} &= 3 f\pqty{n} + 1 && \qif f\pqty{n} \text{ is odd} \\
            f\pqty{n + 1} &= \frac{1}{2} f\pqty{n} && \qif f\pqty{n} \text{ is even}
        \end{aligned}
    \]


    The function \(g\) is defined on the positive integers as follows:
    \[
        g\pqty{1} = 3, \text{ and for } n \geq 1: \quad
        \begin{aligned}
            g\pqty{n + 1} &= g\pqty{n} + 5 && \qif g\pqty{n} \text{ is odd} \\
            g\pqty{n + 1} &= \frac{1}{2} g\pqty{n} && \qif g\pqty{n} \text{ is even}
        \end{aligned}
    \]

    Find \(f\pqty{1000} - g\pqty{1000}\).
\end{example}

\begin{example}[MAT 2010, Question G]\label{tmua-4-unit-005}
    The function \(f\), defined for whole positive numbers, satisfies \(f\pqty{1} = 1\) and also the rules
    \begin{align*}
        f\pqty{2n} &= 2 f\pqty{n} \\
        f\pqty{2n + 1} &= 4 f\pqty{n}
    \end{align*}
    for all values of \(n\). Find the number of \(n\)s that satisfy \(f\pqty{n} = 16\).
\end{example}

\begin{example}\label{tmua-4-unit-007}
    Find the sums
    \[
        \sum_{i = 1}^{n} a_{2i} \qand \sum_{i = 1}^{n} a_{2i - 1}.
    \]
\end{example}

\begin{example}\label{tmua-4-unit-008}
    For an arithmetic sequence, \(a_2 + a_4 = 36\). Find the sum of the first \(5\) terms.
\end{example}

\begin{example}\label{tmua-4-unit-009}
    For an arithmetic sequence, \(a_2 + a_4 = 4\) and \(a_3 + a_5 = 10\). Find the sum of the first \(10\) terms.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2016 P2 Q12]\label{tmua-4-unit-010}
    The first term of an arithmetic sequence is \(a\) and the common difference is \(d\). The sum of the first \(n\) terms is denoted by \(S_n\).

    If \(S_8 > 3 S_6\), what can be deduced about the sign of \(a\) and the sign of \(d\)?
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2017 P1 Q7]\label{tmua-4-unit-011}
    The first three terms of an arithmetic progression are \(p\), \(q\) and \(p^2\) respectively, where \(p < 0\). The first three terms of a geometric progression are \(p\), \(p^2\) and \(q\) respectively.

    Find the sum of the first \(10\) terms of the arithmetic progression.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2017 P1 Q17]\label{tmua-4-unit-012}
    The two functions \(F\) and \(G\) are defined as follows for positive integers \(n\):
    \begin{align*}
        F\pqty{n} &= \frac{1}{n} \int_{0}^{n} \pqty{n - x} \dd{x} \\
        G\pqty{n} &= \sum_{r = 1}^{n} F\pqty{r}
    \end{align*}

    What is the smallest positive integer \(n\) such that \(G\pqty{n} > 150\)?
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2016 P1 Q14]\label{tmua-4-unit-014}
    The terms of an infinite series \(S\) are formed by adding together the corresponding terms in two infinite geometric series, \(T\) and \(U\).

    The first term of \(T\) and the first term of \(U\) are each \(4\).

    In order, the first three terms of the combined series \(S\) are \(8\), \(3\) and \(\flatfrac{5}{4}\).

    Find the sum to infinity of \(S\).
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2017 P2 Q3]\label{tmua-4-unit-015}
    The first term of a geometric progression is \(2 \sqrt{3}\) and the fourth term is \(\flatfrac{9}{4}\). What is the sum to infinity of this geometric progression?
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TMUA 2016 P1 Q3]\label{tmua-4-unit-020}
    A line is drawn normal to the curve \(y = \flatfrac{2}{x^2}\) at the point on the curve where \(x = 1\). This line cuts the \(x\)-axis at \(P\) and the \(y\)-axis at \(Q\). Find the length of \(PQ\).
