\begin{example}[TMUA 2017 P2 Q20]\label{tmua-5-unit-015}
\textit{Exercise: the proposed statements or reasoning below must be assessed; they are not all asserted as true.}\par

    I have forgotten my \(5\)-character computer password, but I know that it consists of the letters \texttt{a}, \texttt{b}, \texttt{c}, \texttt{d}, \texttt{e} in some order. When I enter a potential password into the computer, it tells me exactly how many of the letters are in the correct position.

    When I enter \texttt{abcde}, it tells me that none of the letters are in the correct position. The same happens when I enter \texttt{cdbea} and \texttt{eadbc}.

    Using the best strategy, how many \textbf{further} attempts must I make in order to \textbf{guarantee} that I can \textbf{deduce} the correct password?
\end{example}

\label{tmua-6-subsection-001}

In classical logic, a proposition has a truth value after its variables and interpretation are fixed. The law of excluded middle states \(A\lor\lnot A\); noncontradiction states \(\lnot(A\land\lnot A)\). An open formula with unassigned free variables is not yet a proposition.

Statements often take the form of ``if \dots, then \dots.''

\begin{example}\label{tmua-6-unit-001}
\textit{Exercise: the proposed statements or reasoning below must be assessed; they are not all asserted as true.}\par

    Determine whether the following sentences are statements.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The equation \(x^3 + y^2 = 88\) has no integer solutions.
        \item What is your name?
        \item The number \(2147483647\) is a prime.
        \item If \(x^2 = 9\), then \(x = 3\).
        \item It will rain tomorrow.
        \item \(x + 2 = 5\).
    \end{itemize}
\end{example}

\begin{problem}[Barber's Problem, Russell's Paradox]\label{tmua-6-unit-002}
    Is the following sentence a statement: a male barber in the town shaves exactly those men in the town who do not shave themselves. Can such a barber exist?
