Course: TMUA Lecture 6 -- Logic and Proof
Section: Logic of Arguments > And, Or, Not
Type: prose
Local macros: \compl := ^{\mathrm{c}}; \pqty := \left(#1\right)
Content:
If we have two statements \(A\) and \(B\), we may combine them using certain logical symbols:
\begin{itemize}
 \item and, denoted \(A \land B\), has truth value true exactly when both \(A\) and \(B\) are true;
 \item or, denoted \(A \lor B\), has truth value true exactly when at least one of \(A\) and \(B\) are true (inclusive or, i.e. if \(A \land B\) is true, then \(A \lor B\) is true).
\end{itemize}

For a single statement \(A\), the not operator, denoted \(\lnot A\), has truth value true exactly when \(A\) is false.

And and Or are both commutative and associative, and distributes over each other.

Suppose \(S\) and \(T\) are situations (e.g. values of \(x\)) that make \(A\) and \(B\) true, respectively. Then the situations that make:
\begin{itemize}
 \item \(A \land B\) true, is \(S \cap T\)
 \item \(A \lor B\) true, is \(S \cup T\)
 \item \(\lnot A\) true, is \(S\compl\) (the complement of the set \(S\))
\end{itemize}

We have the following rules:
\begin{itemize}
 \item Commutativity. \(A \land B = B \land A\); \(A \lor B = B \lor A\);
 \item Associativity. \(\pqty{A \land B} \land C = A \land \pqty{B \land C}\); \(\pqty{A \lor B} \lor C = A \lor \pqty{B \lor C}\)
 \item Distributivity. \(\pqty{A \land B} \lor C = \pqty{A \lor C} \land \pqty{B \lor C}\); \(\pqty{A \lor B} \land C = \pqty{A \land C} \lor \pqty{B \land C}\);
 \item Double negation. \(\lnot \pqty{\lnot A} = A\);
 \item De Morgan's Laws. \(\lnot \pqty{A \land B} = \pqty{\lnot A} \lor \pqty{\lnot B}\); \(\lnot \pqty{A \lor B} = \pqty{\lnot A} \land \pqty{\lnot B}\)
\end{itemize}

