Course: TMUA Lecture 6 -- Logic and Proof
Section: Proofs > Contradiction
Type: prose
Local macros: \pqty := \left(#1\right)
Content:
Proof by contradiction of a statement of a form \(A \implies B\) is done by assuming both \(A\) and \(\lnot B\), and using logical deduction to reach a result that contradicts with \(A\), i.e., we prove \(\lnot \pqty{A \land \pqty{\lnot B}}\) This is precisely proving the negation of the implication cannot be true.

Concretely speaking, to prove some statement \(A\), we:
\begin{itemize}
 \item start by assuming not \(A\) is true;
 \item logical deducting to two contradicting statements, \(B\) and \(\lnot B\);
 \item therefore our assumption not \(A\) is true must therefore be false;
 \item finally concluding that \(A\) must be true.
\end{itemize}

