    \emph{No drawing was supplied in the source for this figure.}\par
\caption{List of Symmetries of an Equilateral Triangle}
    \label{fig:equilateral-triangle-symmetry-list}
\end{figure}

Natural questions arise: what are the symmetries of a square, a regular pentagon, or in general, a regular \(n\)-gon?

We can define `multiplication' on symmetries, specifically by composing the symmetries, as shown in \autoref{fig:symmetry-composition}.

\begin{figure}[ht!]
    \centering
    \emph{No drawing was supplied in the source for this figure.}\par
\caption{Composition of Symmetries}
    \label{fig:symmetry-composition}
\end{figure}

We note the following features (and non-features) arise in the previous example. \bigbreak

\noindent
\textbf{Important features.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item There is an \emph{identity}.
    \item Every symmetry has an \emph{inverse}, as shown in \autoref{fig:symmetry-inverse}.

    \begin{figure}[ht!]
        \centering
        \emph{No drawing was supplied in the source for this figure.}\par
\caption{Symmetries are invertible}
        \label{fig:symmetry-inverse}
    \end{figure}

    \item Symmetries can be \emph{composed} (to form another symmetry).
    \item Composition of symmetries are \emph{associative}, as shown in \autoref{fig:symmetry-associative}.

    \begin{figure}[ht!]
        \centering
        \emph{No drawing was supplied in the source for this figure.}\par
\caption{Compositions of Symmetry are Associative}
        \label{fig:symmetry-associative}
    \end{figure}
\end{itemize}

\noindent
\textbf{Non-feature.} The symmetries need not commute. An example is given in \autoref{fig:symmetry-non-commutative}.

