The direct product of two groups is a group, and it is easy to see for any group \(G, H, K\),
\begin{itemize}
 \item \(G \times H \isomorphic H \times G\): the direct product is commutative up to isomorphism;
 \item \(\pqty{G \times H} \times K \isomorphic G \times \pqty{H \times K}\): the direct product is associative up to isomorphism;
 \item \(\trivialgp \times G \isomorphic G \times \trivialgp \isomorphic G\): the trivial group is an identity up to isomorphism (for infinite groups, \(G \times H \isomorphic G\) does not require \(H\) to be trivial, for example \(\RR \times \RR \isomorphic \RR\), since they are isomorphic when viewed as \(\QQ\)-vector spaces);
 \item If \(G \isomorphic H\), then \(G \times K \isomorphic H \times K\): we may replace elements in the direct product up to isomorphism;
 \item \(\abs{G \times H} = \abs{G} \times \abs{H}\): the order of the direct product is the product of the orders;
 \item \(\ord\pqty{g, h} = \lcm\Bqty{\ord\pqty{g}, \ord\pqty{h}}\): the order of an element is the least common multiple of the order of the separate elements.
\end{itemize}

